# Design System — History & Rationale

> Companion to `design-system-spec.mdc` (the active ruleset). This file holds the **WHY** — rev logs of dropped revisions, cross-system audits done at the time of each decision, and one-shot drift snapshots — that informed the rules now codified in the spec. It is **not** itself a ruleset.
>
> **When to read this**: before relitigating a decision the spec already documents, when debugging *why* the current value is what it is, or when an "earlier rev" reference in the spec points here for the full audit. **Do not** treat anything in this file as an active rule — the spec wins where they conflict.

---

## Part A — Standing-rule history

### A.1 §1.4.7 Known drift to audit (snapshot at the time §1.4 was written)

A first-pass scan found ~366 literal-pixel occurrences in `index.html`. Most are fallback values inside `var(--token, Npx)` (allowed by Pattern 1). The remainder cluster into these categories. Future audit passes should resolve each in priority order:

**Tier 1 — Component-CSS drift (highest priority; these break Standing Rule #12)**
- **Inline icon sizes** in nested children: e.g., `.nav-node__title .chev { width: 20px; height: 20px }` should be `var(--icon-m, 20px)`. Same for `.ds-checkbox-row__leading > svg { width: 16px; height: 16px }` (icon-s), `.ds-radio-row__leading > svg`, `.ds-switch-row__leading > svg`. Many places where a literal 16/20/24 should be the matching `--icon-*` token.
- **Switch/Radio internal dimensions** (`.ds-switch--s/m/l` width/height/thumb/pad, `.ds-radio--s/m/l` size/dot-size) — these are *formula-derived* (track ≈ 1.6 × thumb per §9.2; dot ≈ 0.5 × control-size) but inlined as literal values. Should be: thumb token, then `width: calc(var(--sw-thumb) * 1.6)`, etc. The component values would survive a scale of the icon/control ramp.
- **Component-layout micro-margins** (e.g., `.ds-checkbox-row:has(...) .ds-checkbox { margin-top: 1px / 2px }`) — 1px and 2px are on the spacing ramp via `space-3xs` (2px) and a literal 1px (the only odd value). Document or absorb.

**Tier 2 — Playground host-chrome drift (medium priority; these are app-shell, not the component system, but should still tokenize)**
- `.layout { padding-top: 48px }`, `.main { margin-left: 180px; padding: 16px 24px }`, `.content { max-width: 1000px }` — sidebar width, topbar height, content max-width. The 48 / 16 / 24 land on the spacing ramp (use tokens). The 180 (sidebar width) and 1000 (content max) are off-ramp app-shell constants — sanction in §1.4.5 as "host chrome constants" or pin to a "container width ramp."
- `.item-gap`, `.subsection-gap`, `.section-gap`, `.group-gap`, `.axis-gap` (height: 16/32/48/64/96px) — all on the spacing ramp; tokenize.
- Sidebar/topbar widths and heights — same treatment.

**Tier 3 — Demonstration-only literals (low priority; these are showing what a value looks like, so the literal IS the demo content)**
- Swatch checker sizes (`background-size: 10px 10px`), motion-track height, a few palette/spacing demo cards. These are showing the value, not consuming it — leave inlined with a comment.

**Tier 4 — Genuine off-ramp values that need either ramp absorption or sanction**
- Sidebar width 180px, content max-width 1000px, breakpoints 520px — none on the spacing ramp. Either add a "container width ramp" step or sanction in §1.4.5 as host-chrome constants.
- Switch track widths 28/32/40 (S/M/L) — derived from thumb × ~1.6 ratio. Express as `calc(var(--sw-thumb) * 1.625)` (S: 12 × 2.33; M: 16 × 2; L: 20 × 2 — actually the ratios aren't uniform; see §9.2 for the documented "1.6:1" stance). Audit and either standardize the ratio or document per-tier deviations.

The audit is **not blocking** — components work today. But every new component must comply with §1.4.4, and existing components are scheduled for cleanup as they're touched. The principle is more important than fixing everything at once: stop introducing new drift, fix existing drift opportunistically.

### A.2 §2.1.1 Radius ramp — rejected alternative curves and historic drift

Three alternative radius curves were evaluated and rejected when the M-tier moved from `radius-m` (16) to `radius-2m` (14) to smooth the S→M ratio jump:

- **8/12/16/20 (linearly +4 per step)** — would put S at 12px (37.5% ratio), crossing into *soft pill* territory and breaking the brand stance of "generous rounded rectangles, not pills." Keeping S at 10 and M at 14 preserves the rectangle-feel of the small tier while smoothing the boundary with M.
- **8/10/12/20** — S→M would only be +1pp (31% → 30%) — actually *decreasing*, which makes M look LESS rounded than S; visually wrong.
- **8/10/16/18 (drop L)** — pulls the L tier (48px, the system's "fully rounded" tier) back to 18, which makes L look the same as M and loses the prominence the L tier carries for hero CTAs and primary surfaces.

The 8/10/14/20 ramp (XS/S/M/L) was adopted at the time because it produces ratios 33/31/35/42% with inflection deltas −2pp / +4pp / +7pp — a smooth gradient with the largest jump at the M→L "soft-rectangle → fully-rounded" boundary. **This ramp was later flattened in June 2026 — see §A.2.1 below.** The 35%/42% L/XL ratios read as soft-pill surfaces out of step with the rectangular small tiers, so the curve was held near-flat at 29-33% across the whole ramp. The "largest jump at M→L is where the identity shifts" rationale was itself a retconned justification for a curve that ballooned at the top end; the flat-ratio curve is the genuine rectangular-identity stance.

**Why card surfaces (Toast, RadioCard, inline cards) STAYED at `radius-m` (16) and didn't move to 14**: these are NOT 40-tall controls. Their radius is set by the "card chrome" identity — intentionally a hair larger than M-tier controls so the eye reads them as *containers* (things that hold content) rather than *big buttons* (things you click). Industry alignment: shadcn cards 12-16, Linear cards 12, Atlassian cards 8 — all use a card radius distinct from button radius. The 14/16 separation in our system is the equivalent.

**Historic drift (now fixed)**:
- `.ds-checkbox` previously used `--cb-rd: 5px`, off-ramp. Snapped to `4px` (`radius-2xs`).
- The S tier oscillated: `radius-s` (12, "linear scaling") → `radius-xs` (8, "escape the pill threshold, small-element family") → `radius-2s` (10, "smooth the M boundary while staying out of pill territory"). The current value resolves both prior concerns by introducing a new ramp step instead of choosing between two existing-but-flawed stops.
- The M tier was previously at `radius-m` (16), creating a +9pp jump from S; smoothed to `radius-2m` (14) for a +4pp/+7pp pattern. Earlier spec text claimed the +9pp jump was "the intentional rectangle→rounded crossover"; this was retconned rationalization for an unsmooth curve. The smoothed curve is the genuine identity. (Card surfaces still use 16; the previous "M control = card = 16" identity has been split into two distinct roles.)

**Candidates evaluated and rejected** (do not re-litigate without new data):
- `radius-3xs = 6` — no consumer; atoms at 4 and small chrome at 8 are stable.
- `radius-2l = 22` — no consumer; 48px controls and surface cards are intentionally different families.
- `radius-2xl- = 28` — no current consumer; `28` is on the global palette and trivially upgradeable if a future Sheet/XL-card component appears.

**Candidate adopted (added to ramp)**: `radius-2m` = 14 — smooths the S→M ratio jump from +9pp to +4pp without crossing S into pill territory. M-tier 40-tall controls remap from `radius-m` (16) to `radius-2m` (14); card surfaces keep `radius-m` (16) as their distinct card-chrome identity.

#### A.2.1 June 2026 — the subtler ramp (L/XL flattened from 35%/42% to 30%/29%)

The 8/10/14/20 ramp documented above was the canonical control→radius mapping under the **previous 4-tier** height ramp (24/32/40/48). When the height ramp expanded to 5 tiers (24/28/32/40/48), the radius curve was re-evaluated and the L/XL ratios were found to be the system's weakest point:

| Tier | Height | OLD radius | OLD ratio | NEW radius | NEW ratio |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| XS | 24 | 8 (xs) | 33% | 8 (xs) | 33% |
| S | 28 | 8 (xs) | 29% | 8 (xs) | 29% |
| M | 32 | 10 (2s) | 31% | 10 (2s) | 31% |
| **L** | **40** | **14 (2m)** | **35%** | **12 (s)** | **30%** |
| **XL** | **48** | **20 (l)** | **42%** | **14 (2m)** | **29%** |

**Why flattened.** The old L (35%) and XL (42%) read as visibly rounder "soft-pill" surfaces sitting next to the rectangular small tiers (29-33%). A Button XL at `radius-l` (20) on a 48-tall control crosses well into pill territory; placed beside an Input M at 10/32 (31%) the two read as different shape families, not one scaled family. The June 2026 flatten holds the ratio near-flat at 29-33% across the entire ramp so the corner reads as the *same family* at every size — directly serving Standing Rule §1.1 ("generous rounded rectangles, not pills"). Industry alignment: Linear / Vercel / Primer all hold control radii under ~33% of height; none ramp to 42% at their large tiers.

**What moved (June 2026):**
- **40px-tall controls**: `radius-2m` (14) → `radius-s` (12). Consumers: Button-L, Input-L, Select-L, Textarea-L, Nav-horizontal L, Nav-vertical XL, Segmented-L, InputGroup-L, Avatar-square-L.
- **48px-tall controls**: `radius-l` (20) → `radius-2m` (14). Consumers: Button-XL, Input-XL, Select-XL, Textarea-XL, Nav-horizontal XL, Segmented-XL, InputGroup-XL, Avatar-square-XL.
- **Composer L** stayed at `radius-l` (20) — it's a *container surface* (Dialog/Card family) derived via the §2.1.2 inverse rule from its inner Button-L + outer pad, not a control-tier derivation. Same reasoning kept Bottom Nav's per-item backplate at `radius-l` (20).
- **Card-class surfaces (Toast/RadioCard/inline cards at 16, Dialog/Card at 20, panels at 12)** did NOT move — they're inverse-rule-derived container surfaces, not control-height-curve consumers.

**Rejected alternatives for the flatten:**
- **L→14 / XL→16 (hold the old +2 cascade but cap XL at 16)** — XL at 16/48 = 33% still reads a touch rounder than the 29-31% small tiers, and 16 collides with the card-chrome tier (`radius-m`), muddying the control-vs-card radius distinction. Rejected.
- **L→10 / XL→12 (push L down to match M)** — makes L and M visually identical (both ~30% but L would be 10/40 = 25%, reading squarer than M), losing the gentle size progression. Rejected.
- **Keep L/XL as-is, just rename "soft-pill" to "intentional"** — the retcon-rationalization path the M-tier history (above) explicitly warns against. Rejected.

The chosen L→12 / XL→14 keeps a 1-step-per-tier progression (8→8→10→12→14) with every ratio in the 29-33% band — the subtler curve.

### A.3 §6.1.5 Nested text-wrapper padding — rule introduction (May 2026)

The minimal-offset rule was introduced after Banner Rev E and Toast Rev D landed and the third such component (a hypothetical Snackbar) was being planned. Before A.3, the body-wrapper padding values (`2V / 4H`) lived as anonymous numbers inside the Banner and Toast CSS; the *why* lived only in inline comments and history entries. Promoting it to a system rule (§6.1.5) means the next consumer of the three-padded-slots architecture doesn't have to re-derive the padding from scratch.

**Why a separate rule and not a row in the §6.1.1 padding table.** §6.1.1 documents H>V asymmetry for **surfaces** (the outermost rounded container around text). §6.1.5 documents padding for a **nested wrapper** sitting inside a surface that already owns its own padding — a structurally different problem. Stacking the §6.1.1 surface-level pair (e.g., 12H / 8V) on top of a nested wrapper gives 24H visible inset for text vs 16V — too much asymmetry, text floats right of the leading icon's vertical axis. The minimal-offset rule resolves to 2V / 4H instead, giving 12H visible and 13V visible (V dominant by ~1 from line-box leading per §6.1.4), keeping icon and text reading as one cluster.

**Why "one ramp step up from V" and not "match line-box leading exactly."** The naïve answer is "horizontal padding should equal leading-per-side" (~3px for 14/20, ~4px for 16/24, ~2px for 12/16). That's correct at the target-inset level, but it requires per-tier off-ramp values (3, 4, 2 aren't all on the spacing ramp — `space-3xs` is 2, `space-2xs` is 4, `space-xs` is 6, nothing at 3). "One ramp step above V" is the closest on-ramp approximation across font tiers, and Primer Banner / Material 3 Snackbar / Figma's notification components all carry the same 1-step H>V delta on their nested text wrappers — none compute per-tier leading values either.

**Why the rule was scoped to the 2-layer architecture and not generalized to every text-bearing surface**: §6.1.5's coverage audit (May 2026) surveyed every text-bearing surface in the system — Banner, Toast, Dialog, Card, Tooltip, Composer, Dropdown menu item, Select panel item, Nav item, Combobox. Only Banner and Toast have the 2-layer architecture (surface + nested text wrapper, with icon + text + buttons sharing one row). Dialog and Card stack their text sections vertically with no row siblings; Tooltip has at most a trailing kbd handled by the §6.1.2 per-edge override; Composer has a single textarea cell (no title+description stack needing V containment) and uses §6.1.2 H-only padding instead; list-row components (Dropdown / Select panel / Nav) flex-center their labels per §9.3. Adding a nested wrapper to Dialog / Card / Tooltip would not solve any architectural problem and would push text inward without justification.

**The minimal-offset rule is closed for review** unless a real consumer ships text at a different font tier where the leading delta visibly demands a different pattern. Components in the standalone-surface case (§6.1.1) explicitly do not adopt this rule — they are not non-compliant, they are a different architecture.

### A.4 §6.1.5 Nested text-wrapper padding — extension to standalone surfaces (May 13, 2026)

A.3 closed the rule as "Banner/Toast-only at the 2V/4H tier — Dialog/Card/Tooltip don't need it because their stacked architecture doesn't have row siblings competing for corner inset." That stance was overridden one week later (May 13) on user direction: **every text node in every component should live inside a nested wrapper.**

**Why the override.** A.3's framing said "Dialog/Card/Tooltip don't NEED wrappers because surface padding already does V breathing and they have no row siblings." Both halves of that argument were true but missed a separate concern: **surface padding was doing two jobs**, outer-chrome breathing AND text-leading absorption. When Dialog's surface pad tightened from 16 to 12 (May 2026 inverse-rule cascade), the text's leading-induced offset (§6.1.4) became visibly off relative to the new tighter horizontal chrome edges — text appeared to "stick up" because surface pad no longer absorbed the leading delta as a side effect. The fix: separate the two jobs by giving text its own wrapper (0V/2H tier) so surface pad is purely about outer chrome, and the wrapper absorbs leading delta independently.

**Why two tiers, not one universal value.** The 2V/4H tier (Banner/Toast) and the 0V/2H tier (Dialog/Card/Tooltip) reflect a real architectural distinction:
- **Row architecture (Banner/Toast)**: icon + text + buttons share one horizontal row. The text wrapper needs visible structure (2V) so it reads as its own cell between sibling corners; the 4H gives cluster cohesion plus leading absorption.
- **Stacked architecture (Dialog/Card/Tooltip)**: text sits in its own section vertically separated from chrome. No row siblings, so V breathing comes from the surface pad (12 in Dialog/Card, 8 in Tooltip) — wrapper's 0V is sufficient; only 2H is needed for leading delta.

Both tiers follow the same H = V + one ramp step rule. The difference is only the V baseline.

**Icon wrapper rule (formalized)**. When icons sit beside text wrappers (Banner/Toast leading icon today; future Snackbar / Drawer header), the icon wrapper's V matches the text wrapper's V. Horizontal is symmetric (= V), not V+1 — icons are hard-edged and don't have line-box leading to absorb. The two-tier table:

| Text wrapper | Icon wrapper |
|---|---|
| 0V / 2H | 0V / 0H |
| 2V / 4H | 2V / 2H |

**Coverage extension (May 13 audit)**:
- `.ds-dialog__header-text` and `.ds-dialog__body` — added 0V/2H padding via `--dlg-text-px` (= `space-3xs` 2px).
- `.ds-card__header-text` and `.ds-card__body` — added 0V/2H padding via inline `var(--space-3xs, 2px)`.
- `.ds-tooltip__text` and `.ds-tooltip--multiline .ds-tooltip__text` — added 0V/2H padding via inline `var(--space-3xs, 2px)`.

Composer keeps its existing `--cmp-text-px` H-only override (different 2-layer pattern — textarea between button clusters, no title+description stack). List-row components (Dropdown, Select panel, Nav) keep their flex-centered labels (1-layer architecture).

**The rule is now universal across the system** — text always lives in a wrapper, choose the tier by architecture (row vs stacked).

### A.5 §9.6 Dialog — Figma 306-805 partial redesign, dimensional/type reverts (May 13, 2026)

Dialog underwent a two-step revision: an initial pass that adopted four Figma-derived changes (node 306-805), followed by a corrective revert that kept the **architectural shift** but rolled back the **dimensional and typographic overrides** under the system's "no one-off custom overrides" rule (§1.4 parameterizability contract / §2.2.1 anti-bloat). The two passes are recorded together so the next person doesn't relitigate either direction.

**What was kept — the structural architecture (system-rule expression, NOT one-off)**:

1. **`.ds-dialog__header` flex-row with `.ds-dialog__header-text` wrapper + inline `.ds-dialog__close`**. The close button is now a flex-end sibling of the title block inside the header row, not absolutely positioned at `top/right: --dlg-px`. This lets the header participate in the dialog's column flex layout — it picks up the surface pad uniformly without offset math, and the entire layout reads as three padded slots (header row → body wrapper → footer row) all anchored to the same outer ring.
2. **0V/2H text wrappers** on `.ds-dialog__header-text` and `.ds-dialog__body` via `--dlg-text-px` (`space-3xs` = 2). This is the §6.1.5 standalone-surface tier of the minimal-offset rule — applies system-wide to Dialog, Card, Tooltip. The 2H absorbs the line-box leading delta between text cap-top and the horizontal chrome edge; 0V relies on the surface pad to provide V breathing. **Separating concerns** — surface pad owns outer chrome, text wrapper owns leading absorption — fixes the prior overload where the surface pad was doing two jobs and neither read optimally.

Both changes express the §6.1.5 rule (which applies to multiple components, not just Dialog) and the architectural three-padded-slots pattern (which applies to Banner / Toast / Dialog / Card / Tooltip). They are **system-rule expressions**, not Dialog-specific overrides.

**What was reverted — the dimensional and typographic Figma-specific values**:

| Property | Figma rev (briefly shipped) | Reverted to | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Outer radius | `radius-xl` (24) | `radius-l` (20) | Inverse-rule cascade (§2.1.2): outer = Button S radius (8) + surface pad (12) = 20. Picking 24 was a free taste choice not derived from inner content — exactly the "outer picked by surface-family taste" anti-pattern §2.1.2's inverse rule warns against. The 20 value lands on the §2.1.2 soft-regime ideal exactly (inset 12 > ½ outer 10 → soft classification, but ideal value 8 lands on the ramp = no compromise). Card sits at the same 20 tier with the same math; Dialog and Card are now peer primary surfaces in the same shape family, differentiated by behavior (modal vs inline) rather than radius. |
| Section gap | `space-l` (16) | `space-s` (8) | 3:2 padding-to-gap ratio with `--dlg-px` (12). Padding owns *outer chrome*; gap owns *internal separation*. A 1:1 ratio (or worse, pad < gap) flattens the visual hierarchy — every white-space block reads as "container chrome," the dialog feels uniformly airy. The 12/8 split lets padding establish the outer frame and gap establish a subtler second-level rhythm. Industry alignment for compact dialogs: shadcn 16/12 (4:3), Atlassian 16/12 (4:3), Linear 20/16 (5:4) — all keep gap < padding. Our 12/8 (3:2) sits at the tighter end of the band. |
| Title type | `body/m` (14/20) Medium | `body/l` (16/24) Medium | Hierarchy preservation: title must be the visual anchor of the header. Body sits at `body/m` (14/20) to match Input / Button / Select baseline; title must read one tier larger so the header zone is unambiguously the title's. Weight 500 on title is justified under the §5.1.1 three-part necessity test: locally dominant + cannot scale up (already at `body/l`, the largest body tier) + color cannot carry contrast (both title and body use `text/primary`) → all three criteria pass → weight 500 sanctioned. |
| Body type | `body/l` (16/24) Regular | `body/m` (14/20) Regular | Body MUST be smaller than title to preserve hierarchy. The Figma rev inverted this on "body IS the message" reasoning, but the inversion flattened title-vs-body distinction at the size axis (only weight 500 separated them) — the user's "no one-offs" feedback effectively said: don't ship a dialog where size hierarchy is sacrificed for a Figma reference. 14/20 also keeps body type aligned with Input / Button / Select so prose and embedded controls share the same baseline. |
| Close button tier | M (32×32) | S (28×28) | **Tier-by-context rule** (restated, corrected): Toast = XS (24, compact float); **Dialog = S (28, primary modal surface)**; Banner = S (28, persistent inline). S — not M — is the system's standard inline close tier for primary container surfaces. The Figma rev escalated to M on "fits the larger body type (16/24)" reasoning, but with body reverted to 14/20, that justification evaporates. S is the right peer of Banner's close (both are persistent acknowledgment surfaces, both want the same inline close affordance scale). |

**Why the revert**. The user's instruction was direct: "revert the padding and corner radius change overrides if it doesnt make sense, the figma values were just an example, i dont want any one off component custom overrides." The Figma redesign's four dimensional changes (radius, gap, title size, body size, close tier) each had Figma-source justification but lacked **system-derived** justification — they were taste choices imported from a reference, not consequences of system rules cascading through the inverse rule, pad-to-gap ratio, hierarchy preservation, or tier-by-context. Under §1.4's parameterizability contract, dimensional values in components must trace back to a base ramp token or a derivation formula composing base tokens. Picking `radius-xl` because Figma showed 24 doesn't pass that test; deriving `radius-l` from `Button S radius + surface pad` via §2.1.2 does.

**The two architectural changes that were kept (`__header-text` wrapper + inline close + 0V/2H text padding) DO pass the parameterizability test** because they're expressions of §6.1.5 (the system-wide text-wrapper padding rule) and the three-padded-slots pattern (shared by Banner, Toast, Dialog, Card, Tooltip). They reflow correctly when base tokens scale: change `--space-3xs` from 2 → 3 and every text wrapper picks up the new offset uniformly; change `--space-m` from 12 → 14 and the surface pad scales with the radius cascade automatically.

**The lesson**. Figma references are inspirational; the system's rules are normative. When a reference value conflicts with a rule-derived value, the rule wins unless the reference value has a stronger system-derivable justification. The architectural insight from the Figma reference (separate text wrappers, inline close as flex sibling) was independently valuable and was retained; the dimensional choices around it were not, and were reverted.

### A.6 §6.1.5 Nested text-wrapper padding — extension to standalone text slots (May 13, 2026)

A.4 promoted the §6.1.5 rule from "two-row notification containers only" to "all contained surfaces with text wrappers" (Banner, Toast, Dialog, Card, Tooltip). A.6 extends it one step further: the rule now applies to **every text-bearing element in the system, including standalone text outside any contained surface** — Field labels, Field captions, Fieldset legends, Fieldset captions, Fieldset validation messages, and any other "naked text" rendered directly into the document flow.

**What changed**:
- `.ds-field__label`, `.ds-field__caption`, `.ds-fieldset__legend`, `.ds-fieldset__caption`, and `.ds-fieldset__validation` each gain `padding: 0 var(--space-3xs, 2px)` (the 0V/2H standalone-surface tier).
- Spec §6.1.5 rule statement updated from "applies to text wrappers in EVERY surface" to "applies to EVERY text-bearing element in EVERY surface, including standalone text outside contained surfaces." The choice between tier 1 (0V/2H) and tier 2 (2V/4H) is now framed exclusively by surrounding architecture (stacked-or-standalone vs row-sibling), not by whether the text lives in a contained surface.
- Audit table extended with five new rows for the Field/Fieldset standalone-text members, all at 0V/2H tier.
- Spec acknowledges that whether the padding lives on a wrapper `<div>` or directly on the text element is an implementation detail — visible padding is what counts.

**Why it changed**. User feedback after the A.4/A.5 work: "even labels and captions and basically all text should have a 2px horizontal padding div added. add this to all components. identify candidates like banner where it would be necessary to have 4px horizontal and 2px vertical padding instead, either to have more padding or to align with an icon." The Figma reference (node 306-805) showed a Field label inside a Dialog body where the label's text started 2px to the LEFT of the surrounding body text — visibly inconsistent because the Dialog body has its own 0V/2H wrapper but the label inside it didn't.

The root cause: previous spec language scoped §6.1.5 to "components" (contained surfaces). Form chrome — Field, Fieldset, captions — lived outside that scope and rendered at 0H padding. When such chrome was placed inside a contained surface that DID carry text-wrapper padding (Dialog body, Card body), the nested label's text edge diverged from the surrounding body text edge by exactly 2px. The fix is to apply the same 0V/2H tier to standalone text directly, so the rule is local and uniform regardless of where the label sits.

**Why 0V/2H specifically for standalone text**:
1. **Vertical**: standalone text doesn't sit inside a padded surface that needs internal "leading absorption" — the text uses natural margin spacing (e.g., `margin-bottom: var(--space-s, 8px)` on the label, `margin-top: var(--space-2xs, 4px)` on captions) which already does the V-axis work. 0V keeps the rule simple and avoids inflating the rendered height of every form element by 4px.
2. **Horizontal**: 2px is the minimum offset that absorbs the line-box leading delta between cap-top and the parent-content-edge. This consistent 2H buffer means a label rendered inside a Dialog body (which itself has 0V/2H wrapper) lines up at 2H + 0 = 2H from the Dialog's content area, and a label rendered inside a Card body (also 0V/2H wrapper) lines up the same way. Industry alignment is rare here (Material 3, shadcn, Radix, Atlassian, Apple HIG all leave Field labels at 0 horizontal padding), but the consistency-across-nesting benefit outweighs the 2px shift.

**Banner is a "tier-2 candidate" but stays at tier-1 by accident**. The user explicitly named Banner as a candidate where "4 horizontal + 2 vertical might be necessary, either for more padding or to align with an icon." Banner's body wrapper is already at tier-2 (2V/4H via `--bn-body-py` 2 + `--bn-body-px` 4) — it doesn't need additional changes from this A.6 work. The naming was a forward-looking flag, not a request to re-pad Banner.

**Scope and non-changes**:
- Selection control labels (`.ds-checkbox-row__label`, `.ds-radio-row__label`, `.ds-switch-row__label`) do NOT receive standalone padding. They live inside row wrappers (`.ds-checkbox-row`, etc.) that already apply their own padding via the row container, so adding 2H padding on the inner label would double-pad against the row's own inset.
- RadioCard's title/description text also doesn't change — those texts live inside the card's flex column under the card-container's own padding scheme, similar to selection rows.
- Input/Select/Textarea internal text doesn't change — their padding is owned by `--inp-px` / `--sel-px` / `--txa-px` per §2.3 control ramp, which is the canonical "control-internal padding" rule and orthogonal to §6.1.5's "text-wrapper offset" rule.
- Body prose (paragraphs in a Dialog body, free-form descriptions in a Card body) inherits the existing wrapper's 0V/2H padding — no per-paragraph change needed.

**The lesson**. §6.1.5 was framed as "text wrappers inside contained surfaces" through A.4. A.6 corrects the framing: the rule is "every text node carries its own minimal inset, regardless of surrounding architecture." Whether the inset lives on a wrapper element or directly on the text is an implementation detail. The visible padding is what makes the rule legible. Standalone text without padding is drift; contained surfaces without text wrappers are drift; both are now caught by the same audit.

### A.7 §6.1.5 Nested text-wrapper padding — row-control / DropZone / DatePicker pass + parent-padding-satisfaction clarification (May 13, 2026)

A.6 declared §6.1.5 universal but only audited the Field/Fieldset standalone-text slots. The user's follow-up question — "was this applied to all components, even smaller atomic elements like buttons and badges? i would like the text rule to be applied in ALL cases of text" — surfaced two unfinished pieces of work:

1. **Standalone-text slots that A.6 missed.** Selection-control row labels and descriptions (Checkbox row, Radio row, Switch row), RadioCard label and description, DropZone title and hint, and DatePicker title were all rendering as bare text inside their parent layouts without the 0V/2H wrapper. A.6's "no double-padding against the row's own inset" reasoning was over-cautious — these labels DO need the 2H offset to keep their visible left edge consistent with any sibling text inside the same row layout, and the parent row's padding doesn't apply equally to every nested element (row wrappers typically pad the outer container, not each text member). The fix: apply `padding: 0 var(--space-3xs, 2px)` directly to `.ds-checkbox-row__label`, `.ds-checkbox-row__description`, the matching Radio/Switch row classes, `.ds-radio-card__label`, `.ds-radio-card__description`, `.ds-dropzone__title`, `.ds-dropzone__hint`, and `.ds-date-picker__title`.

2. **Atomic inline-flex labels (Button, Badge, Nav item, Menu item, etc.)**. The user explicitly asked whether the rule had been applied to buttons and badges. A wrapper-padding approach there would have been wrong — adding `padding: 0 2px` on an inner `<span>` inside a Button would (a) be redundant against the Button's own `--btn-px` (which is 6–16 per tier — far exceeding the 2px the wrapper would provide) and (b) inflate the Button's icon↔text gap by 2px on each side, breaking the §2.3 control-gap table. The correct framing is that **the rule has two satisfaction paths**: direct wrapper padding (for block text in surfaces and standalone text), and parent-padding satisfaction (for atomic inline-flex labels whose parent control already pads exceedingly past the 2px floor). The atomic-label case isn't drift — it's the second documented path.

**What changed**:
- CSS: `padding: 0 var(--space-3xs, 2px)` added to 11 new selectors covering row-control labels/descriptions, RadioCard label/description, DropZone title/hint, and DatePicker title.
- §6.1.5 spec: a new "Two satisfaction paths" subsection documents direct-wrapper-padding vs parent-padding-satisfaction with worked examples for Button/Badge/Nav item explaining why a wrapper there would be wrong.
- §6.1.5 audit table: extended from 17 rows to 31 rows, adding entries for every atomic inline-flex label (Button, Badge, Chip, kbd, Input value, Select trigger value, Tabs trigger, Bottom Nav item, Segmented Control item, Breadcrumb item) AND for the newly-padded row-control / RadioCard / DropZone / DatePicker slots. Each row now carries a "Path" column making the satisfaction path explicit.
- Closing summary: rewritten to enumerate three rules for future components — direct-wrapper 2V/4H for row architecture, direct-wrapper 0V/2H for stacked architecture or standalone, parent-padding satisfaction for atomic inline-flex controls.

**Why this completes the rule (and why A.6's "no row-label padding" reasoning was wrong)**. A.6 declared standalone text outside contained surfaces needed 0V/2H but explicitly excluded "Selection control labels (`.ds-checkbox-row__label`, `.ds-radio-row__label`, `.ds-switch-row__label`)" on the rationale that "they live inside row wrappers (`.ds-checkbox-row`, etc.) that already apply their own padding via the row container, so adding 2H padding on the inner label would double-pad against the row's own inset." That reasoning conflated two different things — the row's padding (applied to the row's outer edge) doesn't propagate to each text member inside the row layout; the row uses `display: flex` with `gap` between children, not internal padding around each child. So the label's actual left edge against its left sibling (the checkbox/radio/switch control) was governed by `gap` alone, not by the row's outer padding. When a row label rendered next to surrounding body text (e.g., a Field's caption rendered above and a row label below), the two text nodes had different visible left edges — exactly the inconsistency A.6 was trying to fix elsewhere.

The corrected understanding: A.6 was right that the row's *outer* edge gets padding, but wrong that this propagates internally. Each text member inside a row still needs its own 2H offset. The "double-padding" worry was misplaced — adding `padding: 0 2px` to a row label doesn't double-pad against the row's outer padding; it gives the label's text edge a consistent 2px offset from its parent's flex-content-edge, which is what every other text node in the system now has.

**Why atomic inline-flex labels don't get a wrapper** (worked through):
1. **Parent padding already exceeds the floor.** Button M's `--btn-px` is `space-s` (8) — 4× the 2px the wrapper rule would provide. Badge M's `--bdg-px` is also 8. Nav item M's `--ni-px` is 8. Tabs trigger M's `--tab-px` is 8. Every atomic inline-flex control has a parent padding ≥ 4px at every tier — the rule's 2H job is already done at the parent level.
2. **Wrapping inflates icon-to-text gap.** §2.3 specifies the icon↔text gap per tier (`space-2xs` 4 at S/M, `space-xs` 6 at L, `space-s` 8 at XL). Adding `padding: 0 2px` on an inner label `<span>` would shift the label inward by 2px from the wrapper's perspective, but the wrapper's outer edge stays at the same flex position — net effect is the gap appears 2px wider than the spec. Multiple Buttons in a row would look "looser" than intended.
3. **No surface edge to mediate.** The rule's intent is to absorb the line-box leading delta between cap-top and the **surrounding surface's hard edge**. Inside a Button, the text's "surrounding surface" is the button's content-area (the area inside `--btn-px`), and the leading delta is absorbed by the parent's pad already. There's no second surface edge inside the Button against which a wrapper would mediate.

**Industry alignment for the parent-padding-satisfaction path**: every modern design system (shadcn, Material 3, Atlassian, Apple HIG, Primer, Vercel) renders Button labels as direct text children of the button element, with no inner wrapper. None pad their button labels independently of `--btn-px`. The same holds for Badge text, Tabs trigger labels, Nav item labels, etc. The "every text gets a wrapper" framing only makes sense for block text inside surfaces; the inline-flex case has always been satisfied by parent padding industry-wide.

**The lesson**. The rule's intent is "no text glyph kisses a hard surface edge directly." There are two ways to satisfy that intent: (a) put padding inside the text's immediate wrapper, or (b) let the text's parent control's padding do the job. Both are valid; pick by architecture. Atomic inline-flex labels take path (b); everything else takes path (a). The audit catches drift in both directions — block text without a wrapper IS drift; inline-flex labels with an unnecessary inner wrapper IS also drift (inflated gaps, redundant padding). A.7 closes the rule by documenting both paths explicitly.

### A.8 §6.1.5 Nested text-wrapper padding — universal direct-wrapper ratification (May 13, 2026)

A.7 closed the rule with two satisfaction paths (direct wrapper vs parent-padding satisfaction). The user pushed back the next day: *"so there should also be a div around this text in button with 2px horizontal padding added to it. same with badge. go through each component and validate how this can be implemented to bring better optical horizontal spacing for text in containers."* And earlier: *"i would like the text rule to be applied in ALL cases of text."*

The user wanted the rule applied universally — every text node, including atomic inline-flex labels (Button, Badge, Chip, Nav item, Menu item, Select panel item, Tabs trigger, Bottom Nav, Segmented Control, Breadcrumb, Select trigger value). A.7's "parent-padding satisfaction" path was retired.

**Why universal won over two-path** (per the user's directive and direct comparison):

1. **Consistency reads stronger than the gap delta**. The old objection was that an inner wrapper inflates Button M's icon↔text gap from 4px (`space-2xs`) to 6px (4 + 2 from the wrapper's H pad). In side-by-side review, the looser rhythm reads more breathable, not crowded — and the visible improvement of "every text node in the system carries the same wrapper" outweighs the deviation from the §2.3 control-gap exact targets. The control-gap table is now read as **minimum declared gaps**, with visible gap = declared + 2H when text wrapper is present.
2. **Removes the cognitive cost of "which path does this component use?"** Two-path systems drift over time because the path-selection judgement is subjective ("is this 'atomic enough' for parent-padding to suffice?"). Universal direct wrappers remove the question — every text node gets a wrapper, end of decision.
3. **The wrapper enables density tier scaling without per-component code**. A future compact-mode could redefine `--space-3xs` from 2 to 1 and every text node would re-flow simultaneously. Path-(b) elements would need per-component re-tuning.

**What changed**:
- CSS: added `padding: 0 var(--space-3xs, 2px)` to selectors covering atomic inline-flex labels — `.ds-btn__label`, `.ds-badge__label` (new selector), `.ds-chip__label` (new selector), `.ds-nav-item__label`, `.ds-menu__label`, `.ds-select-panel__text`, `.ds-select__value` / `.ds-select__placeholder`, `.ds-tabs__label` (new selector), `.ds-segmented-control__label` (new selector), `.ds-breadcrumb__label` (new selector), `.ds-bottom-nav__label`.
- CSS: adjusted `.ds-menu__group-label` and `.ds-select-panel__group-label` to pad by `calc(parent + 2H)` so group-label text visually aligns with the now-2px-wrapped item-label text below.
- JS: modified `badgeEl`, `breadcrumbEl`, `tabsEl`, `segmentedControlEl` to wrap label text in named span classes (`__label` / `__text`) rather than emitting bare text or unclassed `<span>`. The other components (`buttonEl`, `chipEl`, `navItemEl`, `menuItemEl`, `selectPanelEl`, `selectEl`, `bottomNavEl`) already emitted named-class spans; only the CSS needed the padding rule.
- §6.1.5 spec rewritten: replaced "two satisfaction paths" framing with "universal direct-wrapper padding" framing. Audit table rebuilt with element-class column and tier column; atomic inline-flex labels now show `0V / 2H` instead of `parent-padding`. Exclusions list explicitly enumerates the 6 cases where wrapper doesn't apply (icon-only controls, Avatar initials, Pagination ellipsis/status, native inputs, table cells, kbd).
- Closing summary collapsed from three paths to two tiers — `0V/2H` (default, for everything including atomic labels) and `2V/4H` (only row-architecture surfaces like Banner/Toast).

**Components NOT padded (explicit exclusions, documented in audit table)**:
1. **Icon-only controls** (Dialog close, Banner dismiss, chevron buttons) — no text to wrap.
2. **Avatar initials** — centered char(s) in a fixed-shape backplate; padding would off-center.
3. **Pagination ellipsis** — centered `…` glyph; padding doesn't help (centered char already has letterboxed breathing).
4. **Pagination status text** — already has 8px padding on the same element (parent satisfaction directly on the text element, not via an inner wrapper).
5. **Input/Textarea value text** — native form controls can't take wrapper elements inside; parent `--inp-px` ≥ 6px satisfies the inset.
6. **Table cells** — `<td>` / `<th>` cell padding (8/12 at M) IS the wrapper.
7. **Tooltip / Dropdown `<kbd>` badges** — single-char chrome with `padding: 0 4px` on the kbd itself; the kbd IS the wrapper.

**Why the icon↔text gap inflation is acceptable** (the trade A.7 was wrong to reject): the visual cost is small (2px on the gap), and it applies uniformly across every text-bearing control in the system, so the proportional rhythm holds. A Button with `gap: 4 + 2H wrapper` reads as a 6-gap Button rather than a 4-gap Button — but every other Button in the same row also reads as a 6-gap Button, so the row composition is internally consistent. The §2.3 table is now read as "declared gap floor" rather than "exact visible gap"; visible gap = declared + 2H wrapper. No re-tuning of the table needed.

**The two-path framing's failure mode in retrospect**: A.7 argued that wrappers on atomic labels would be "drift, not improvement" — but that was framed against a worry (gap inflation) the user had already weighed and accepted. The architectural distinction between "block text in surfaces" and "atomic inline-flex labels" was an internal author concern, not a user-facing one. From the user's perspective, *every text node should look the same way* — same buffer, same rhythm — and any path-selection logic that produced "this one has a wrapper, that one doesn't" was an inconsistency, not a feature. A.8 ratifies the universal rule and retires the architectural distinction.

**Industry alignment for the universal approach**: this is a non-standard choice (most modern systems don't wrap atomic button labels — shadcn / Material 3 / Apple HIG / Vercel / Primer all render Button labels as direct text children). Our system departs because we prioritize internal consistency across nested vs atomic vs standalone text over industry convention; the 2px text-wrapper rule is now system-wide and universal, even where industry leaves it absent.

**The lesson** (A.8 closing): the rule's intent is universally satisfied via the direct-wrapper path. The two-path framing was a transitional state where the author justified parent-padding satisfaction as "good enough" for atomic cases; in practice the user wanted full consistency. The audit catches drift in one direction now (block text or atomic label without a wrapper IS drift); the parent-padding satisfaction path is retired as a backup option. New components ship with the wrapper from day one.

### A.9 §6.1.5 + §2.3 — universal gap compensation, supersedes A.8 "gap inflation acceptance" (May 13, 2026)

A.8 closed with "the icon↔text gap inflation is acceptable" — a trade where every Button / Badge / Chip / Nav item / etc. read 2px looser than its §2.3 control-gap target because the universal text wrapper added 2H between the icon and the text-wrapper edge. The user revisited the next day: *"if you must adjust the gap between text and icon as a result of this extra text padding, then you can do so by subtracting 2px? look into this and decide what would be best. i want to be consistent. so all components will adopt this change with no exceptions. be thorough."*

A.8's gap-inflation acceptance was a transitional compromise. The mechanically correct answer — keep BOTH the universal wrapper AND the §2.3 calibration — is to subtract 2px from the declared `gap` everywhere a text wrapper is present, so the visible icon-to-text distance lands on the §2.3 target.

**The mechanic**: every component whose declared `gap` was calibrated against the §2.3 control-gap table now uses `gap: calc(var(--space-*) - var(--space-3xs, 2px))`. Example at Button M: `--btn-gap: var(--space-2xs, 4px)` becomes `--btn-gap: calc(var(--space-2xs, 4px) - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` = 2px declared in CSS, + 2px from the label wrapper = **4px visible** = §2.3 M-tier target exactly.

**Why subtraction won over A.8's acceptance**:
1. **The §2.3 control-gap table calibration survives the wrapper change.** A.8 implicitly re-tuned the table to a +2 floor; A.9 keeps the table as the visible target, just shifts the declared values 2px tighter on compensated components. The §2.3 numbers don't drift.
2. **Side-by-side comparison after A.8 showed the looser rhythm read slightly off** — Button M at 6px visible vs the original 4 visible across the dense-default tier produced a noticeable density shift on rows of icon+text controls. The compensation restores the dense-default identity.
3. **The compensation is mechanical and one-pass** — there's no per-component judgement involved. Subtract 2px from every horizontal flex-sibling icon↔text-wrapper pair calibrated to §2.3. The exclusions (Toast/Banner row layout, Bottom Nav vertical stacking, Breadcrumb inter-segment, Tooltip `--keys-below`, Input/Textarea native value, Composer inter-row/inter-cluster, Dialog inter-zone) all share the property that their gap is NOT an icon-to-text-wrapper pair, so compensation doesn't apply.

**What changed (CSS in `index.html`)**:
| Component | Variable / selector | Before | After |
|---|---|---|---|
| Button | `--btn-gap` (per S/M/L/XL tier) | `var(--space-*)` | `calc(var(--space-*) - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Badge | `--bdg-gap` (per S/M tier) | `var(--space-2xs, 4px)` | `calc(var(--space-2xs, 4px) - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Nav vertical + horizontal | `--ni-gap` (per tier) | `var(--space-*)` | `calc(... - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Chip | `--chip-gap` (per XS/S tier) | `var(--space-2xs, 4px)` | `calc(... - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Dropdown menu item | `--mnu-item-gap` + inline `.ds-menu__item` rule | `var(--space-2xs, 4px)` | `calc(... - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Select panel item | inline `.ds-select-panel__item` rule | `var(--space-2xs, 4px)` | `calc(... - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Select trigger | `--sel-gap` (per tier) | `var(--space-*)` | `calc(... - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Tabs trigger | `--tab-gap` (per S/M/L tier) | `var(--space-*)` | `calc(... - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Segmented Control item | `--seg-gap` (per tier) | `var(--space-*)` | `calc(... - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Breadcrumb inner-gap | `--bc-inner-gap` (per S/M tier) | `var(--space-2xs, 4px)` | `calc(... - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Tooltip inline gap | inline `.ds-tooltip` rule | `var(--space-s, 8px)` | `calc(var(--space-s, 8px) - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| Checkbox/Radio/Switch row | inline row rules | `var(--space-*)` | `calc(... - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |
| RadioCard | `--rc-gap` | `var(--space-s, 8px)` | `calc(var(--space-s, 8px) - var(--space-3xs, 2px))` |

**Components explicitly NOT compensated** (and why):
| Component | Variable | Why no compensation |
|---|---|---|
| Toast | n/a (`.ds-toast__row` has `gap: 0`) | Icon-body-button spacing comes from `__body` own H padding, not from a flex `gap`. Wrapper math already balanced inside the body's row layout. |
| Banner | n/a (same row architecture as Toast) | Same as Toast — body H padding owns the spacing, not a `gap`. |
| Bottom Nav | n/a (icon + label stack vertically) | No horizontal icon↔text `gap` to compensate. The wrapper sits below the icon, padded vertically, not horizontally adjacent to a flex sibling. |
| Breadcrumb inter-segment | `--bc-gap` | Gap is between chevron-separated segments, not between icon and text inside one segment. The text wrapper sits inside one segment and doesn't affect inter-segment spacing. |
| Tooltip kbd-cluster gap | `--tt-keys-gap` | Gap between adjacent kbd badges inside a cluster (kbd-to-kbd), not text-to-kbd. Kbd is a chrome atom with its own internal padding, not a text wrapper. |
| Input / Textarea | n/a (native form control) | Browsers don't allow wrapper elements inside `<input>` / `<textarea>`. The parent `--inp-px` already provides the inset on the parent element. |
| Composer | `--cmp-gap` / `--cmp-cluster-gap` | Gaps are between inter-row sections (vertical) and between sibling Buttons inside a cluster (horizontal). Neither is an icon-to-text-wrapper pair. The cluster's Buttons already carry their own internal gap compensation via Button's `--btn-gap`. |
| Dialog | `--dlg-gap` | Vertical gap between header / body / footer sections. Not an icon-to-text pair. |

**§6.1.5 spec rewrite**: replaced A.8's "gap-inflation acceptance" paragraph (which said "the table is now read as minimum declared gaps, visible = declared + 2") with the "2px gap-compensation rule" paragraph (which says "declared = §2.3 token − 2px wrapper offset; visible = §2.3 target exactly"). Added a full compensation-vs-exclusion table inline in the spec for any future component author to consult before declaring a `gap`. The "Universal direct-wrapper padding" rationale bullet 1 was rewritten from "consistency reads stronger than the 2px gap delta" to "consistency reads stronger than per-component gap exceptions" — the gap delta is no longer a concession, it's mechanically resolved.

**§2.3 spec addition**: added an "Interpretation note" right after the "Gap is non-negotiable" paragraph stating that the §2.3 table values represent the **visible** icon-to-text distance, not the declared `gap` token. Worked example for Button M shown inline so audit doesn't drift back to the A.8 framing.

**Why A.8's framing was wrong in retrospect**: A.8 argued the gap delta was acceptable because "the proportional rhythm holds" (every Button in a row is +2, so the row is internally consistent). That's true but produces a system-wide density shift that the §2.3 table was specifically calibrated against. A.8 implicitly re-tuned the entire control-gap calibration to absorb the wrapper; A.9 restores the calibration and absorbs the wrapper via declared-value subtraction instead. The result is the same universal-wrapper consistency that A.8 wanted, with the original §2.3 density restored. The "no per-component re-tuning needed" claim A.8 made still holds — A.9's compensation is also one mechanical pass, just applied to declared `gap` values via `calc()` instead of re-tuning the §2.3 table.

**The lesson** (A.9 closing): when a universal architectural change (the text wrapper) collides with a calibrated value system (the §2.3 control-gap table), the right move is to compensate mechanically in code, not to re-tune the calibration to absorb the change. A.8's acceptance treated the §2.3 table as the variable; A.9 treats it as the invariant. The invariant should always win — the table was the result of side-by-side calibration across every control tier, and shifting it to absorb a 2px wrapper that arrived later is exactly the kind of compounding drift §1.4 was written to prevent.

### A.10 §6.1.2 + §11 Badge — `--bdg-px` collapses to the icon-formula, removes `:has()` override (May 13, 2026)

User question on a Badge M with leading icon: *"why are these paddings on the left and right different? why does badge in general have such large padding on the horizontal side, but not on the vertical side"*. DevTools showed Badge M at 6L / 8R — visibly asymmetric — and the overall horizontal padding felt loose against the 0V raw padding (with V breathing supplied by the 16px line-box in a 24px frame).

**Root cause**: the Badge had two horizontal padding rules in conflict:
- Default `--bdg-px` was tuned for **text-only** Badges using a "case-B asymmetric for text" stance — S: 6 (`space-xs`), M: 8 (`space-s`).
- A `:has(> .ds-badge__icon:first-child)` override pulled `padding-left` into the **icon-corner-symmetric** formula `(--bdg-h - --bdg-icon) / 2 - 1px` when a leading icon was present — S: ~3, M: ~5 (minus 1px border) → effectively 4L and 6L.

The override produced visibly asymmetric raw padding (4L/6R at S, 6L/8R at M) any time a Badge had a leading icon. The wider H padding on the icon-less side also made every Badge feel horizontally stretched relative to its 20–24 height.

**The fix**: collapse the two paths into one. `--bdg-px` is now derived directly from the formula `(--bdg-h - --bdg-icon) / 2` per tier — S: 4 (`space-2xs`), M: 6 (`space-xs`). Default text-only padding and icon+text padding land on the same raw value at every tier:
- Badge S: 4L / 4R, 20-tall, 12 icon → uniform 4px inset around an icon glyph (`(20 - 12) / 2 = 4 ✓`).
- Badge M: 6L / 6R, 24-tall, 12 icon → uniform 6px inset around an icon glyph (`(24 - 12) / 2 = 6 ✓`).

The `:has(> .ds-badge__icon:first-child)` override is now redundant and is removed. CSS comment added in `index.html` explaining the derivation so future audits don't reintroduce the override.

**Why the formula is the right base padding** (not the text-only "case-B asymmetric" value):
1. **Badge has both content modes** — text-only AND icon+text. The two modes need a single base padding that satisfies both. The formula value satisfies the icon-corner-symmetric requirement; the line-box's intrinsic vertical breathing (~4px above/below cap-height at 12/16 mono) provides the V breathing without raw V padding, so the H raw value can land on the formula without text reading cramped.
2. **The text-only "case-B asymmetric" framing assumed the H was free to be larger than the V** — but for a 20–24 tall pill with a 12px icon, "H larger than V" was already true at the formula value (S: 4H/0V, M: 6H/0V) because the V is genuinely 0 and the line-box fills the height. Pushing H further to 6 (S) or 8 (M) was overshooting — text already had asymmetric H>V at the formula value via the line-box mechanic.
3. **Industry alignment** — shadcn Badge sits at ~4–6 H padding per tier, Linear status pill ~4, Atlassian Lozenge ~4, Vercel Geist Tag ~6. Our pre-fix S: 6 / M: 8 was at the loose end of the band; the post-fix S: 4 / M: 6 sits at the dense end, matching the system's overall dense-default identity.

**Spec updates** (`design-system-spec.mdc`):
- §6.1.2 audit table — `Badge` row updated from "override `padding-left: calc(...)`" to "**no override needed** — `--bdg-px` is derived per tier from the formula". Override removed; rationale documented inline.
- §11 Badge entry — added "Padding is formula-derived per tier" paragraph explaining the derivation, the rev history (loose `space-s` base + `:has()` override → tight formula base, no override), and industry density alignment.

**The lesson**: when a component has both icon+text and text-only modes, the base padding should land on the value that satisfies the icon-formula requirement (the harder constraint — icons need geometric corner symmetry; text accepts V<H asymmetry from line-box leading). The override-when-icon-present pattern is a smell: it means the base value was tuned against the easier constraint (text), and the override is patching it for the harder constraint. Collapsing both modes onto the formula value removes the override entirely and produces uniform raw padding regardless of content type — exactly the "math-first, parameterizable" stance §1.4 mandates.

### A.11 §6.1.2 + §11 Chip — `--chip-px` collapses to the icon-formula, removes both `:has()` overrides (May 13, 2026)

User follow-up to A.10: *"are there other components that exist that had the same offset padding applied from the earlier horizontal padding on text rule that need to be fixed? perhaps chip?"* The audit pass found Chip carrying the same shape of bug Badge had — same family (fixed-height, dense, optional leading icon), same conflict (raw H tuned for text-only, `:has()` override pulls icon-side to the formula), same visual symptom (visibly asymmetric L/R when icon present).

**The mismatch at the entry point** (Chip S, default, 28-tall, 14-icon, 1px border):
- Default `--chip-px` was `space-s` (8) — tuned to give text-only chips comfortable case-B H>V breathing at the 28-tall tier.
- `:has(> .ds-chip__icon:first-child) { padding-left: calc((--chip-h − --chip-icon) / 2 − 1px) }` pulled the icon-side padding to `(28-14)/2 − 1 = 6` when a leading icon was present.
- Net: `6L / 8R = 2px L/R asymmetry around the chip's content`, with 6 / 6 visible inset around the icon glyph but 8H visible breathing around text — the same "two different paddings on the two sides" Badge had.

The same conflict held for the trailing `.ds-chip__dismiss` slot: a `:has(> .ds-chip__dismiss:last-child)` override pulled `padding-right` to the formula value when a dismiss button was present, giving `8L / 6R` for icon-less-but-removable chips and `6L / 6R` for icon+dismiss chips — three different padding states per chip tier instead of one.

At Chip XS (24-tall, 12-icon, 1px border) the conflict was milder (`5L / 6R = 1px asymmetry`, formula = 5, raw = 6) but the same shape.

**The fix** (identical to A.10): collapse the two paths into one. `--chip-px` is now derived from the formula directly per tier:
- Chip S: `--chip-px: var(--space-xs, 6px)` (was `space-s` 8). With 14-icon and 1px border: visible inset around an icon glyph = 1 + 6 = 7H, vertical = 1 + (28 − 2 − 14)/2 = 1 + 6 = 7V → 7H/7V ✓ uniform. Same uniform inset around the trailing dismiss (12×12 hard-edged).
- Chip XS: `--chip-px: var(--space-xs, 6px)` (unchanged — already at 6). With 12-icon and 1px border: visible inset around glyph = 7H / 6V (1px H>V), around dismiss = 7H / 6V. The 1px H bias is the correct case-B direction at the 24-tall tier (text needs visible H ≥ visible V; the line-box leading provides ~5px V breathing, so 1px H bias around a hard-edged 12-glyph is fine).

Both `:has()` overrides (`.ds-chip:has(>.ds-chip__icon:first-child)` and `.ds-chip:has(>.ds-chip__dismiss:last-child)`) are now redundant and are removed. A CSS comment block was added in their place documenting the derivation so future audits don't reintroduce them.

**Why the same fix works**: Chip and Badge sit in the same micro-component family — fixed-height, dense (20–28 tall), optional leading icon, optional trailing dismiss (Chip only), text label always present. Both families suffered from the same architectural bug: the default `--*-px` was tuned for the *text-only* visual at each tier, and the `:has()` override silently rewrote one or both edges when an icon was present. The "default + override" pattern is correct when the two constraints genuinely require different values (Button L, Input M, Nav XL — where the larger tier wants case-B H>V around text but a smaller geometric inset around the icon glyph, and the L/R difference of 2–4px at 32–48 tall is visually imperceptible). At 20–28 tall, that 2px L/R difference is proportionally too large (8–10% of height) and visibly reads as a bug.

**Why other components were audited and kept their `:has()` overrides**: the audit pass touched every `:has(>__icon)` override in the codebase. The keep-as-is verdicts:
- **Button XS / S** (24 / 28 tall): the L/R asymmetry is 1–2px and runs in the opposite direction (icon-side > text-side, e.g., S = 7L / 6R), which is the correct case-A direction for the larger absolute padding around the glyph. Imperceptible at this tier.
- **Button M / L / XL** (32 / 40 / 48): asymmetry is 2–4px with text-side > icon-side, which is case-B for text. Within the §6.1 sanctioned band (visible H/V ratio 1.0–1.5 for text-bearing surfaces), and visually correct because larger tiers want more text breathing.
- **Input / Select / Textarea M / L / XL**: same as Button M/L/XL — case-B for text, sanctioned.
- **Nav item M / L / XL**: case-B for text at the larger tiers, sanctioned.
- **Segmented Control L / XL**: case-B for text, sanctioned.
- **Menu item (all tiers)**: §9.3 explicitly documents per-tier formulas where the text-side keeps a uniform raw `--mnu-item-px: 8` across all tiers (text-only menu rows are common, so uniform text-side padding is a per-spec requirement) and the icon-side adapts via the formula. The L/R asymmetry reverses direction across tiers (S: icon < text; L: icon > text), which means there's no single "uniform" raw value that works at every tier — the per-tier override IS the right answer. Documented in §9.3, not drift.

The Badge/Chip family is the only place where (a) the surface is small enough that L/R asymmetry is proportionally large, (b) text-only mode is uncommon enough that the override-first-icon pattern was the wrong default, and (c) the formula and a text-comfortable value differ by 2 ramp steps (8 → 6) instead of 1.

**Spec updates** (`design-system-spec.mdc`):
- §6.1.2 audit table — `Chip` row updated from "override `padding-{left,right}: calc(...)`" to "**no override needed** — `--chip-px` is derived per tier from the formula". The override CSS rule was removed; rationale documented inline in `index.html`.
- §11 Chip entry — added a "Padding is formula-derived per tier (same as Badge per A.11)" paragraph linking back to the rationale.

**The closing lesson** (extends A.10): the override-when-icon-present pattern, applied across two micro-component families, hid the same architectural mismatch in both. A.10 fixed Badge in isolation; A.11 confirms the bug was a systemic one and applies the same fix to Chip. The audit pass also confirmed the fix does NOT extend to larger control families (Button, Input, Nav, etc.) where case-B asymmetry is sanctioned at scale — the difference is the height tier. **The rule for future component authors**: when a fixed-height micro-component (20–28 tall) has both icon+text and text-only modes, derive `--*-px` from the icon-formula directly; do NOT default-to-text and override-on-icon. For taller controls (≥ 32), the override-on-icon pattern remains correct because the asymmetry is imperceptible and the text breathes more, which is what case-B wants.

### B.1 §9.10 Banner — cross-system audit (May 2026 redesign)

The May 2026 Banner redesign moved from a 4px-left-stripe pattern (rev 1) to forced-different neutral bg (rev 2) to the current shared-chrome-with-Toast model (rev 3). The audit table that drove that decision:

| System | Chrome | Stripe? |
|---|---|---|
| Apple HIG (iOS / macOS) | No "Banner" component. Uses `Notification banner` (toast-like float), `Alert` (modal), or tinted text inline. iOS Settings "Software Update" notice = full-width tinted bg + colored heading, no border, no chrome at all. | ❌ |
| Material 3 Banner | `surface-container` bg + optional 1px outline + 24px supporting icon + text + action buttons. **No tones at all** — single neutral variant; severity comes from icon glyph + content. | ❌ |
| Primer (GitHub) Flash | 4 tones (default/success/warning/error). Tinted bg + **uniform 1px tone border** + icon + text + action. | ❌ |
| Atlassian SectionMessage | 5 appearances. Tinted bg + **uniform 1px tone border all around** + icon + text + actions below. | ❌ |
| shadcn Alert | 2 variants (default + destructive). Subtle bg + **uniform 1px border** + icon + title + description. | ❌ |
| Vercel Geist Note | 4 types. Tinted bg + **uniform 1px tone border** + icon + text. | ❌ |
| Linear | Quiet tinted strips, often full-width. No icon emphasis. Tinted bg only or tinted bg + subtle uniform border. | ❌ |
| Notion Callout | Tinted block + emoji/icon + text. Background only or very subtle uniform border. | ❌ |
| Stripe Notification | Tinted bg + **uniform 1px tone border** + icon + text. | ❌ |
| Sentry / Bootstrap (older) | 4px tone left stripe + tinted bg | ✅ |

**Conclusion that drove the rule**: ~9 of 10 systems use **tinted bg + uniform 1px tone border** with NO stripe. Stripes are a Bootstrap-era pattern; modern systems abandoned them because they visually conflict with generous-radius surfaces (stripe-corner-radius interaction artifact). The rev 1 stripe was dropped on this basis.

**Industry research on Toast-vs-Banner chrome differentiation** (May 2026, drove the shared-chrome decision):

| System | Toast chrome | Banner-equivalent chrome | Differentiated by chrome? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Material 3 | Snackbar — `inverse-surface` (DARK) | Banner — `surface` (LIGHT) | ✅ Yes (Android tradition) |
| Polaris (Shopify) | Toast — dark pill (DEPRECATED) | Banner — tinted bg with status icon | ✅ Yes (legacy; Toast deprecated) |
| Atlassian | Flag — light raised w/ shadow | SectionMessage — tinted bg | ⚠️ Similar light bg, hard to tell |
| **shadcn** | **Sonner — neutral theme bg + shadow** | **Alert — `bg-card` neutral** | **❌ No — same neutral chrome** |
| **Sonner standalone** | **Theme-matching white/dark + shadow** | n/a (no banner) | n/a |
| **Primer (GitHub)** | **No Toast component** | **Banner — tinted tonal bg** | n/a (no toast to compare) |
| **Chakra** | **Single `Alert` for both** | **Single `Alert` for both** | **❌ No — one component, same chrome** |
| Vercel Geist | Toast — theme bg + shadow | Note (inline) + Project Banner (full-bleed) | Mixed — Toast and Note can look similar |
| Cross-system UX literature (Supercharge Design, UX.SE, etc.) | — | — | Frames the difference as **position + persistence + prominence**, never chrome |

Material 3 is the only major system that strongly differentiates Toast (dark) from Banner (light), and that's a Material/Android tradition rooted in v1/v2 conventions. shadcn / Sonner / modern Vercel / Chakra / Primer all share chrome between Toast and Banner-equivalents or don't ship both. Cross-system UX literature describes the difference as **position + persistence + prominence**, never chrome. The current spec follows shadcn/Sonner: chrome shared, layout + behavior + shadow differentiate.

**Dropped Banner revisions**:
| Rev | Approach | Why dropped |
|---|---|---|
| Rev 1 | 4px left stripe (Sentry / Bootstrap pattern) + 1px subtle on top/right/bottom | ~9 of 10 modern systems do not use stripes; created radius-corner artifacts; user feedback was direct: "looks terrible" |
| Rev 2 | Banner neutral bg = `surface/subtle` (force a one-token chrome difference from Toast) | Created an industry inconsistency (shadcn / Sonner / Vercel / Chakra / Primer all share chrome between Toast and Banner). Forcing a chrome difference for the static docs grid was over-engineering. User feedback: "if it's better for them to look similar, change it back." |

**Dropped Banner close-button revisions** (May 2026):
- Earlier rev shipped Banner close at XS (24-tall, 12px glyph) on the rationale "close is dismissive chrome regardless of surrounding row size." That diagnosis was correct that S-with-12px-icon read at a 43% icon-to-chrome ratio (the only ramp outlier — every other tier sits at 50%) but the wrong fix: shrinking chrome was a single-component patch where the actual issue was a missing ramp value (no 14px icon size existed). The current fix added `--icon-2s: 14` to the global icon ramp (§2.3), restoring S-tier to 50% icon ratio across every consumer (Button, Input, Select, Segmented Control, Tabs, Nav vertical M, Nav horizontal S, Chip base, Breadcrumb M).

### B.2 §9.13 Card — earlier revisions

An earlier rev shipped a `--spacious` modifier (upgraded to `radius-l` 20 + `space-xl` 24 padding + `space-l` 16 gap + `radius-2s` 10 media radius). Removed per user request: the variant produced a noticeably different "feature card" silhouette but added a second card-shape vocabulary that fragmented the system's chrome. Consumers wanting feature-promo emphasis are better served by the **tone** axis (`tone="tertiary"` for the saturated brand surface) than by re-padding. If a future need for a larger-padded card emerges, reconsider — but design lessons since the rev say `--full-bleed` (edge-to-edge media) is more useful as the next Card axis than `--spacious`.

**Three earlier revs of Card typography were dropped** on the way to the current "same size, weight-only differentiation" model:
- Rev 1: Figma 295-672 1:1 (title 16/24 w500 + body 16/24 w400, both at body/l) — felt heavy and busy in the 264-square shell.
- Rev 2: Title 16/24 w400 + body 14/20 w400 (size step 16→14 carrying hierarchy) — calmer but the user later wanted "same size, different weight."
- Rev 3 (current): Both at 14/20 with weight 500/400 split, plus the gap-collapse rule so the size unification doesn't crowd the text.

**Header-actions slot removed entirely** (CSS, JS API, spec) May 10, 2026. Earlier rev had a trailing icon-button slot at the S tier, derived from the inverse-rule audit that picked Card's outer radius. Removed same day: header chrome buttons compete for attention with the title and don't compose well with the small-square card geometry. Affordances belong in the footer where buttons can carry primary/secondary hierarchy without competing.

**Default button tier S (not Figma's M)**: Figma 295-672 ships footer at M-tier (32-tall). A 1:1 implementation read as overcrowded in the 264-square shell (2 icons + 2 labels competing with title + body for attention). S aligns Card with the Dialog/Toast/Banner footer convention. The Figma's M-tier is preserved as a documented reference but not implemented.

**Variants simplified May 2026** from 8 demos to 3 + Default + Tones, per user feedback "no need to show so many variations of the card and just show how any content can go into the card, but do it simply": Header (title only), Body (title + body), Footer (title + body + 2-button stretched footer). Dropped variants (Description, Header actions, Avatar header, Split footer, separate Media demo) — they were all "Default plus one extra slot" which felt redundant.

**Card outer radius rev**: pre-May 10, 2026 Card was at `radius-xl` (24) on the "general-purpose container = largest tier" rationale. The May 10 retcon derived outer from inner via §2.1.2 inverse rule (`outer = inner_radius + inset = 8 + 12 = 20`), placing Card at `radius-l` (20) alongside Dialog. `radius-xl` (24) is now reserved for future Sheet / Drawer where inner radius 8 + spacious-tier inset 16 = 24.

**Internal dividers removed** (May 2026): an earlier rev used `border-bottom` on `.ds-card__header` and `border-top` on `.ds-card__footer` to split the card into three explicitly-divided zones. Per user request and §3.5 stroke-removability tier 3 (definitional dividers — fully removable for a flat-fill aesthetic), all internal dividers were removed. Padding moved from per-region (`padding: var(--cd-pad)` on header, body, and footer separately) to the **card root** (`padding: var(--cd-pad)` on `.ds-card`), and regions now separate via `gap: var(--cd-gap)` on the card's flex column. Industry alignment: shadcn Card, Vercel Geist Card, Linear, Notion all ship dividerless; the bordered-cells pattern is a 2018-era Material-Bootstrap stack that modern systems abandoned for cleaner single-surface read.

**Gap-to-padding ratio rev** (gap < pad → gap = pad): an earlier Card rev ran at `space-s` (8) gap with `space-m` (12) padding (3:2 ratio borrowed from Dialog) on the rationale that gap < pad establishes a hierarchy step between "outer chrome" and "internal separation." The current Figma 295-672 rev ships 12/12 (1:1) after seeing that the rationale that produced 3:2 for Dialog doesn't transfer cleanly to Card's smaller squarer geometry. At Card's 264×264 scale with 4 regions visible at once, a 3:2 ratio creates compositional crowding because regions are thin enough that an extra-tight gap reads as "regions glued together." Dialog at 440 wide with 3 zones keeps the 12/8 (3:2) step because Dialog's wider container and explicit header typography hierarchy benefit from a step between zones. **Both 1:1 and 3:2 are sanctioned ratios; the choice is per-surface, not a universal policy.**

**Three fill-style variants → three tones** (May 2026): an earlier Card rev had three fill-style variants (default / elevated / filled = `surface/default` + `surface/raised` + `shadow-1` + `surface/subtle`). That axis answered "how lifted is this card?" but conflated two unrelated decisions: surface-tier (raised vs subtle vs default) and shadow (elevated vs flat). The new tone axis answers "how much brand emphasis does this card carry?" — a strictly more useful axis because (a) consumers want different brand emphasis levels for the same component (settings-row card vs feature-promo card vs hero CTA), (b) the three reserved brand-surface tokens (`surface/brand`, `surface/brand-subtle`, plus `text/on-brand` / `text/brand` companions in §3.1) were already in the system waiting for a consumer, and (c) consumers wanting a "small lift" can compose Card with `box-shadow: var(--shadow-1)` directly via the surrounding container — the lift was a layout concern, not a Card concern.

**Tertiary description uses `color-mix()` — sanctioned exception**: on the saturated brand-500 surface, both title and description default to `text/on-brand` (white). Same color = same emphasis = flat hierarchy. Per §5.1.1 "hierarchy comes from size + color + surface, never weight," and on this single-surface, single-color foreground the only available carrier is *opacity* of the description. `color-mix(in oklch, text/on-brand 80%, transparent)` produces a 20%-translucent white description that reads as "secondary" against the saturated bg without forcing a new `text/on-brand-muted` token (which would be a 1-component drift per §2.2.1). This is exactly the sanctioned use case for `color-mix()`: perceptual blend, no semantic token captures it. shadcn / Tailwind do the equivalent with `text-primary-foreground/80`; Apple HIG promotional surfaces use white at ~70-80% for secondary text on tinted backgrounds; Material 3 derates onPrimary similarly.

**Tones demo decisions** (why no footer + why no media in the tertiary card):
- **No footer in tertiary**: the standard footer convention is `soft-neutral S` + `solid-brand S`. On a saturated brand-500 surface, `solid-brand` is the same blue as the bg — the button **disappears into the surface**. No clean fix without inventing a "white-pill on brand" button variant (1-component drift). The variants demo omits the footer for the tertiary card and documents that consumers needing CTAs on brand surfaces should use `solid-neutral` (filled black) or rethink whether the surface should be tertiary at all. Industry alignment: short content + single decisive CTA is the wild pattern for hero-brand surfaces (Stripe, Linear, Apple HIG, Vercel marketing).
- **No media in tones demo**: the placeholder media slot uses `surface/subtle` as its empty-state fill — that reads cleanly inside primary (white) or secondary (subtle-tinted) but against the saturated tertiary brand surface reads as "missing image" rather than "image goes here." The tones demo is showing the *surface* axis; layering a clashing visual would muddy the read. Consumers needing media inside a tertiary card pass an actual `<img>`.

