| Rev | Inner Button tier | Outer pad | Outer radius | Why dropped |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Rev 1 | XS (24) | `space-m` (12) | `radius-l` (20) | XS buttons read smaller than the 28-tall Select that sat next to them — 4px cluster-height mismatch, the cluster felt visually inconsistent. |
| Rev 2 | **S (28)** | `space-m` (12) | `radius-l` (20) | Resolved the Select-vs-Button mismatch (both at 28). User feedback: buttons "look small" overall, padding could be tightened, the 2-row default textarea floor felt too tall. |
| **Rev 3 (current default M)** | **M (32)** | **`space-xs` (6)** | **`radius-m` (16)** | Matches Cursor-compact density; surface family moves from Dialog/Card to Toast/Banner per the inverse-rule cascade. The earlier rationale "Composer stays in the Dialog/Card family because outer radius shouldn't change" was relitigated against the §2.1.2 inverse rule (outer is DERIVED from inner + pad, not chosen). |
| **Rev 3b (current L size)** | L (40) | `space-s` (8) | `radius-l` (20) | Claude-homepage-density Composer. Surface family returns to Dialog/Card. Soft-regime concentric corners (spread 6, matching Dialog precedent). |
| **Rev 4 (text-padding ramp, mid-May 2026)** | unchanged across all three tiers | S bumps from `space-2xs` (4) to `space-xs` (6) | unchanged across all three tiers | Earlier `--cmp-text-px` ramp (S: `space-xs` 6, M: `space-s` 8, L: `space-m` 12) produced visible H/V ratios of 1.19 / 1.35 / 1.61. User feedback at L: "text padding is too much horizontal and not enough vertical" — the L tier overshot the canonical case-B band. Rebalanced to S/M/L = 4/6/8, ratios 0.96 / 1.15 / 1.29 — all inside the §6.1.1 band. S `--cmp-pad` also bumped from 4 to 6 per the same feedback. |
| **Rev 5 (text-padding, mid-late May 2026)** | unchanged | unchanged | unchanged | Earlier user feedback: "the banner and composer both have too much additional horizontal padding applied to them. if you are going to offset the padding, can you use a smaller px amount?" — Rev 4's text-px-equals-pad pattern at M and L produced visible H breathing (12 / 16) that read as making the textarea row visibly inset from the action-row buttons' corner positions. Rev 5 unified `--cmp-text-px` to `space-2xs` (4) across all three tiers. The textarea wrapper's role became strictly "subtle inset from surface chrome." |
| **Rev 6 (current, late May 2026)** | unchanged | unchanged | unchanged | Later user feedback: "the text is too close to the edge of the composer." Rev 5's uniform 4px subtle offset under-breathed the textarea at M and L — at the larger tiers, the absolute padding values around the text (10H raw at M, 12H raw at L) felt cramped against the action-row buttons which sit further inward via their own internal centering. Rev 6 restores the pad-matching pattern at M and L (text-px = pad: M=6, L=8) while keeping S at the minimum 4 because S's compact identity wants the tightest text inset that still reads as breathing. Visible H 10 / 12 / 16 across S / M / L, ratios 0.96 / 1.15 / 1.29 — all inside the §6.1.1 sanctioned band. The "text aligns with action-row button corner inset" read is acceptable here because the user's direct feedback prioritized text breathing over visual-edge-alignment in the action row. |

**Lesson the rev history encodes**: surface radius is NEVER picked by surface-family taste — it's DERIVED from the inner Button tier and the surface padding via §2.1.2. Re-tiering the inner Button (a §1.4.3 customization lever per §1.4) reflows the outer chrome through the cascade; the system math doesn't fight the consumer's tier choice.

### B.4 §9.16 Pagination — five dropped active-state revisions

| Rev | Active | Rest | Why dropped |
|---|---|---|---|
| Rev 1 | `solid-neutral` (filled black) | `ghost-neutral` (bare text) | Hierarchy too steep + brand color absent. |
| Rev 2 | `outline-brand` | `outline-neutral` | Non-canonical brand-as-stroke usage; user pushed back after May 2026 selected-state audit. |
| Rev 3 | `soft-brand` with default chrome | `outline-neutral` with default chrome | **INVERTED hierarchy** — `neutral/200` rest hairline read more prominently than `blue/100`-on-`blue/50` active chrome. |
| Rev 4 | `solid-brand` (saturated) | `ghost-neutral` (bare text) | Decisive selected but bare-text rest felt unbalanced — "row felt empty at rest, chip felt shouted." |
| Rev 5 | `soft-brand` (1-stop contrast) | `ghost-neutral` (bare text) | Not inverted but too-quiet selected indicator (1-stop contrast chip needs adjacent contained chrome to read as selected). |
| **Rev 6 (current default)** | **`soft-brand` with `--btn-br` overridden to `selection/border` (blue/300, ~3-stop contrast)** | **`outline-neutral` with `--btn-br` overridden to `border/subtle` (neutral/150, lighter rest stroke)** | Outlined-style chrome on every page with calibrated stroke contrast — soft hairline rest + prominent brand stroke selected. Honors the "rest lighter, selected darker" feedback while keeping every page visibly interactive. |

**Lesson**: when the design rule is "chrome on every page, hierarchy from rest-vs-selected stroke contrast," picking the active/rest pair is an integrated decision — the active's stroke calibration depends on the rest's stroke and vice versa. Granular `arrowVariant` / `activeVariant` / `inactiveVariant` props exist as escape hatches but the named presets (default + filled) prevent accidentally producing intermediate combinations that invert hierarchy.

**Cross-system alignment (researched references)**:

| System | Active treatment | Inactive treatment | Prev/Next |
|---|---|---|---|
| **shadcn Pagination** | `outline` button (outlined, with stronger border) | `ghost` button | Icon + text label by default |
| **GitHub Primer Pagination** | `default` (neutral filled) | Bare link | Icon-only |
| **Vercel Geist Pagination** | Filled black (`solid-neutral`) | Bare text | Icon-only chevrons |
| **Atlassian Pagination** | Filled brand (`solid-brand`) | Bare text | Icon-only |
| **Material 3 Pagination** | Filled brand-tonal surface | Text button | Icon-only |
| **Linear** | Filled neutral with brand text | Ghost | Icon-only |
| **Tailwind UI Pagination** | Saturated `solid-brand` | Bare text | Icon-only chevrons |
| **Ours (default preset)** | **`soft-brand` chip with `--btn-br` overridden to `selection/border` (blue/300 light, blue/700 dark — darker stroke for "you are here") on `blue/50` fill** | **`outline-neutral` with `--btn-br` overridden to `border/subtle` (neutral/150 — lighter rest stroke) on transparent fill** | **Icon-only `outline-neutral` matching rest stroke override** |
| **Ours (filled preset)** | `solid-brand` (saturated brand-500 fill + white text + standard luminous-edge rim) | `soft-neutral` (`neutral/50` fill + `neutral/100` hairline + neutral text) | Icon-only `soft-neutral` |

The default preset doesn't fit cleanly in the industry table because no major system pairs an outlined-rest with an outlined-active distinguished only by stroke color. shadcn `Pagination`'s "active = `outline` variant + ghost inactive" is the closest analog (both use the outline shape language) but shadcn keeps rest as ghost; we promote rest to outline so the row carries strokes everywhere. The result is a hybrid: shadcn's chrome-balanced active/rest pairing (both outlined) plus a calibrated stroke-color contrast (rest = `border/subtle`, active = `selection/border`) that the Material 3 / Atlassian / Tailwind UI brand-tonal-or-saturated patterns use for fills — applied to strokes instead. The filled preset lands more squarely in the **Tailwind UI / Atlassian saturated-brand lane** for consumers who want the row to feel like filled chips. Consumers wanting still other emphasis can swap `activeVariant` to `solid-neutral` (Vercel/Linear filled-black) — the granular props are escape hatches, not the system stance.

### B.10 §9.28 Composer — text-padding rev history

The Composer's `--cmp-text-px` arrived at its **pad-matching at M/L, minimum at S** pattern via three dropped revs:

1. **Rev A — `--cmp-text-px` one ramp step ABOVE `--cmp-pad` at every tier** (S: 4/6, M: 6/8, L: 8/12). Produced H/V ratios of 1.19 → 1.35 → 1.61. User testing of the L tier revealed the framing was wrong — at L the raw H breathing (20) was 8px more than the raw V (12.4), which visibly read as "text floating in a wide pillow."
2. **Rev B — `--cmp-text-px` = `--cmp-pad` at every tier** (S:4, M:6, L:8). Ratios 0.96 / 1.15 / 1.29 — satisfied case-B comfortably but the text-row at M and L was visibly inset from the action-row buttons' corner positions. User feedback: "too much additional horizontal padding."
3. **Rev C — uniform `space-2xs` (4) at every tier**. Visible H/V lands at 10/10.4 → 10/10.4 → 12/12.4 across S/M/L. Sits at the "visibly uniform" end of case-B's sanctioned band. The subtle 1-step inset kept the text-row aligned with the action-row buttons but the under-breathing was visible at M and L. User feedback: "the text is too close to the edge of the composer."
4. **Current — S = `space-2xs` (4), M = `space-xs` (6), L = `space-s` (8)** — pad-matching at M and L, minimum at S. Visible H 10 / 12 / 16, ratios 0.96 / 1.15 / 1.29 — all inside §6.1.1. Returns to Rev B's pattern at M and L (the user's most-recent feedback prioritized text breathing over visual-edge-alignment in the action row) while keeping S at minimum 4 because S's compact identity wants the tightest text inset that still reads as breathing.

**Lesson the rev history encodes**: text-padding inside a composer-class surface has two competing constraints — case-B asymmetry (visible H ≥ visible V) AND visual alignment with adjacent action-row buttons' corner inset. There's no single rule that satisfies both at every tier. The current state prioritizes case-B comfort at M and L (where the absolute pad values are large enough that the action-row-alignment cost is small) and prioritizes alignment at S (where the absolute pad values are small enough that any additional text-px would visibly over-breathe). Future composer-class surfaces should follow the same per-tier judgment, not adopt a single uniform rule.

### B.9 Color Picker — handle-overhang bug + dropped revs

**The handle-overhang bug was a POSITIONING problem, not a sizing problem** (this distinction matters because three earlier revs misdiagnosed it as sizing and "fixed" it by shrinking the handle, which both looked anemic AND didn't actually solve the underlying off-by-half issue). The CSS pattern `position:absolute; left:<pct>%; transform: translate(-50%,-50%)` positions the handle's CENTER at `pct%` of the track. At `pct=0` that puts the center at the track's left edge, so half the handle's body sits outside the track to the left. At `pct=100` half the handle sits outside to the right. Shrinking the handle from 20 → 16 → 12 reduced the absolute overhang but never eliminated it — the handle's left edge always extended past the track's left edge by half-handle's width.

**The real fix is to constrain the handle's center to the inner range** `[handle_size / 2, track_width − handle_size / 2]` so the handle's body stays inside the track at every position. CSS implementation uses `--pct` (0–100) set inline + `left: calc(var(--pct) * 1%)` + `margin-left: calc(-1 * var(--pct) * var(--cp-slider-handle) / 100)`. The math:

```
pos_x  =  (pct / 100) × track_width  −  (pct / 100) × handle_size
       =  (pct / 100) × (track_width − handle_size)
```

which sweeps the handle's left edge from `0` at `pct=0` to `(track_width − handle_size)` at `pct=100` — exactly the range where the handle's body fits flush inside the track at both endpoints. Vertical centering stays at `top: 50%; transform: translateY(-50%)` since the cross-axis isn't constrained by the same problem. The canvas handle uses the same model with `--sx` / `--sy` and additionally `margin-top: calc(var(--sy) * (canvas_h − handle) / 100)` so the canvas handle stays inside the 2D area at all four corners.

**Why this matters as a system rule for any future slider primitive** (Slider, Range, future audio scrubber, future timeline): the `translate(-50%, -50%)` pattern is the wrong model for any "thumb on a track" interaction because the thumb is a physical object that the user perceives as needing to sit ON the track, not centered ON the endpoint of an abstract scale. Use the `--pct` + `margin-left` constraint pattern for all future track-based handles.

**Dropped Color Picker revs**:
- **Three-segment InputGroup footer rev** (May 2026, reverted): joined mode + hex + alpha into one wide InputGroup. The visual read was "one very wide field" rather than "a mode choice modifying a color value," so it was reverted to the current two-piece composition (separate Select + InputGroup) per §9.27.1. Industry alignment: Figma, Adobe Color, Sketch all render the mode dropdown beside (not joined into) the value field.
- **Canvas radius revs**: an earlier pairing (`space-l` 16 padding + `radius-s` 12 canvas) predated the §2.1.2 audit and satisfied NEITHER the strict-formula ideal (`20 − 16 = 4`) NOR a clean soft-regime value — 12 was picked by eye, not by math, and worked acceptably because the canvas is large enough that the corners don't dominate. A still-earlier rev sat at `radius-2xs` (4) on strict-rule grounds; user feedback ("the corner radius of the color area itself seems quite sharp") confirmed the strict answer read too rectangular on a 280-wide canvas. The current pairing (`space-m` 12 padding + `radius-xs` 8 canvas) avoids both failure modes by tightening padding to 12, which lets the math land cleanly per §2.1.2 soft-regime ideal.
- **Hex field swatch + `#` rev** (corrected May 2026): an earlier rev shipped the hex Input with `colorSwatch={color}` and `value={color}` (including the `#` prefix) on the rationale "match the Color Input trigger chrome exactly." User feedback after side-by-side comparison with the target reference: the picker's canvas above the footer already shows the picked color far more clearly than a 16px swatch can, and the `#` is redundant chrome since the Mode select directly to the left already says "Hex". Removing both opens up the hex segment for the value text and matches Figma / Adobe Color / Sketch / VS Code convention.

### B.8 §9.21 Bottom Nav — IA fold and active-state revs

**IA fold (May 2026 architectural change)**: Apple HIG, Material 3, and every major mobile-shipping system treat `TabBar` / `NavigationBar` as part of the same conceptual family as their desktop nav primitives — they're all "navigation" in the IA, even when they ship as separate code primitives. The earlier rev had Bottom Nav as a parallel top-level component (parallel to Navigation); audit showed this caused real consumer confusion ("which is Navigation? what's a Bottom Nav?"). Folding into Navigation > Bottom places it in the right mental bucket while preserving the implementation distinction (a distinct CSS primitive `.ds-bottom-nav` because the layout — stacked icon-above-label, single canonical size, edge-anchored chrome — is meaningfully different from the inline `.ds-nav-item` used by Vertical and Horizontal placements).

**Active-state rev (May 2026 — backplate-driven, not typography-only)**: an earlier rev (May 2026, immediately prior) used typography-only active (color shift `text/tertiary → text/primary` + weight 500 bump) on the rationale that mobile bottom nav active state is a "near-universal industry convention" pairing color with weight. Audit found this conflicted with the rest of the Navigation system: Vertical and Horizontal Nav both use bg + inset ring as the canonical active treatment per §9.3, and Bottom Nav's typography-only treatment created an asymmetry where one Nav placement carried state via typography and the other two via chrome. Material 3 NavigationBar uses an oval pill backplate around active items (very close to what we now do); Linear and Vercel mobile both use a tonal backplate. Apple iOS Tab Bar's typography-only convention is platform-specific (iOS pairs color with a Filled SF Symbol variant our §5.3.1 one-weight rule forbids us from doing) — without the Fill swap, color alone produces a subtler active state than users expect, which is why iOS pairs it with weight. The fix is to drop the iOS-specific convention and use the system's own canonical Nav active treatment instead. This unifies the Navigation language and removes the §5.1.1 weight-500 exception — the system is now uniformly weight 400 across every component.

**Why weight 400 across all states (no §5.1.1 exception)**: with the backplate carrying the active signal (bg + inset ring + color shift), there's no longer a typography-level signal needed to compensate for our no-Filled-icon-variant constraint. The previous "weight 500 here is a sanctioned exception" rationale no longer applies; per §5.1.1 the system defaults to 400 everywhere unless the three-part necessity test passes, and Bottom Nav's active state passes none of the three because the bg + ring chrome carry what color alone could not.

**Why the icon stays at Phosphor Regular weight (no Fill/Bold variant for active)**: §5.3.1 standing rule "one weight system-wide, no exception." Phosphor ships Regular, Bold, and Fill variants and many mobile systems swap to Fill for active icons (iOS most notably), but adopting variant icons would require the system to maintain two icon stacks per glyph and breaks the rule that's been a hard invariant since the system shipped. The active icon's perceived darkness comes from the foreground-color shift (`text/secondary → text/primary`) — same mechanism that darkens the label. This trades a subtler active icon than iOS in exchange for system consistency; tradeoff is documented as accepted.

### B.7 §9.8 Toast — icon-wrapper and action-tier revisions

**Icon-wrapper history**: an earlier rev kept the wrapper square (`--tst-icon × --tst-icon`) on the rationale that "corner-symmetric 8/8 inset to the wrapper" mattered more than glyph-to-text alignment. Multi-line testing proved that wrong (icon visibly stuck up above title cap). A second rev claimed "glyph at 8/10 in multi-line is the right answer" — but only because that rev had ALSO wrongly overridden `padding-left` to 8 (single-line case became 8/12 around glyph, also wrong). The current single-line answer (12/12 ✓ uniform around glyph, no padding-left override) and multi-line answer (10/12 around glyph top-left, 2px asymmetry accepted to preserve cap-alignment) follow the corrected §6.1.2 formula and match Apple HIG, M3 snackbar, Radix, shadcn, Sonner, Atlassian Banner, and Primer Flash.

**Action layout framing**: a previous rev framed inline vs footer as "transient peer responses" vs "deliberate decisions." That framing overstated a purpose distinction that's actually a *consequence* of content density, not a cause. The honest framing follows industry: the patterns are different content-density layouts of the same stacked-corner notification.

**Inline single-button styling**: an earlier rev made the single inline button a peer-only style (no primary fill) on the rationale that "primary fill at XS is too punchy." User testing of side-by-side `["Button","Button"]` demos showed the opposite: two identical buttons next to each other forces unnecessary cognitive load ("which do I pick?") and breaks parity with Dialog footer. Industry alignment: Sonner — the most influential modern toast library — has its `action` slot styled as a filled/branded CTA and `cancel` as ghost, the same primary+secondary convention.

**Inline action tier**: an earlier iteration sized the single inline action at S (32×32) on the worry that XS-with-label would invert visual hierarchy. That worry is correct *standalone* but wrong *contained*: inside the toast, the surrounding chrome carries the affordance hierarchy and the small chip reads as inset/secondary, exactly as intended. The S iteration also forced toast min-h up to 48 to fit the action; XS keeps the toast at 40 whether 0, 1, or 2 inline actions are present, matching every modern toast's compact-mode height.

**Single-line title styling**: an earlier rev relied on weight 500 to differentiate title from description in single-line cases. That rev was reverted when the §5.1.1 weight rule landed (components default to weight 400; hierarchy comes from size + color + surface). Authors of new toast helpers should prefer `title` over `text`; `text` exists only as a back-compat alias and produces identical output.

**Internal-padding architecture (Rev C, late May 2026)**: Toast's earlier rev had `.ds-toast` with `padding: 8 12` on the surface itself + a `:has(.ds-toast__actions) { padding-right: 8 }` override that collapsed the right edge to corner-symmetric inset around the trailing close button. The override worked geometrically (close had 8V/8H ✓, body kept 12H breathing on the left) but expressed the asymmetry through a CSS `:has()` selector rather than through the structure. Rev C refactored to cluster-based padding matching Banner Rev D: Toast surface had `padding: 0`, an internal `.ds-toast__content` cluster wrapped the icon + body and carried 8V/12H padding, a sibling `.ds-toast__actions` cluster wrapped the inline action(s) + close button and carried 8V/8H corner-symmetric padding. For the `--footer` variant, the same two clusters composed via CSS grid (`grid-template-areas: "content actions" "footer footer"`) and a third `.ds-toast__footer` cluster spanned row 2.

**Internal-padding architecture (Rev D, current — matches Banner Rev E §B.11 and Figma node 278:1813)**: Toast's structure is `.ds-toast` (surface with its own padding, flex-column with gap) containing `.ds-toast__row` (the main flex row) and optionally `.ds-toast__footer` (sibling row below). Inside the row, three sibling elements each own their own padding: surface (`--tst-pad: 8`) which anchors the close-button corner inset via §6.1.2; icon wrapper (`--tst-icon-pad: 2` equal padding around the 16-icon); body wrapper (`--tst-body-py: 2`, `--tst-body-px: 4` — minimal text-offset per §6.1.5, one ramp step H above V to absorb the line-box leading delta). The buttons cluster (`.ds-toast__buttons`) carries no padding — it sits as a direct flex child alongside icon and body. **Why Rev D and not Rev C**: user feedback after Rev C ("look at primer's structure again. there is padding being applied to the component overall. the button in the top right corner uses this padding. then there is a separate div around the icon svg which has its own equal padding. then there is another div around text which has its own padding"). Rev C's cluster-based approach hid the "the button is anchored to the surface corner via the surface's own padding" relationship behind two named clusters that each had to invent their own padding values duplicating that intent. Rev D makes the corner-inset derivation explicit: surface padding alone defines where the close button sits relative to toast corners, and text/icon get their own internal padding without competing with the button's anchor relationship. Industry alignment: matches Primer Flash, Material 3 Snackbar, and the Figma component exactly. For the `--footer` variant, the surface switches to `flex-direction: column` with `--tst-section-gap: 8` between the main row and the footer row — no CSS Grid, no per-area padding overrides; the footer carries `--tst-footer-gap: 6` between its buttons but no padding of its own (the surface's 8px padding handles the footer's outer inset on all four sides). The Rev C cluster-based approach and the earlier `:has()`-on-surface rev are both dropped; the current three-padded-slots rev is the canonical pattern for any future notification-class surface where text, icons, and buttons share a row with different padding needs.

### B.5 §9.21 Bottom Nav — geometry rev history

Active-state rule and geometry rule are coupled: changing one forces the other:
- Rev 1: 48-tall container + 20-icon + 2-gap + filled-pill active → 3px breathing each side (read tight)
- Rev 2: 56-tall container + 24-icon + 4-gap + filled-pill active → 4px breathing each side (read tight WITH the filled pill — the chromatic active block magnified the perceived tightness against its own edges)
- Rev 3: 64-tall container + 24-icon + 4-gap + filled-pill active → 8px breathing each side (comfortable internally, but the filled active pill still read as visually heavy at the bar level, dominating content)
- Rev 4: 64-tall container + 24-icon + 4-gap + typography-only active → 8px breathing, but oversized in product/docs contexts (read as desktop chrome, not mobile nav) — typography-only active doesn't *need* 8px of internal breathing the way a filled pill did
- Rev 5: 56-tall container + 24-icon + 4-gap + typography-only active → only 4px breathing (the icon size was carried over from when the bar was 64-tall and didn't get re-validated against the smaller 48-item area)
- Rev 6: 56-tall container + 20-icon (icon-m) + 4-gap + typography-only active + floating-pill chrome (`surface/subtle` track, uniform border, full-pill radius) → 6px breathing each side; the icon-vs-bar geometry landed at canonical proportions but the chrome read as a "contained widget" rather than the canonical edge-anchored mobile nav
- Rev 7: 56-tall full-width container with white bg + 1px top hairline only + 20-icon + 4-gap + typography-only active → 10px breathing each side. The chrome read unmistakably as "mobile bottom nav" but the typography-only active state created a Navigation-system asymmetry: Nav vertical and Nav horizontal both used bg + inset ring active treatment, and Bottom Nav was the only placement using typography. The §5.1.1 weight-500 exception was scoped to this single component.
- **Current (May 2026)**: 64-tall full-width container (with 4px outer padding → 56-tall inner) + 20-icon + 4-gap + horizontal-nav-style backplate active (`action/hover` bg + inset 1px `border/default` ring per §9.3) + per-item radius-l (20). Reads as "stacked version of horizontal nav" — every Navigation primitive (Vertical, Horizontal, Bottom) now shares the same per-item backplate idiom, with only layout direction and per-item content arrangement differing. Removes the §5.1.1 weight-500 exception and unifies the Navigation grammar.

**Lesson**: geometry is a system, not a stack of independent tokens. Container height, icon size, gap, label tier, AND active-state chrome all participate in producing the visual identity. When an active-state rule changes, the *whole geometry* has to be re-validated. Rev 5 was the worked example of geometry drift (icon stayed at 24 after the bar shrank from 64 to 56); the current rev is the worked example of grammar drift correction (active state aligned with the rest of the Navigation system, even though it required regrowing the bar by 8px to fit the per-item backplates).

### B.6 §9.29 Table — earlier padding revisions

Pre-promotion shipped three padding pairs across S/M/L plus 16/16 for docs. After the promotion, first unification was 8V/12H case-B (matching Polaris IndexTable). User feedback on that rev: "padding should be V=H, neutral/100 too dark." The current 12/12 + `surface/quiet` header addresses both directives in one pass.

**Industry alignment for the 12/12 symmetric**: Material 3 Data Table runs 16/16 symmetric (same hard-edged-sub-element rationale — cells routinely contain Badges, Avatars, Buttons where the §6.1.2 priority rule says hard-edged controls win and corner-symmetric inset matters more than text H>V breathing); Polaris IndexTable runs 12V/16H asymmetric; shadcn defaults 16/16. We sit at the symmetric-and-tight end of the band.

**Section radius rev**: pre-May 2026 product Table was at `radius-l` (20) derived per §2.1.2 inverse rule, docs at `radius-xl` (24). Both dropped to `radius-m` (16) per the May 2026 unification — Table's outer corners are rarely corner-adjacent to nested rounded elements (cells are square + inner controls have their own 12px `--tbl-px` margin ≫ ½ × outer-radius 8 = soft regime, so the inverse rule doesn't force a specific outer value). Surface-family taste wins and the system-wide stance is "match the docs-frame tier."

### B.12 §9.X Segmented Control — track tint remap (May 2026)

The track was previously `surface/subtle` (`neutral/100` light) which carried ΔL 0.050 against the active pill (white) — a clear track-vs-active step, but the track itself read visibly tinted against `surface/page` (`neutral/50`), which the user flagged as too heavy. Lifting to `surface/quiet` (= `neutral/50` light / `neutral/1000` dark, ΔL 0.020 vs the white active pill) drops the track to L=0.980. The active pill keeps its `surface/default` fill + `var(--shadow-1)` lift + 1px inset `border/subtle` ring, and the shadow + ring carry the active-state signal that the chromatic ΔL no longer fully carries on its own.

**Industry alignment with the lighter track**: iOS UISegmentedControl in light mode uses `secondarySystemFill` (≈ `alpha/black/4` overlay on white, L≈0.960 — closer to our `surface/quiet` than `surface/subtle`), and the iOS active pill relies on the same shadow + border combination for state. Material 3 Segmented Button and shadcn ToggleGroup sit closer to the heavier-track end of the band; we land deliberately on the lighter side because the system's overall stance (per Standing Rule #1.1) is "quiet at rest, responsive on interaction" — a quieter track at rest is the right read, and the active pill's shadow + border provide responsive interaction the moment a state changes.

### B.14 §9.3 Dropdown panel — separator iteration history

The current "edge-to-edge full-width via negative horizontal margin" rule arrived via three iterations:

1. **Iteration 1 — "match item padding" inset**: separator's horizontal margin matched the item's leading inset so the line started under the first label. Didn't align with anything in practice — items with leading icons had a different x-offset than items without, group labels had a third offset, sub-menu rows a fourth. No single value satisfied all row variants.
2. **Iteration 2 — `margin: 0`**: separator stretched to the panel's content area but stopped 4px shy of the inner border edge (because the panel has `padding: var(--mnu-pad, 4px)` on every side, and `margin: 0` doesn't escape that padding). Visibly inset, read as "weak attempt at structural break."
3. **Iteration 3 (current) — negative horizontal margin equal to panel padding**: separator escapes the panel padding via `margin: 0 calc(-1 * var(--mnu-pad))` and reaches the inner border edge. Produces a hard structural break between row groups that matches the semantic of "section ends, new section begins."

**Industry alignment**: shadcn / Radix `DropdownMenu.Separator` ships `-mx-1` to negate the panel's `p-1` — same mechanism. Linear, Material 3 menu divider, Apple HIG menus all render edge-to-edge.

