### B.11 §9.10 Banner — padding rev history and leading-icon-as-content rationale

**Padding ramp rev** (May 2026):
- **Rev A — 12V / 16H** (`--bn-px: space-l` 16, `--bn-py: space-m` 12). Ratio 1.33, matching the canonical Card / inline-notice band. With the trailing close collapsing to `--bn-py` (12) per §6.1.2, the corner-anchored close had 12V/12H ✓ but the offset to the text cluster's 16H was a full 4px — visibly read as "too much extra horizontal padding around text." User feedback: "the banner has too much additional horizontal padding."
- **Rev B — 12V / 14H** (`--bn-px: space-2m` 14, `--bn-py: space-m` 12). Ratio 1.17, a subtler asymmetry. The May 2026 `--space-2m` (14) ramp addition made this granularity available. Close-button offset dropped from 4px to 2px, eliminating the "too much padding" perception while preserving subtle text-breathing.
- **Rev C (late May 2026) — 8V / 12H** (`--bn-px: space-m` 12, `--bn-py: space-s` 8). Compressed outer chrome to match Toast exactly, on user direction: "the overall padding for the whole banner, dialog, toast, and other container components can be smaller. then the corner radius would also have to adjust as well." Ratio still 1.5 (case-B comfortable), but the absolute padding values are smaller — close button now sits with 8V/8H corner-symmetric inset (down from 12V/12H), and the text cluster sits with 12H breathing (down from 14H). The visible text-vs-close offset is 4px (12H − 8H), matching Toast's text-vs-close offset of 4px exactly. Banner and Toast are in the same outer-chrome family with identical close-button inset patterns; the only differences are width (Banner full-width vs Toast 280–360), persistence (Banner persistent vs Toast auto-dismiss), and shadow (Banner flat vs Toast `--shadow-3`). Implementation used `:has(.ds-banner__actions)` on the surface to collapse `padding-right` from 12 to 8.
- **Rev D (late May 2026) — cluster-based padding: surface 0, content cluster 8V/16H, actions cluster 8V/8H** (`--bn-content-py: space-s` 8, `--bn-content-px: space-l` 16, `--bn-actions-py: space-s` 8, `--bn-actions-px: space-s` 8). User feedback after Rev C: "can you not create a div around the icon + text area only, and apply specific custom padding there? see how primer does this with banners and dialogs. they have specific padding for icons and text." Rev D refactored the structure: Banner's HTML became two sibling clusters (`.ds-banner__content` and `.ds-banner__actions`) each owning its own padding tokens; the Banner surface itself had `padding: 0`. The content cluster got bumped from 12H to 16H to give text "slightly more horizontal breathing" per the user request. The actions cluster kept 8V/8H corner-symmetric for the close button.
- **Rev E (current, May 2026) — three padded slots in a row: surface 8, icon-wrap 2, body 2V/4H, buttons cluster no padding** (`--bn-pad: space-s` 8, `--bn-icon-pad: space-3xs` 2, `--bn-body-py: space-3xs` 2, `--bn-body-px: space-2xs` 4, `--bn-buttons-gap: space-2xs` 4). User feedback after Rev D, with the Figma node 278:1813 reference: "look at primer's structure again. there is padding being applied to the component overall. the button in the top right corner uses this padding. then there is a separate div around the icon svg which has its own equal padding. then there is another div around text which has its own padding. that padding will be slightly offset horizontally. look at the way this figma component is structured and copy the div structure." The Rev D cluster-based approach hid the "the close button is anchored to the surface corner via the surface's own padding" relationship behind two named clusters that each had to invent their own padding values duplicating that intent. Rev E makes the corner-inset derivation explicit: `.ds-banner` carries its own `--bn-pad` (8) and the close button as a direct flex child picks up its corner inset from that padding alone (no separate `__actions` cluster needed); icon and text each get their own per-wrapper internal padding — `__icon` for equal letterbox around the icon, `__body` for case-B-asymmetric breathing around the text — without competing with the close button's anchor relationship. Industry alignment: matches Primer's Banner component exactly (the dismiss button is a direct child of the banner row that picks up the surface's overall padding for its corner inset; the icon and text live in their own padded wrappers).

**Leading icon as content, not corner-anchor chrome** — why per-wrapper internal padding is the right model in Rev E. Banner's leading icon visually *belongs* with the text cluster: icon + title + description all read as one left-aligned content group, where the icon serves as a tone marker for the prose. The icon does NOT function as a corner affordance (close button) that the eye reads relative to the surface corner; it functions as the head of a content group that the eye reads relative to the text it labels. Rev E preserves this rationale through per-wrapper padding: the icon's wrapper (`__icon`, equal padding of 2 on all sides) gives the icon a small letterbox of breathing room that doesn't compete with the surface's corner anchor, and the body wrapper (`__body`, slight H offset) gives the text its case-B asymmetric breathing without forcing the icon to participate in that asymmetry. Industry alignment: Primer Banner / Material 3 Banner / Atlassian SectionMessage all ship per-element internal padding for the same reason.

**System-wide pattern after Rev E.** Notification-class containers now use the **three-padded-slots** pattern when the surface has text + icon + buttons that each need different padding intents: Banner (surface 8, icon-wrap 2, body 2V/6H, buttons as direct children) and Toast (same architecture, see §B.7 Rev D). Composer (§9.28) keeps its symmetric per-tier outer padding because its inner-button cluster is on the row below the textarea (vertical separation) rather than to the right of the text (horizontal asymmetry); the text-vs-buttons axis doesn't force a per-wrapper split there. Dialog stays at uniform `space-m` (12) on the surface because Dialog's close is absolutely positioned at the corner (its inset comes from `top` / `right`, not from flex padding) and the body/footer flow shares the surface padding cleanly. The pattern: when a surface has text + icon + buttons each needing different padding intents, use **per-wrapper internal padding** (each inner element owns its own breathing) plus **surface padding** (anchors button corner inset); when the asymmetry can be expressed by absolute positioning (Dialog close) or by row separation (Composer), keep padding on the surface.

### B.15 §9.6 Dialog — close button size revision (late May 2026)

**Rev A — XS close** (`Button XS icon-only ghost-neutral`, 24×24, 12px X glyph). Originally chosen because Toast's close is XS and the "close as Button XS" pattern was meant to be system-wide. Issue: at Dialog's primary-surface scale (440 wide × variable height), the 24-tall close button read as visually undersized against the body content (14/20 paragraphs, 16/24 title, S-tier footer buttons). The close was the smallest interactive element on the surface by a meaningful margin.

**Rev B (current) — S close** (`Button S icon-only ghost-neutral`, 28×28, 14px X glyph at the May 2026 `icon-2s` tier). Promoted to S to match Banner's close (also S). Dialog and Banner are both in the "primary / persistent container" family; both benefit from a more substantial close affordance than Toast's compact XS. Toast keeps XS because Toast's 40-tall floor needs the close to fit inside without inflating the surface — the close-tier-by-context rule is now: **Toast = XS, Banner = S, Dialog = S**.

**Lesson**: "close button at one tier system-wide" is the wrong rule. The right rule is "close button at the tier that fits the surface" — the surface's content scale dictates which close size reads as proportional. Toast's compact identity wants XS; Banner's full-width-but-thin identity wants S; Dialog's primary-surface identity wants S. Future container components should pick their close-tier from the same per-context judgment, not adopt a uniform rule.

### B.16 §9.28 Composer — `.ds-composer__field` padding revs (May 14, 2026)

**Rev sequence**:

**Rev 1 (pre-existing)**. `__field` shipped with `padding: 0 var(--cmp-text-px)` where `--cmp-text-px = space-xs (6)` at M. V padding was 0; horizontal padding was `--cmp-text-px`. In `--double` mode (textarea + control row below), this read fine — the textarea was the only thing in its row, so its natural line-box height carried the V breathing. The bug was specific to `--single` mode where the text-input and button-clusters share a row.

**Rev 2 — V + H padding added, reverted same day**. User DOM-inspected the M-tier `--single` composer and reported the input rendered at `padding: 0 6px`, with text glyphs sitting flush against `__field`'s top and bottom edges. User's literal proposal:
- `padding-top: 0 → 4px`, `padding-bottom: 0 → 4px`
- `padding-left: 6 → 8px`, `padding-right: 6 → 8px`

The assistant implemented this via two new token scales (`--cmp-text-py: 2/4/6` and `--cmp-text-px: 4/8/12` across S/M/L) and changed `__field`'s `align-items: stretch` → `center` so the centered input/textarea respected the padded region. Visible H/V landed at 14H/13V at M, 20H/17V at L, inside §6.1.1's case-B band.

User then immediately reversed the decision: "the height of this should not be increased, it should still hug the buttons at 6px padding on all sides. the height of the single row composer should be 32px button + 12 padding combined top and bottom = 44px? is that correct?" Followed by: "i was referring to why this div has a generous 8px padding horizontally, and where that value even came from."

**Rev 3 (current) — V and H both 0**. Reverted `--cmp-text-py` and `--cmp-text-px` to 0 across all tiers. The single-row composer height now resolves cleanly to `outer-pad + control-h + outer-pad`:

| Tier | Outer pad × 2 | Control h | Single-row total |
|---|---|---|---|
| S | 12 | 28 | **40** |
| M (default) | 12 | 32 | **44** |
| L | 16 | 40 | **56** |

Text breathing now comes from:
- **V**: `--cmp-pad` (6 at S/M, 8 at L) + natural line-box leading (~3px from line-box to cap-top at 14/20 mono). At M: 6 + 3 = ~9px visible V, comfortably inside the case-B band.
- **H (between text and buttons)**: `--cmp-cluster-gap` (4 at every tier per §6.5 tight-cluster rule). At M: text is 4px from the leading cluster and 4px from the trailing cluster.
- **H (text to surface L/R, `--double` mode only)**: `--cmp-pad` (6 at S/M, 8 at L).

**Why the H padding was "generous 8px" in Rev 2 and where it came from**. The 8px H at M was the `--cmp-text-px = space-s (8)` value introduced as part of Rev 2's "case-B asymmetric breathing" rationale — one ramp step above `--cmp-pad`. The rationale was inherited from §6.1.5's "+2 horizontal" text-wrapper rule, which applies to inline-flex `__label` spans (Button/Badge/Chip/Nav-item label) that need an internal H wrapper to compensate for the parent's symmetric chrome padding. It does NOT apply to direct `input`/`textarea` elements that have no inner text-wrapper — the text glyph in an `<input>` is already inset from its own content edge by the browser's default `padding: 0` (or whatever the cascade resolves it to). Wrapping the input in a flex container with H padding adds chrome the text glyph doesn't need; the outer surface pad + cluster gap already supply horizontal breathing.

**Why V padding was missing in Rev 1 and why returning to 0 is correct**. The text in `--single` mode IS in the same vertical zone as the buttons — and that's the chat-input identity. Every modern reference (Claude, Cursor, ChatGPT compose row) renders the input fitting the row exactly with no additional internal V padding; the text-vs-button separation comes from the **cluster gap** (4px horizontal), not from V padding (which would inflate the row). Rev 2's V padding solved a non-bug by inflating the row, which is what the user noticed and reversed.

**Tokens preserved in the cascade**. `--cmp-text-py` and `--cmp-text-px` remain in the CSS at 0 across all tiers so consumers can dial inward V/H breathing per use case (e.g., a chat-input look with explicit inset text) without losing the per-tier scale or changing the `__field` CSS rule. The system defaults stay at 0 to preserve the canonical "button + 2×pad" composer arithmetic.

**Lesson**: the §6.1.5 "+2 horizontal" text-wrapper rule applies to inline-flex `__label` spans inside hard-edged control chrome, not to direct `input`/`textarea` elements in container surfaces. The composer's `__field` is a layout wrapper for the input, not a text-wrapper for a label — the input's own native rendering owns its text inset. Adding internal padding to `__field` adds chrome the text doesn't need, breaks the canonical "button + 2×pad" height arithmetic, and pushes the composer past industry norms for the dense chat-row identity.

### B.18 §9.8 Toast + §9.10 Banner — icon-wrapper V-compensation (May 16, 2026)

**The bug.** In Toast and Banner, the leading icon appeared **vertically centered** with the title in **single-line** content but **3px above the title cap-top** in **multi-line** content (title + description). The user inspected the rendered DOM and reported the icon felt visually disconnected from the text in the multi-line case.

**The geometry, worked out.** Both components use the §6.1.3 universal icon-wrapper pattern (`display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center`) with a flex-centered SVG inside. Before the fix:
- Toast icon wrapper: **20W × 20H** around a 16-icon (4px equal padding on all sides via `--tst-icon-pad: 2` + the inner glyph's `--tst-icon: 16`).
- Banner icon wrapper: **24W × 24H** around a 20-icon (4px equal padding on all sides via `--bn-icon-pad: 2`).
- Body wrapper: text inside `.ds-toast__body` / `.ds-banner__body` carrying V padding via `--{tst,bn}-body-py: 2`. The body's **first-line row height** therefore was `line-height (20) + 2 × body-py (2) = 24`.

In **single-line layout** (`align-items: center` on the row): the row container is 24-tall (content row = surface − 8/8 padding = 24 for Toast; the same calc for Banner). The 20-tall icon wrapper centers in the 24-row at `(24 − 20) / 2 = 2` from row-top; the 24-tall body wrapper fills the row exactly. The icon-glyph's vertical center sits at row-top + 10; the title-line's vertical center sits at row-top + 12 (the line's leading-trimmed cap-mid). Off by 2px in absolute terms but **the eye reads them as centered** because both children flex-center on the same row baseline.

In **multi-line layout** (`align-items: flex-start` on the row, fires when `:has(.ds-{tst,bn}__title):has(.ds-{tst,bn}__description)`): both children top-align at row-top. The 20-tall icon wrapper sits at row-top + 0 with the glyph centered inside at wrapper-top + 2 = row-top + 2. The body wrapper's first-line row sits at row-top + 0 with the title cap-top ~3px below that (line-box leading per §6.1.4). The icon glyph at row-top + 2 vs the title cap-top at row-top + ~3 doesn't match — **and the visual asymmetry shows because both children have different intrinsic heights** (20 vs 24). The line-box leading provides ~3px of "padding above the cap" for free; the icon-glyph in a flex-centered wrapper doesn't have an analog.

**The fix — V-compensate the icon wrapper to match the body's first-line row height.** Make the icon wrapper 24-tall (same as the body's first-line row) by setting V padding to `--{tst,bn}-icon-pad + --{tst,bn}-body-py` (= 4), keeping H padding at `--{tst,bn}-icon-pad` (= 2). Wrapper dimensions become:
- **Toast**: 20W × 24H around 16-icon (V=4, H=2).
- **Banner**: 24W × 24H around 20-icon (V=4, H=2).

With wrapper = body-row in height, both children share a vertical center in **both** layout modes:
- Single-line (`align-items: center`): both 24-tall children center in the 24-tall content row → icon-glyph center and title-line center both sit at row-vertical-center ✓.
- Multi-line (`align-items: flex-start`): both 24-tall children top-align → icon-glyph center and title-line center both sit at row-top + 12 ✓.

H position of the glyph is unchanged (wrapper stays icon + 4 wide, body wrapper position unaffected). The remaining ~1px in absolute pixels (icon-glyph center vs title cap-top) is the system-wide leading-induced inset per §6.1.4 — accepted across every modern toast/banner reference (Apple HIG, M3 snackbar, Radix, shadcn, Sonner, Atlassian Banner, Primer Flash).

**Dropped revs (why other fixes were rejected)**:
- **Rev α — symmetric 2px wrapper (20×20 / 24×24, no V-compensation)**: the pre-fix state. Correct single-line read but multi-line off-by-3 because wrapper top-aligned at row-top while body's first-line row was 24-tall.
- **Rev β — `padding-top: 10` on the surface**: would shift the glyph down to title cap-top in multi-line, but forces an off-ramp value (10 isn't in the spacing ramp) AND breaks the universal `--tst-pad` / `--bn-pad` = 8 default for all toasts/banners. Single-line breaks too because the surface padding affects EVERYTHING in the row, not just the icon.
- **Rev γ — remove letterbox entirely (16×16 wrapper for Toast, 20×20 for Banner)**: worse single-line because the glyph sits low (it loses the V-centering breathing the letterbox provided in single-line). Multi-line still off.
- **Rev δ (current) — V-compensate**: only solution that fixes multi-line without breaking single-line or the surface padding tokens. **Banner adopts the same fix in lockstep with Toast** because both components share the same internal-architecture pattern (§9.10 explicitly says "Same fix as Toast — see §9.8 for full rationale").

**The rule for future container components with icons + multi-line bodies**: when the body wrapper has V padding that makes its first-line row taller than the icon's natural frame, V-compensate the icon wrapper to match the body row's height. This is exactly the §6.1.4 "leading-induced inset" pattern applied at the wrapper level — the line-box reserves leading above the cap for free, so an icon-glyph wrapper that wants to align with the cap-top needs to either (a) live in a wrapper sized to match the body's first-line row, OR (b) accept the ~3px gap as the §6.1.4 sanctioned asymmetry. Toast and Banner pick (a); Card, Dialog, and Tooltip (whose icon use cases are different) pick whichever fits their content shape.

**Lesson**: the icon-wrapper V-padding is **derived from the body's first-line row height**, not chosen by feel. When a container component changes its body's V padding or line-height, the icon wrapper must follow in lockstep. This is the same kind of cascade Card and Dialog have for outer radius being derived from inner buttons (§2.1.2 inverse rule) — derived values, not chosen ones.

### B.19 §9.5 Tooltip + §9.8 Toast + §9.10 Banner — uniform-8/8 family with square icon wrappers (May 16, 2026)

**The request.** Within hours of B.18 landing, the user pushed for a simpler, more uniform model. The B.18 V-compensated icon wrapper (rectangular wrapper with V > H internal padding) fixed the multi-line alignment but produced **asymmetric internal chrome** (icon glyph sat at 4V/2H inside the wrapper, not 2V/2H), and the three sibling components (Toast/Banner/Tooltip) still had divergent surface paddings (Toast 8/8, Banner 8/8, Tooltip 8/12) — three near-identical floating/in-flow surfaces with three different padding contracts.

**The new rule (Rev F/G/B).** Unify the three components into a "uniform-8/8 family":

1. **Surface padding: 8/8 uniform** on all three. Tooltip switches from 8/12 to 8/8.
2. **Icon wrappers: SQUARE, sized to the body's text line-height** (Toast 20×20 around 16-icon, Banner 24×24 around 20-icon). Internal padding is 2/2 symmetric on all sides. Replaces the B.18 V-compensated rectangular wrappers.
3. **Body wrapper padding chosen per-component** so the visible distance from the OUTER surface edge to the text glyph stays comfortable:
   - Banner: 2V / 4H text wrapper → 8 (surface) + 4 (wrapper) = **12H visible** from surface edge to text glyph
   - Tooltip: 2V / 4H text wrapper → matches Banner exactly (12H visible)
   - Toast: 0V / 2H text wrapper (compact-identity exception) → 8 + 2 = **10H visible**, tighter than Banner because Toast's compact-overlay identity (40px min-height, 360px max-width) favors a tighter cell

**Why a single rule (uniform surface pad) replaces three different surface paddings**. The user's framing: "the goal here is to make it so all components have the same padding all the way around." Pre-rev, the rationale for Tooltip's 8/12 was "case-B asymmetric H breathing for text-bearing tight surfaces," matching Toast's old text-side padding. But once Toast/Banner moved to a body-wrapper-handles-the-asymmetry model, Tooltip's surface-level asymmetric padding became a holdout. Moving Tooltip to 8/8 + adding the body wrapper (2V/4H, matching Banner) closes the gap: the case-B asymmetric breathing is now entirely a body-wrapper responsibility across the family, and the surface pad is a clean 8/8 across all three.

**Why the icon wrappers become square** (replacing B.18's V-compensated rectangles):

The B.18 V-compensation (wrapper V > H) fixed the multi-line alignment by making the wrapper match the body's first-line row height. But it produced asymmetric chrome around the icon glyph — 4V/2H internal padding meant the glyph wasn't centered to its own wrapper, just centered to the body row. **Two costs**:

1. **Asymmetric internal chrome reads as drift**: the icon wrapper looked like a deliberate 20×24 rectangle (Toast) or 24×24 box with 4V/2H padding (Banner) — neither of which fits the system's "icons live in square frames" mental model. Every other icon wrapper in the system (Button, Badge, Nav, Input, etc.) sits in a frame where V = H.
2. **The fix was overspecific**: the asymmetry only fired because of the body wrapper's V padding (2px) adding to the natural line-box leading. If body wrapper V padding changes (e.g., Toast drops to 0V to differentiate from Banner), the icon V padding has to change with it. That's a per-component derivation, hard to remember.

**The square-wrapper approach trades 2-3px of multi-line residual for systemic clarity**. With square wrappers, multi-line still has the icon-glyph sitting ~2-3px above the title cap-top (the same §6.1.4 leading-induced asymmetry every modern toast/banner accepts). Single-line is perfect (square wrapper of height = body wrapper's natural height = both center on the row). The trade:

| Approach | Single-line | Multi-line | Internal chrome | Cascade complexity |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Rev B.18 (V-compensated rectangle)** | ✓ centered | ✓ aligned | Asymmetric V/H around glyph | High (wrapper V = `icon-pad + body-py`) |
| **Rev B.19 (square wrapper)** | ✓ centered | 2-3px residual (§6.1.4 sanctioned) | Symmetric 2/2 around glyph | None (wrapper V = wrapper H = line-height) |

The user explicitly accepted the trade ("the goal here is to make it so all components have the same padding all the way around") — perfect single-line alignment + ~2-3px multi-line residual is the canonical industry pattern (Apple HIG, M3 snackbar, Radix, shadcn, Sonner, Atlassian Banner, Primer Flash all show this exact residual in their multi-line states). The B.18 fix was correct geometry for the wrong target; Rev B.19 is correct geometry for the right target (systemic clarity).

**Tooltip's role in the family** (no icon, but joins the family anyway):

Tooltip doesn't have a leading icon, so the "square icon wrapper" rule doesn't directly apply to it. But the **surface pad + body wrapper architecture** does:
- Tooltip surface: 8/8 (was 8/12) — joins Banner/Toast
- Tooltip text wrapper: 2V/4H — joins Banner
- Visible H from surface edge to text glyph: 8 + 4 = 12 → matches Banner exactly

The kbd cluster in Tooltip's trailing slot still uses the §6.1.2 per-edge override (`padding-right` collapses to 8 when kbd is the trailing element, so the rightmost kbd has corner-symmetric 8/8 inset to the surface). The surface pad rule + the per-edge override rule co-exist.

**Cross-system family analog.** Most modern systems have a "small floating/in-flow surface family" with uniform internal architecture:

| System | Family members | Shared architecture |
|---|---|---|
| Sonner | Toast (only; no banner-equivalent) | n/a |
| shadcn | Toast (Sonner), Alert (banner-equivalent), Tooltip | Shared theme bg, similar padding tokens |
| Atlassian | Flag (toast), SectionMessage (banner), Tooltip | Different chrome, but similar information-density tier |
| Material 3 | Snackbar (toast), Banner (inline alert), Tooltip | Different chrome (dark vs light vs inverse), similar information-density |
| **Ours (Rev B.19)** | **Toast, Banner, Tooltip** | **8/8 surface pad + square icon wrappers (when present) + body wrapper handles case-B asymmetry** |

Our rev makes the family relationship **explicit at the architecture level**: same surface pad, same icon wrapper rule, same body wrapper rule (with one principled per-component exception for Toast body). Future "small floating/in-flow surface" additions (Snackbar, inline alert, simple Drawer header) inherit this architecture by joining the family.

**Dropped revs in the path to B.19**:
- **Rev α (Toast pre-B.18, asymmetric 8/12 surface)**: had case-B asymmetric breathing baked into the surface pad. Replaced by body wrapper.
- **Rev β (Toast B.18, V-compensated wrapper)**: solved multi-line at the cost of asymmetric internal chrome. Replaced by square wrapper + accepted residual.
- **Rev γ (considered: Banner with 24×24 icon glyph matching the 24×24 wrapper, 0 internal pad)**: would eliminate the wrapper-larger-than-line-height question, but produced an icon glyph too large (24px) for the body-tier line-height (20). The 20-icon + 24-wrapper combination is the cleaner answer because the icon-glyph stays at icon-m (20) which is the canonical "body-tier in-flow surface" icon size across the system.

**Lesson**: when three sibling components show similar architecture but diverge in details, the right move is usually to **unify at the architecture level** (same surface pad rule, same body wrapper rule, same icon wrapper rule) and document one principled exception per component (Toast body 0V/2H for compact identity). This is preferable to either (a) maintaining three different contracts that look the same to consumers, or (b) forcing perfect uniformity at the cost of each component's identity.

