### B.20 §9.17 Breadcrumb — item H pad realigned to the 28-tall row family (May 17, 2026)

**The observation.** User flagged that the Breadcrumb backplate "looks slightly too large" and asked whether padding could shrink without breaking coherence with button sizes.

**The audit.** Breadcrumb M was at `--bc-px: space-s` (8). Every other 28-tall row component in the system (Button S, Input S, Select S, Tabs S, Nav vertical M, Nav horizontal S — all routed through the §2.3 "S tier" control ramp slot) uses **`space-xs` (6)** as its horizontal padding. Breadcrumb was the outlier. The 8 came from an earlier rev where Breadcrumb chrome was being audited in isolation and the larger pad happened to match Button M's 32-tall geometry — but breadcrumb sits in body/m text flow at 28-tall row height, so M-tier button pad was the wrong reference family. Breadcrumb S was already at 6, so the divergence was M-only.

**The fix.** `--bc-px: space-xs` (6) at M, matching the existing S tier value. Net effect: H pad shrinks by 2px on each side (4px total backplate width per item), bringing breadcrumb's chrome density into line with every other 28-tall row component. The `__label` wrapper's 2px H padding (§6.1.5 minimal-offset rule) continues to provide the final visible text breathing, so the visible label-to-edge inset drops from ~10px (8 + 2) to ~8px (6 + 2) — same direction every modern reference sits in (Linear ~6-8, Vercel ~6, Apple HIG path bar ~6).

**Why this doesn't break coherence with buttons** (the user's worry):
- Breadcrumb chrome is **already differentiated from buttons** at the typographic level (body/m flow text, no weight bump, `text/secondary` rest state) and at the affordance level (inline path indicator, not a click target with primary action semantics). Tightening padding doesn't increase the conflation risk; it actually *reduces* it by making breadcrumb visually more "row in a list" and less "row of buttons."
- The button family Breadcrumb is closest to in size is Button S (28-tall), and Button S also uses `space-xs` (6) H pad. The change *strengthens* coherence with that specific button tier rather than weakening it.
- Industry alignment: every modern reference (Linear, Vercel, Atlassian, Apple HIG path bar, shadcn) uses 4-8px H pad on breadcrumb items, with 6 sitting comfortably in the median.

**Radius observation (noted, not changed).** During the audit, also noticed that the inline comment block above the breadcrumb CSS claimed "Radius is intentionally one tier below Button S (radius-3xs 4 vs radius-xs 8)" — but the actual `--bc-rd` resolves to `radius-2s` (10), the M-tier control radius from §2.3. Comment was a stale carry-over from a prior rev that experimented with the smaller radius before reverting. Comment updated in this rev to reflect the current 10px value; the radius itself was left at 10 because (a) it tracks the body/m font tier breadcrumb runs at, (b) reducing it now would compound with the padding change and produce a smaller, more rectangular backplate that diverges further from the system's "generous rounds" stance (§1.1), and (c) no user feedback flagged the radius itself as too large.

### B.21 Code-area background + Composer double-row `__field` padding (May 17, 2026)

Two unrelated fixes landed in the same pass, both responding to direct user feedback after the §B.19 uniform-8/8 rev and the §B.20 breadcrumb realignment.

**1. `.comp-frame__code-area` background — `surface/subtle` → `surface/quiet`.**

The code-area background was set to `surface/subtle` (`neutral/100` light / `neutral/1100` dark) in an earlier rev as part of the syntax-highlighting refresh that mapped `--code-tag`/`--code-attr`/`--code-str` to blue/indigo/pink 600. User feedback: "the code area background is too dark." The right token is `surface/quiet` (`neutral/50` light / `neutral/1000` dark) — the targeted in-component tint introduced May 2026 in §3.1, specifically for backgrounds where the surface needs ~0.030 ΔL above `surface/default` (vs `surface/subtle`'s 0.050 ΔL). Same family as Table's header tint, Segmented Control's track tint, and the legacy docs-table cell hover. Two-stop drop in perceived darkness fixed the read.

**Why `surface/quiet` and not a one-off light-gray override**: a private alias would have been drift (§2.2.1 anti-bloat) — the value already exists in the system for exactly this kind of "barely-elevated background tint" use case. Picking `surface/quiet` ties code-area bg to the same token Table header runs on, so a re-tonalization (or a switch to a different shade ramp for the quiet family) reflows both consumers in lockstep.

**2. `.ds-composer--double .ds-composer__field` — layout-scoped `2V / 4H` padding override.**

User flagged that in the double-row composer the textarea text "looks too close to the container" while the single-row composer "looks okay." The asymmetry has a clean structural cause:

- **Single-row** composer: input has `height: var(--cmp-control-h)` and visually fills the row at the surface's `--cmp-pad`. Text glyph's top/bottom land cleanly at `--cmp-pad` from the surface chrome (M: 6px V breathing + ~3px line-box leading = ~9 visible V; well inside the case-B band). No inward padding needed.
- **Double-row** composer: textarea auto-grows from 1 line and has no fixed control height. Without inward V padding, the text glyph's first-line cap sits flush against the surface chrome at the top edge (and the last-line baseline sits flush at the bottom). The line-box leading provides ~3px of breathing above the cap, but no comparable horizontal buffer — and 3px of "free" V breathing is too thin to read as deliberate.

**The fix**: a layout-scoped CSS override `.ds-composer--double .ds-composer__field { padding: var(--space-3xs, 2px) var(--space-2xs, 4px) }` — uniform `2V / 4H` across all three tiers (composer text is uniform 14/20 mono at S/M/L, so per-tier scaling is unnecessary). Same `2V / 4H` case-B asymmetric breathing as banner's `__body` and the §6.1.5 minimal-offset rule, applied at the layout-modifier scope so the single-row arithmetic (`outer-pad + control-h + outer-pad`, e.g., M's canonical 44px height) stays untouched.

**Why this is the right shape of fix**:

| Option | Why dropped |
|---|---|
| Apply 2V/4H to all composers via `--cmp-text-py`/`--cmp-text-px` defaults | Reverted in §B.16 because it broke the single-row canonical `2×pad + control-h` arithmetic. Single-row M went from 44 → 48, breaking the "input fills the row" identity. |
| Bump `--cmp-pad` from 6/6/8 → 8/8/10 in double mode | Would also push the trailing button cluster (32-tall at M) further from the surface edge, breaking the corner-symmetric inset that §6.1.2 guarantees for hard-edged sub-elements. Surface pad is the wrong layer to fix this on. |
| Add per-tier scaling (S: 1/2, M: 2/4, L: 3/6) | Composer text is uniform 14/20 mono across all three tiers (§9.28). The breathing rule for 14/20 mono is uniform per §6.1.5. Per-tier scaling would invent variation that doesn't exist in the source data. |
| **Layout-scoped 2V/4H override on `__field`** (chosen) | Targets exactly the case that needs the fix (double-row textarea, no fixed control height) without touching single-row, and matches the cross-component rule (`2V / 4H` is what every text-only wrapper in the system uses per §6.1.5 / §9.10). |

**Visible result after fix** (cap-top to surface inset, all tiers):
- Single-row composer: ~9V visible (= outer pad 6/6/8 + line-box leading 3) — unchanged.
- Double-row composer: ~11V visible at S/M (= outer pad 6 + inner pad 2 + line-box leading 3), ~13V at L (= 8 + 2 + 3). Matches banner exactly at L since both surfaces sit at outer pad 8 + inner pad 2.

The change also rationalized the `--cmp-text-py` / `--cmp-text-px` token semantics: they remain the *consumer-overridable defaults* (0 across all tiers), with the double-row override sitting in CSS rather than the token cascade. Consumers who want a different inward V/H breathing on either layout can still set `--cmp-text-py: Npx` / `--cmp-text-px: Npx` on a per-instance `.ds-composer` and the cascade will pick it up.

**Why not promote the override to a token (`--cmp-text-py-double: 2px` etc.)**: the override is a layout-conditional behavior, not a tier-conditional one. Tokens are for "value taste per tier"; CSS rules are for "behavior per layout." Promoting it to a token would imply per-tier tunability, which the rev intentionally rejects (uniform 2V/4H is the right answer for all three tiers because the text size is uniform). The CSS rule encodes "this is structural, not configurable" and that's the correct semantic.

**3. Search component — decision: no new primitive.**

User asked whether a dedicated Search component should be added separate from Combobox. The answer is **no, not necessary**, on the standard §2.2.1 anti-bloat grounds + clean composition path through existing primitives:

| Use case | Existing primitive | How to invoke |
|---|---|---|
| Simple search field (no dropdown, no autocomplete) | `Input` | `inputEl({ leadIcon: ICON_MAGNIFYING_GLASS, placeholder: "Search…", clearable: true })` |
| Search with autocomplete / type-to-filter results | `Combobox` (§9.15) | `comboboxEl({ leadIcon: ICON_MAGNIFYING_GLASS, items: [...], emptyState: "No results" })` |
| Search with rich result rows (avatar + name + meta) | `Combobox` with custom item content | Same as above, items rendered with `avatarEl()` + label + metadata |
| Global search with scope chips and filters | Layout composition: `InputGroup` (scope Select + search Input) + Combobox panel for results | Compose existing primitives |

**Why composition wins here** (rule of last resort applied):
- Search isn't a distinct visual primitive — it's a use case that's already fully expressed by `Input` (chrome) + `ICON_MAGNIFYING_GLASS` (the universal "search" semantic glyph per §5.3 / Standing Rule #8) + optional `clearable` affordance + optional Combobox panel for results.
- Every modern reference treats it the same way: shadcn ships `Input` with the search-glyph convention (no separate `Search`); Material 3 has a `Search bar` that composes Input + button + optional list panel; Apple HIG `Search Field` is `UITextField` with a search-style leading affordance. Primer ships a `TextInput` with an `--leading-visual` slot — same composition pattern.
- Adding a `Search` primitive would either duplicate Input's chrome (one component for the field, one for the field-with-leading-glyph — drift) or wrap Combobox with a marginally different default (one component for autocomplete-without-glyph, one for autocomplete-with-glyph — also drift). Neither earns its own component page.

**When this rule would change**: if a real consumer ships a search pattern that needs (a) substantively different chrome than Input (e.g., a Spotlight-style command-palette overlay with global-keystroke handling and Esc-to-dismiss) AND (b) that pattern can't be expressed by composing Combobox inside a Dialog. The first known consumer that would justify it is a global Cmd+K command palette — if/when one ships, evaluate then. For all other "search field with a magnifying glass" cases, the answer is to compose existing primitives.

Documentation lives in `design-system-spec.mdc` Part 11 (Input row) — the Search field pattern is already listed alongside Email, URL, Money, Date as a composition-over-proliferation example, with `leadIcon: ICON_MAGNIFYING_GLASS` as the canonical invocation. No new entry needed.

### B.22 Composer tiered `__field` padding + demo simplification + "Dropdown" → "Model" rename (May 17, 2026, evening)

Three changes landed in one pass after the §B.21 layout-scoped `2V / 4H` rev was tested in product. User feedback drove all three.

**1. Layout-scoped `2V / 4H` → uniform tiered padding across both layouts.**

User reviewed the §B.21 result and asked for **more** breathing at the larger tiers, with the explicit ramp `4V/6H` for S, `6V/8H` for M, `8V/10H` for L — and applied to *all* composers, not just `--double`. Their framing: "the text becomes more inset at larger sizes but still maintains the offset px values" — the case-B H = V + 2 rule preserved, but V and H both scale with surface tier instead of staying uniform.

The new ramp:

| Tier | `--cmp-text-py` | `--cmp-text-px` | Ramp source |
|---|---|---|---|
| S | `space-2xs` (4) | `space-xs` (6) | spacing ramp |
| M | `space-xs` (6) | `space-s` (8) | spacing ramp |
| L | `space-s` (8) | **10px (off-ramp)** | spacing ramp; 10 is between `space-s` (8) and `space-m` (12) |

The L-tier 10px H pad is the only off-ramp value. The spacing ramp jumps from 8 → 12 without an intermediate step, and the case-B rule (H = V + 2) forces H to be 10 when V = 8. The alternatives are both worse:
- L H = 8 (uniform with V) breaks the case-B rule (`8 − 8 = 0`)
- L H = 12 breaks the case-B rule in the opposite direction (`12 − 8 = 4`, more asymmetric than M/S)

10 is the only value that preserves H = V + 2 at L. Documented as a sanctioned per-component constant in §1.4.5. Future absorption path: add `space-2s = 10` to the spacing ramp (analogous to `radius-2s = 10` and `radius-2m = 14` added to the radius ramp in §2.1.1 for the same kind of in-between need). Defer until ≥3 consumers need it; until then, the 10px lives as a per-component literal.

**Why uniform across `--single` and `--double` layouts**: an earlier rev (§B.21) restricted the inward padding to `--double` because the single-row composer's input "visually fills the row." User pushback after testing: the single-row composer's text glyph sat at the surface edges at `--cmp-pad` (6 at M) + ~3px leading = ~9px visible V, which read as "text flush against chrome" rather than "comfortably inset." The fix is to apply the same tiered inward padding to both layouts. The trade-off: the M single-row composer grows from `2×6 + 32 = 44` to `2×6 + 2×6 + 32 = 56` (12px taller), but every tier now reads as a deliberately padded chip with text comfortably inset from the surface edges, matching the visual density of the surrounding ramp (Toast 40 floor + Banner-style inset chrome). The §B.21 "single-row identity = `2×pad + control-h`" arithmetic is superseded — the canonical single-row composer is now `2×outer-pad + 2×field-pad-V + control-h` (S: 48, M: 56, L: 72).

**Why tiered (not uniform)**: composer text *is* uniform 14/20 mono across all three tiers, so a uniform `4V / 6H` would technically satisfy the case-B rule everywhere. But uniform padding doesn't carry the size-tier identity — at L the composer is the focal element of the page (Claude/ChatGPT homepage hero) and benefits from more pronounced inward breathing; at S it's a quiet inset chip (Cursor sidebar / Linear inline comment) and a tighter inward pad reinforces "quiet contained surface." The tiered ramp encodes that identity gradient.

**Why this works as a *system-wide* technique** (the user explicitly asked "can other components use this technique?"). The "tiered V/H padding on a direct text-wrapper" pattern is **safe and applicable** wherever a component:
1. Comes in size tiers (S/M/L), AND
2. Has a direct text wrapper that can carry its own inward padding (a `__field`, `__body`, `__text`, `__caption`), AND
3. Wants size-tier breathing scaled to the surface's overall density identity, AND
4. The case-B asymmetric rule (H > V, with H = V + 2 as a pleasing default) holds for the text content.

But there are constraints — **don't** reach for it on every component:

| Pattern | Tiered V/H technique suitable? | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Composer (current consumer)** | ✓ Yes — uses it | Three tiers, direct `__field` wrapper, scaled identity from compact-sidebar to hero. |
| Card | ⚠️ Could, but doesn't ship tiers today | Card is a single-size layout primitive (§9.13 "no size tiers"); the tiered pattern is moot until tiered Cards exist. |
| Toast / Banner | ✗ No | These already use `2V / 4H` minimal-offset uniformly across the family (§6.1.5); their text is uniform `body/m`, not tier-scaled. Adding tier scaling would invent variation the source data doesn't have. |
| Tooltip | ✗ No | Single canonical size; the §B.19 `4 / 8` padding pair is already uniform per the uniform-8/8 family. No tier to scale against. |
| Dialog | ✗ No | Single canonical width (440px); body uses minimal-offset `2 / 4` per §6.1.5. The whole component is single-tier. |
| Input / Select / Textarea | ✗ No | Already tier-scaled at the *container* layer via `--inp-h` / `--sel-h` / `--txa-h` and `--{prefix}-px`. The text inside is *vertically centered* (single-line) in a fixed-height control — there's no direct text-wrapper that benefits from per-tier inward padding. The exception is `Textarea` where text fills the height vertically; today's `--txa-py` already scales per tier (§6.1.1), so it's effectively already using this technique without the explicit framing. |
| Button / Badge / Chip | ✗ No | Atomic inline-flex labels with `__label` wrappers; per-component `--{prefix}-px` already provides the asymmetric H breathing at the container layer (§6.1.5 Path B). Adding inward padding on the label wrapper would double-apply and inflate the control height. |
| Card title / body / footer | ⚠️ Card-specific | Card uses `--cd-pad` (12) uniform on the surface, with internal regions having no padding of their own. If Card ever ships tiers, *and* internal regions are made into direct text-wrappers, this technique would apply. |

**The general rule for invoking the technique**: if a component is (1) tier-scaled, (2) has a direct text-wrapper, and (3) that wrapper's inward breathing should grow with the tier identity — use this technique. Otherwise the system's existing patterns (uniform minimal-offset, container-layer asymmetric padding) already handle it.

For now, Composer is the only consumer. Document this section's table so the technique is named ("tiered text-wrapper inward padding"), provably scoped, and easy to grep when a future tiered component asks the same question.

**2. Demo simplification — combine state variants into one frame, remove icon permutations.**

User: "combine all the variants so default, focused, error, etc are all in the same frame, make sure this is consistent across all components. no need to show all the icon variations with/without dropdown, etc. remove the 2nd button from leading and the trailing button before the dropdown."

`renderComposer` was carrying:
- A "Default" frame
- A "Sizes" frame
- A "Layouts" frame (double / single side by side)
- A "States" frame (default / focused / error / disabled, each as a separate `compFrame`)
- A "Variants" frame (with-dropdown / no-dropdown / no-leading)

After simplification:
- A "Default" frame combining all four states in one vertical stack
- A "Sizes" frame (S / M / L stacked)
- A "Layouts" frame (double / single stacked)
- No "Variants" frame

The slot permutations (with-dropdown / no-dropdown / no-leading) were enumerating "the same component rendered N ways with one slot toggled" without adding pedagogical value — every Composer demo already shows the canonical configuration, and consumers can toggle slots through the API. Removing the section also cleaned the Composer page from 5 large frames to 3.

The Default frame now stacks all four states (default / focused / error / disabled) using the established vertical-stack pattern (§7.1.1 variant-comparison column with shared left edge, but applied to a single-column-of-state variants instead of a multi-component row). This matches how Card / Toast / Banner render their tones — one frame, multiple instances, single visual unit centered in the frame.

`baseLeading` was reduced from a 2-button cluster to a single button. `baseTrailing` (which previously held one button before the dropdown) was reduced to an empty array. The slot permutations the demos were illustrating (1 button + trailing button + dropdown vs 1 button + dropdown vs dropdown alone) collapsed to one canonical pattern: 1 leading icon-button + Select + primary send. The system's "minimal canonical configuration" stance — render the simplest pattern that exercises the component's identity, not every permutation a consumer could ship.

**Why this is a system-wide pattern**: the user explicitly asked it be consistent across all components. The principle is: each `compFrame` shows ONE pedagogical concept. Default = the canonical look. Sizes = the tier ramp. Layouts = the layout modes. States = ALL the state variants STACKED IN ONE FRAME (not one frame per state). Variants = optional, only when a slot permutation carries a real visual distinction worth illustrating. Most components already follow this; Composer was an outlier with per-state frames. The fix brings it into line.

The forward task is to audit other components for similar drift — anywhere a `renderXxx()` function has multiple `compFrame`s for state variants when one stacked frame would do, simplify. Not in this rev's scope (component-by-component audit can be its own task), but the rule is now documented.

**3. "Dropdown" → "Model" rename in Composer demos.**

User: "would it be better to just rename dropdown to button? is that more consistent with other components."

The renaming is *better than* "Button" but for a different reason than the user proposed. The trailing Select in a Composer isn't "a button" — it's a Select trigger. Renaming the slot value to "Button" would be confusing (the actual primary send Button sits *next to* the Select). The right rename is "**Model**" — that's the canonical value in this slot across every AI-chat reference (Claude, Cursor, ChatGPT, Gemini all label the trailing Select "Model" or show the active model's name). It's a real semantic label, not a slot placeholder, and matches what users see in production chat UIs.

`baseModel` returns `{ value: "Model", width: "auto" }`. The previous `baseDropdown` helper (which returned `{ value: "Dropdown", width: "auto" }`) is removed. Same Select chrome, same composition pattern, more accurate label.

**Why not "Button"**: would conflate the trailing Select with the send Button next to it.

**Why not generic "Select"**: per §7.3 placeholder copy, slot labels use the slot's canonical role name (e.g., Dialog footer buttons are labeled "Button" because their slot is "a button"; Dropdown trigger labels resolve to "Actions" because that's the cross-system slot name for "this opens a menu of actions"). For Composer's trailing Select, the cross-system slot name is "Model" — Claude, Cursor, ChatGPT, Gemini all use it.

**Why not a different domain word (e.g., "Profile", "Account")**: would imply a use case that isn't universal to Composer. "Model" is what AI-chat composers carry; other use cases for Composer (if they emerge) would label their Select differently, but the demo should reflect the canonical AI-chat reference because that's the primary use case the component was built for.

### B.23 Breadcrumb chrome cleanup — no strokes, neutral colors, generic "Breadcrumb" demo copy (May 17, 2026, evening)

User question: "should the breadcrumb have strokes? and should the color just be neutral? also rename the copy to be just breadcrumb."

**Three coordinated changes.** All three flow from the same observation: the implementation had been drifting away from the §9.17 spec grammar ("inline text path with chevron separators; no per-item backplate"). Two segment types had quietly grown chrome that contradicted the spec.

**1. `.ds-breadcrumb__current` — remove the soft-brand chip.**

| Property | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| color | `var(--color-action-soft-brand-text)` (brand 600 — `text/brand` family) | `var(--color-text-primary)` (neutral) |
| background | `var(--color-action-soft-brand-bg)` (brand 50 tinted fill) | (none) |
| border-radius | `var(--bc-rd)` | (irrelevant — no background to round) |

The current segment is the last item in the path — the "you are here" indicator. Across every modern system (Primer, shadcn, Atlassian, Linear, Vercel Geist, Apple HIG path bar, Material 3) the current segment is rendered in neutral primary text with no chip / no border / no background. The brand-colored chip the implementation had been carrying was an older Sentry/Bootstrap-era pattern that no modern system maintains. It was also visually conflicting with brand-colored buttons elsewhere on the page (a Pagination active page in soft-brand + a current breadcrumb in soft-brand on the same view created two "you are here" signals competing).

**2. `.ds-breadcrumb__dropdown` — remove the rest-state chip and the 1px border.**

| Property | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| color | `var(--color-action-soft-neutral-text)` | `var(--color-text-primary)` |
| background | `var(--color-action-soft-neutral-bg)` (filled chip at rest) | `transparent` (no rest backplate) |
| border | `1px solid var(--color-action-soft-neutral-border)` | `0` |
| hover bg | `var(--color-action-soft-neutral-hover-bg)` | `var(--color-action-hover)` (matches link items exactly) |
| hover border | `var(--color-action-soft-neutral-hover-border)` | (none) |

The dropdown trigger is one of three interactive segment types in a breadcrumb (link, dropdown, ellipsis). All three should share the same chrome — the affordance signal is in the trailing chevron-down (or `…`), not in a chip. Adding a chip at rest pulled the dropdown trigger visually toward Button-soft-neutral and blurred the §9.17 grammar (which says the breadcrumb's identity is the text path + chevron separators, NOT chips). After the fix, all three interactive segments have identical chrome: transparent at rest, `action/hover` bg overlay on hover, neutral text.

**Why this is enforced as a hard rule, not a "designer preference"**: when the implementation drifted to add chips, the breadcrumb visually fragmented into "regular link, chip-styled dropdown, chip-styled current page" instead of a uniform path. The fragmentation broke the §9.17 grammar table's claim that breadcrumb is "distinct from every other navigation primitive." Once the dropdown and current chrome started looking like a Chip or a Button-soft-neutral, the eye stopped reading "this is a breadcrumb" and started reading "this is a row of mixed pills." The fix restores the single grammar (path + chevrons, hover overlay only) and §9.17 now has a "No strokes, no rest-state backplates" subsection that lists the banned configurations explicitly so this drift doesn't reappear.

**3. Demo copy — every breadcrumb segment relabeled to "Breadcrumb".**

User: "rename the copy to be just breadcrumb."

Every demo variant in `renderBreadcrumb()` was updated. Per §7.3 placeholder copy convention ("Default preview" → component name; "State demos" → state name on first segment), the implementation:

- **Default / Sizes / Icons / Icon-only / With dropdown / Truncated demos**: every segment labeled "Breadcrumb". The repetition is intentional — it puts the eye on the *chrome and structure* rather than on per-segment content variety. Same pattern Toast and Card use for tones demos (every tone card uses the same generic labels so the eye reads the color difference, not the copy).
- **States demo**: first segment uses the state name (`Default`, `Hover`, `Pressed`, `Focused`, `Disabled`, `Current`) and the rest are "Breadcrumb". Per §7.3 "State demos" pattern (state name = subject of the demo).
- **Icon-only `aria-label` and `tooltipEl` text**: also updated to "Breadcrumb" so screen reader announcement matches visible content.

Earlier the demos used "Home / Section / Page" which read as a realistic site IA. That worked when the demos were demonstrating "what a breadcrumb on a real product page looks like," but the simplification rev (§B.22) pushed every component toward generic slot-label demos so the system reads as docs-density consistent. Breadcrumb was the last component carrying realistic copy without justification (no §7.3 "sanctioned realistic copy" rule applies — there's no "URL pattern" or "shortcuts" semantic in a breadcrumb that requires realism).

**Forward note**: §9.17's new "No strokes, no rest-state backplates" subsection includes a banned-configurations table. Future audits should flag any breadcrumb implementation that adds `background`, `border`, or `border-bottom` at rest to any segment, anywhere on the breadcrumb container. The hover overlay is the only chrome breadcrumb segments are allowed to carry, and it lives in `:hover` selectors only.

