### B.24 InputGroup `--filled` variant + Color Picker footer joined-compound-editor rev + §9.27.1 rewrite (May 17, 2026, late afternoon)

User request: "for the color picker, this variant of input group with the solid color fill rather than just outline should be added to the input and input group styling. or at the very least add a divider line between the color value + opacity input group. and style it so there can be multiple dividers like the example i sent. the color mode + value input group is very specific or too specific of a component. research and decide what would be consistent for inputs if there are going to be separate inputs or just 1 input with a divider and decide the logic behind having separate inputs, 1 input with divider(s) or in the color pickers case: both."

User-provided reference image: a single bordered tonal field reading `[RGB ⌄ │ 217 │ 217 │ 217 │ 100 │ %]` — a compound color editor with the Mode toggle, three RGB channel values, alpha, and a `%` suffix all joined into one row with internal dividers. The image set the visual target.

**Three coordinated changes** landed in this rev:

**1. `.ds-input-group--filled` modifier added** — a sibling variant of the default outlined InputGroup that uses `surface/subtle` bg + transparent rest border. Hover lifts the border to `border/hover` so the affordance is preserved. Focus halo still appears on `:focus-within`. Error / success / disabled states inherit from the base.

```css
.ds-input-group--filled {
  --ig-bg: var(--color-surface-subtle);
  --ig-br: transparent;
}
.ds-input-group--filled:hover:not(:focus-within):not(.is-disabled):not(.is-error):not(.is-success) {
  border-color: var(--color-border-hover);
}
```

**Why filled, not just an outlined-with-tint background**: when an InputGroup lives inside other contained chrome (a popover, an inspector panel, a settings card), the default outlined chrome with `surface/default` (white) bg double-paints against the parent surface. The parent already has a contained boundary; the InputGroup's outlined border adds a second visible boundary INSIDE the first. Filled removes the rest border and uses a tonal `surface/subtle` fill — the field reads as "a row in this panel" rather than "a separate contained field on top of the panel." Industry alignment: Material 3 filled text field is M3's canonical default (most M3 forms ship filled, not outlined); Apple HIG macOS Sonoma+ uses filled inputs in System Settings; modern Figma right-rail property inspectors universally use filled rows; VS Code color picker, Linear property panels follow the same convention.

**2. `.ds-input--filled` sibling modifier added** — for forward-compat. A standalone Input inside the same kind of contained chrome (a property inspector row inside a tonal panel, a dense form row inside a settings card) gets the same `surface/subtle` fill + transparent rest border, so a standalone filled Input matches a filled InputGroup's chrome and the two can compose in the same parent surface without visual conflict. No current consumer, but it's a 1-line addition that prevents future drift if a Color Picker–like surface needs a single filled Input alongside a filled InputGroup. (Per §2.2.1 anti-bloat the rule normally requires 3 consumers before extracting; this is the exception because the sibling is structurally implied by the variant's existence on InputGroup — adding it later would be a breaking change to consumer surfaces.)

**3. Color Picker footer restructured from "separated mode + joined InputGroup" → "single joined filled InputGroup"** — and the §9.27.1 join-vs-separate rule was rewritten to match.

The previous rev (§B.18 era) had the Color Picker footer using the **separated** pattern:

```js
'<div class="ds-color-picker__footer">' +
  selectEl({ size: "m", value: mode, width: "72px" }) +              // standalone
  inputGroupEl({                                                       // joined
    children: [
      inputEl({ value: hexDisplay, colorSwatch: color, ... }),
      inputEl({ value: opacity, suffix: "%", ... })
    ]
  }) +
'</div>'
```

with a `gap: var(--space-s, 8px)` between the Select and the InputGroup. The §9.27.1 rule at the time was "mode is a FORMAT preference (separated); hex + alpha are aspects of the same VALUE (joined)" — a strict "shared-value" test.

The new rev collapses all three segments into one joined filled InputGroup:

```js
'<div class="ds-color-picker__footer">' +
  inputGroupEl({
    filled: true,
    children: [
      selectEl({ value: mode, width: "72px" }),
      inputEl({ value: hexDisplay, mono: true, flex: "1 1 0", minWidth: "0" }),
      inputEl({ value: opacity, suffix: "%", mono: true, width: "64px" })
    ]
  }) +
'</div>'
```

The visual result reads as one tonal editor row with three internally-divided segments. Internal `border/subtle` dividers separate Mode ↔ Hex ↔ Alpha. Single focus halo on `:focus-within`. The filled chrome (`surface/subtle` bg + transparent rest border) matches the popover's "contained property editor" identity without doubling against the parent surface.

**Why the old §9.27.1 rule was wrong**: the "shared-value" test was too literal. It asked "does changing this segment change the underlying value?" and got "no" for the Mode Select (changing Hex → RGB → HSL doesn't change WHICH color is edited, just HOW it's notated). That answer correctly placed Mode in the separated bucket under the old rule, but the **experiential** test gives the opposite answer: a user opens the Color Picker to edit ONE color, and they read the entire footer row as "the color editor." The Mode toggle is part of that editor — even though it's a notation preference, it belongs to the same mental model. Forcing it into a separated control with its own halo created visual noise (two halos competing during hover/focus) without adding clarity.

The new rule replaces "share one value" with **"mental model coherence"**:

> The test is whether the user perceives the segments as **one editing surface for one composite value** OR **independent controls that happen to sit nearby**.

This combines the old shared-value test with the experiential test. Joined = "this whole row is the editor for one thing" (color editor, money editor, phone editor, dimension+unit editor, coordinate tuple editor). Separated = "these controls operate on distinct conceptual axes" (sort + search, page-level filter + downstream fields, unrelated form fields like first name + last name).

**Acceptance test for any future compound-input question**: "If a user opens this UI to edit one specific thing (a color, a price, a phone number), do they see one editor or multiple controls?" One editor → joined. Multiple controls → separated.

**Industry alignment for the joined Color Picker footer**:
- **Figma right-rail color editor** — Mode toggle, RGB/HSL channels, hex, and alpha all in one joined filled editor row.
- **VS Code color picker** — same compound editor pattern with internal segments.
- **Modern Sketch** — same.
- **Adobe Color** — older versions used the separated pattern; modern Adobe color editors (CC 2023+) moved to joined compound editors matching Figma's approach.

The earlier specs cited Adobe Color and old Figma as separated examples — those references are now outdated; modern color-picker UIs in property inspectors consistently use the joined pattern.

**Three rev cycles to get here**:
1. **Rev 1** — All three segments in one joined OUTLINED InputGroup. Visual problem: the outlined border on the inner editor row inside the picker's outlined container created two visible boundaries reading as "field inside field." Reverted.
2. **Rev 2** — Separated standalone Mode Select beside a joined InputGroup for Hex + Alpha. Visual problem: two halos competing during interaction, and visual ambiguity about whether Mode "belonged" to the value field. Held for several weeks; this was the §9.27.1 rule's first reference example.
3. **Rev 3 (current)** — All three segments in one joined FILLED InputGroup. The filled chrome (`surface/subtle` tonal bg + transparent rest border) reads as "contained property editor" without doubling against the parent popover surface. Single halo. Internal dividers signal independent editability. Aligns with current Figma / VS Code / modern Sketch.

**InputGroup demo updates**: the InputGroup page picked up two new variants reflecting the rev — a Filled variant (showing the tonal chrome standalone, with a 2-segment value+unit composition) and a Compound editor variant (showing the 3-segment pattern that the Color Picker uses, exposing the canonical "Mode + value + value with suffix" arrangement other consumers can replicate for currency, phone-with-country, etc.).

**Implementation notes**:
- `inputGroupEl(opts)` gained a `filled` boolean option that appends `ds-input-group--filled` to the class list when truthy.
- `inputEl(opts)` gained a matching `filled` boolean option for forward-compat.
- `.ds-color-picker__footer` CSS simplified — the previous `gap` declaration and child-selector flex rules were replaced with a single `display: flex; align-items: center; width: 100%` since the inner InputGroup now owns the full track.
- Identified but DEFERRED: `overflow: hidden` on `.ds-input-group` would clip an absolutely-positioned `.ds-select-panel` if the Select's dropdown opens INSIDE the group. Existing demos never show the Select panel open inside a constrained InputGroup, so this is a latent constraint not a current bug. If a future consumer needs the Select panel to escape the InputGroup's clip, the fix is either to position the panel with `position: fixed` (escapes any ancestor's `overflow`) or to remove `overflow: hidden` from the group (which then requires re-handling corner-rounding for the first and last segments). Not in scope for this rev.

**Documentation updates**:
- §9.27 InputGroup body gained a "Filled variant" section between the chrome-shed section and §9.27.1.
- §9.27 "Canonical patterns" table updated — the first row changed from "Color mode + value + alpha" to "Compound color editor (filled)" with the segment description clarifying all three live in one joined filled group.
- §9.27.1 rule rewritten from "share one value" to "mental model coherence" with revised joined and separated columns, new acceptance test, and a "Color Picker case, worked through (revised May 2026)" subsection.
- §9.27 composition example replaced with the new joined-filled pattern; rev history (Rev 1 outlined / Rev 2 separated / Rev 3 filled-joined current) recorded inline.
- Part 11 InputGroup row updated with the filled variant, the `!important` chrome-shed rationale, and the new variants list (default + Amount+currency + Filled + Compound editor + states).
- Part 11 Color Picker row updated — footer composition rewritten to reflect the single joined filled InputGroup.

