### B.25 InputGroup `--filled` REMOVED + Color Picker footer reverted to separated pattern + 2-segment maximum rule (May 17, 2026, evening)

User feedback after testing §B.24 in production:

> "the color picker should go back to separate input boxes in the same row for the hex selector, and the color code + opacity input should have a visible divider separating the 2 sections. use the actual component example that we have that shows the visible stroke. 3rd pic: perhaps there is not need to show such specific variations of the input, and to just show basic placeholder examples of compound editors. again, i think there should be a limit of 2 inputs tho and if there is a 3rd selector it should be a separate input.
>
> is it worth having a secondary visual option for inputs? do other systems have 2 visual variations?"

This rev reverses §B.24's three core changes and lands a stronger rule: **`InputGroup` carries exactly 2 segments**, and the system ships **one canonical Input chrome (outlined only)**.

**Industry research on filled vs outlined inputs (May 17, 2026)**:

| System | Filled variant? | Outlined variant? | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Material 3** | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes | Filled | Both ship; M3 ships filled as default because it predates the modern dense-product convention |
| **Apple HIG** | ✅ Yes (macOS Sonoma+) | ✅ Yes | Context-dependent | System Settings uses filled; document fields use outlined |
| **shadcn** | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | Outlined only | Only outlined ships; theming via background tokens covers contained-chrome contexts |
| **Vercel Geist** | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | Outlined only | Same as shadcn — one chrome, theming handles contained surfaces |
| **Linear** | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | Outlined only | Inspector rows use the same outlined chrome as form rows; the parent panel provides contrast |
| **Atlassian** | ❌ No (Subtle is a separate Compact pattern, not Input-level) | ✅ Yes | Outlined only | Property-editor rows use a custom `Compact` field pattern, not a filled Input variant |
| **Notion** | ❌ No (custom inline editors) | n/a | n/a | Notion's "inline editing" is a custom pattern, not a filled Input |
| **Stripe Dashboard** | ❌ No | ✅ Yes | Outlined only | Settings inspectors and forms share one chrome |

**Verdict: industry is split, with the modern dense-product systems (shadcn / Vercel / Linear / Atlassian / Stripe) shipping one outlined chrome only.** Material 3 and Apple HIG ship both — but both are large platform systems serving very broad product surfaces; their two-chrome model exists to support both Material's tonal-surface pattern AND traditional form chrome. Our system's positioning (designer-forward, dense-default, math-first) aligns more closely with the modern dense-product set.

**Three coordinated reversions** landed in this rev:

**1. `colorPickerEl()` footer restructured back to the SEPARATED + JOINED pattern from §B.18.**

```js
'<div class="ds-color-picker__footer">' +
  selectEl({ size: "m", value: mode, width: "64px" }) +              // standalone
  inputGroupEl({                                                      // 2-segment outlined
    size: "m",
    children: [
      inputEl({ value: hexDisplay, mono: true, flex: "1 1 0", minWidth: "0" }),
      inputEl({ value: opacity, suffix: "%", mono: true, width: "64px" })
    ]
  }) +
'</div>'
```

The Mode Select is standalone (64px wide); the Hex + Opacity Inputs join inside a 2-segment outlined InputGroup with a single internal `border/subtle` divider. The two pieces share a flex row with `gap: var(--space-s, 8px)` per §6.5 default cluster.

`.ds-color-picker__footer` CSS reverted to:

```css
.ds-color-picker__footer{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:var(--space-s,8px);width:100%}
.ds-color-picker__footer>.ds-input-group{flex:1 1 0;min-width:0}
```

**Why the revert is correct (user-facing rationale)**:
- The user explicitly preferred the separated pattern. End of story for design intent.
- The §B.24 "mental model coherence" rule was an over-correction. The original §B.18-era "shared value" test was actually right for this case — Mode is a notation preference (format), Hex+Opacity carries the value. The §B.24 rev introduced the joined-filled compound editor to chase a Figma-style aesthetic, but the resulting chrome conflicted with the system's overall stance (designer-forward dense-product, not Material-tonal-surface).
- Two halos during interaction isn't a real problem in production — the user agent shows the focus halo on the currently focused element, not on both Mode AND Hex+Opacity at the same time. The "competing halos" concern in §B.24 was theoretical.

**2. `.ds-input-group--filled` and `.ds-input--filled` CSS modifiers REMOVED.**

Removed entirely:
```css
/* DELETED */
.ds-input-group--filled { --ig-bg: var(--color-surface-subtle); --ig-br: transparent; }
.ds-input-group--filled:hover:not(:focus-within):not(.is-disabled):not(.is-error):not(.is-success) { border-color: var(--color-border-hover); }
.ds-input--filled { --inp-bg: var(--color-surface-subtle); --inp-br: transparent; }
.ds-input--filled:hover:not(:focus-within):not(.is-disabled):not(.is-error):not(.is-success) { border-color: var(--color-border-hover); }
```

And the `filled` boolean option removed from `inputGroupEl()` and `inputEl()`.

**Why removed (anti-bloat per §2.2.1)**: with the Color Picker reverted to the separated outlined pattern, `filled` has ZERO consumers. The §2.2.1 rule is "do not add a token / class that only one component uses unless that component family will plausibly grow consumers" — `filled` failed both clauses (zero consumers, no other contained-chrome surfaces lined up to consume it). Keeping a 1-line CSS rule with zero consumers is the kind of latent bloat that the anti-bloat rule exists to prevent.

If a future surface genuinely needs a tonal-fill Input (a property inspector panel with strong contained-chrome semantics, à la Material 3), the path is: (a) build the surface, (b) demonstrate that ≥2 consumers need the tonal fill, (c) reintroduce `--filled` as part of THAT consumer rollout. Until then, the system stays at one chrome.

**3. The `2-segment maximum` rule formalized as non-negotiable.**

§9.27 InputGroup gained a new explicit rule:

> **Two-segment maximum (non-negotiable, May 2026 revision).** An InputGroup carries exactly **two** segments separated by exactly one `border/subtle` divider. If a use case needs a third control (a mode/format/scope selector that influences the pair), that third control lives as a **standalone sibling Select** in the same row, with a `space-s` (8) gap separating it from the InputGroup.

Justifications:
- Beyond two segments, the joined-field grammar starts to feel cramped — the eye loses track of which segment owns which value, and dividers stack visually instead of reading as discrete separators.
- A third selector usually represents a format/notation/scope preference applied to the pair (Mode applied to Hex/Alpha, Format applied to Date start/end), not a third value of the composite. Separating it makes that role explicit.
- Industry alignment: Material 3 joined inputs, Vercel Geist Input.Group, shadcn InputGroup, Atlassian InlineGroup, Apple HIG joined fields, Figma color picker hex+alpha row — all cap at two segments.

**Updated §9.27.1 join-vs-separate rule (back to the shared-value test, with a clearer acceptance test)**:

> Does changing one control change WHAT the other holds (its value), or HOW the other is interpreted (its notation/format/scope)?
> - Changes WHAT → the controls share a composite value → JOIN them in a 2-segment InputGroup.
> - Changes HOW → the controls are independent axes → SEPARATE them with a `space-s` gap.

This is a cleaner rule than §B.24's "mental model coherence" because it gives a concrete acceptance test instead of asking the consumer to introspect about how the user perceives the row.

**InputGroup demos simplified** — the "Filled" and "Compound editor" variants from §B.24 were removed. The InputGroup page now ships three demos:
1. **Default** (Value + unit): canonical 2-segment outlined pattern
2. **Amount + currency**: 2-segment with leading `$` prefix and trailing currency Select
3. **States**: error / success / disabled

The "Compound editor" demo was a 3-segment filled pattern justified by the Color Picker; with that justification gone, the demo became a one-off (anti-bloat fail).

**Rev history captured in §9.27.1 inline**:
1. Rev 1 — separated mode + joined InputGroup (pre-§B.24)
2. Rev 2 — joined three-segment OUTLINED InputGroup (briefly attempted in §B.24 prep)
3. Rev 3 — joined three-segment FILLED InputGroup (§B.24)
4. **Rev 4 (current, §B.25)** — separated Mode Select beside a 2-segment outlined InputGroup containing Hex + Opacity. No `filled` variant exists.

**Industry alignment for Rev 4**:
- **Figma classic color picker** (pre-2024): separated mode + joined hex+alpha row, outlined chrome — matches our current pattern exactly.
- **Adobe Color (older)**: separated pattern.
- **Sketch**: separated mode + joined value row.
- **VS Code color picker**: separated.
- **Modern Figma** (2024+): joined compound editor — the §B.24 rev tried to chase this; reverted.

The point of this rev isn't "Figma's modern pattern is wrong" — it's "the system's stance is the modern dense-product set (shadcn / Vercel / Linear), and those systems converge on separated patterns + one outlined chrome." Staying aligned with that set matters more than chasing a specific Figma aesthetic.

**Documentation updates**:
- §9.27 InputGroup body: "Filled variant" section deleted; replaced with "One canonical chrome — no `filled` visual variant (May 2026 revision)" subsection with industry-research justification.
- §9.27 "Canonical patterns" table: row 1 changed from "Compound color editor (filled)" back to "Color value + opacity"; column "Inner segments" simplified to 2-segment patterns.
- §9.27 "Two-segment maximum (non-negotiable, May 2026 revision)" subsection added.
- §9.27.1 join-vs-separate rule rewritten — reverted from "mental model coherence" to "shared value" with a clearer acceptance test. Joined column documents 2-segment patterns only; separated column documents the standalone-control-beside-InputGroup pattern.
- §9.27 composition example replaced with the new separated + 2-segment-joined pattern; rev history (Rev 1–4) recorded inline.
- Part 11 InputGroup row rewritten — `--filled` references removed, 2-segment-max rule called out, variants list shortened to "Value + unit, Amount + currency, States."
- Part 11 Color Picker row footer composition rewritten — references the new separated + 2-segment-joined pattern.

**Implementation notes**:
- `inputGroupEl(opts)` no longer accepts `filled`. Calling with `filled: true` is silently ignored (boolean prop drops on the floor); the option is removed from helper signatures entirely.
- `inputEl(opts)` no longer accepts `filled`. Same treatment.
- `renderInputGroup()` `variantBlock` calls for "Filled" and "Compound editor" removed.
- `renderColorPicker()` unchanged — the helper accepts a pre-built footer string and the spec restructured that string at the source.

**Forward note**: the §B.24 rev was a rare case of the assistant chasing a Figma aesthetic without first validating against the system's stance. The lesson: when an industry pattern is split (filled vs outlined), default to whichever side matches the system's positioning. Our positioning is dense-product modern (shadcn / Vercel / Linear); when in doubt, go with that set. The next time a "should we add a variant?" question appears, the first check is "do ≥3 consumers exist?" — not "does Figma do this?".

