### §B.29 Color Picker — segmented S, eyedropper L, opacity hugs content (May 17, 2026)

#### User directive

> "is there a smaller size of the segmented control that matches the corner radius of the other components (input) and has the same shorter height as well? what button size is the eyedropper picker as well? can the button be bigger? or match the height of the 2 color sliders combined? also the divider should extend all the way from top to bottom of the input, and change it back so the 2nd input category hugs the content rather than the 2 slots sharing equal space"

Three follow-up tweaks after the §B.28 outer-chrome retier, each refining a specific sub-element so it composes more cleanly against the new 8/16 picker shell.

#### What changed

| Sub-element | Before | After | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Segmented control (view-mode picker) | `size: "m"` (32-tall, item radius 10, container radius 14) | `size: "s"` (28-tall, item radius `radius-xs` 8, container radius 12) | M-tier was reading as too prominent for a "chrome that sits above the canvas" role; the segmented control doesn't need to compete in height with the form controls in the footer. S-tier's container radius (12) sits closer to the picker's outer 16 than M-tier (14) does, so the L/R edges parallel the picker's outer corners more cleanly. Item radius 8 also lands closer to the InputGroup-M radius (10) used in the footer — segmented item and footer InputGroup read as proportionally related rather than two competing radius tiers. |
| Eyedropper button | `size: "s"` (28-tall, icon `icon-s` 16) | `size: "l"` (40-tall, icon `icon-m` 20) | The two stacked slider tracks (hue + opacity, both 16-tall) plus the 8px gap between them sum to exactly 40 — the L-tier button height. At L, the button visually bookends the sliders column, with its top edge aligned to the top of the hue track and its bottom edge aligned to the bottom of the opacity track. Earlier S-tier value left the button center-aligned mid-row, reading as a smaller floating chip rather than a deliberate peer affordance to the slider column. |
| Footer InputGroup opacity input | `flex: "1 1 0", minWidth: "0"` (50/50 split with hex) | `width: "64px"` (hugs content) | The 50/50 split (from a rev between §B.28 and §B.29) was based on the rationale that "hex and opacity read as paired editable parts." User feedback against the Cursor color picker reference confirmed the hug-content treatment reads more cleanly: hex (6 hex chars at monospace = visually wide) gets the room it needs, opacity (2-3 digits + suffix = visually narrow) gets a tight slot, and the divider lands at the natural data boundary rather than at a forced midpoint. Hex stays at `flex: "1 1 0", minWidth: "0"` to fill the remaining track. |

Divider geometry (the fourth part of the directive — "the divider should extend all the way from top to bottom of the input") was already correct in the existing CSS: `.ds-input-group > *:not(:first-child)::before { top: 0; bottom: 0 }` on the relatively-positioned child whose `height: 100%` matches the InputGroup's content area. No change needed.

#### Why these three resolve together (the picker as a vertical-flow surface)

The picker's vertical rhythm (top → bottom) is: **segmented control row → canvas → sliders row → footer**. Each row owns a different visual identity:

| Row | Identity | Sub-elements | Tier choice |
|---|---|---|---|
| Segmented control | "Pick a view mode" — chrome above the picker's content | Standalone Segmented Control | **S** — chrome should feel one step lighter than the content below |
| Canvas | "Pick saturation + lightness" — the picker's primary affordance | 240×240 square + draggable handle | Fixed |
| Sliders | "Adjust hue + opacity" — secondary affordances + eyedropper | 2 stacked 16-tall tracks + L-tier eyedropper | Sliders sit on a tier ramp (16-tall = icon-s); eyedropper at L matches their combined height |
| Footer | "Format choice + value" — output editing | Mode Select + InputGroup (hex flex, opacity hug) | M-tier — same as the trigger Input it pairs with |

Visually scanning top to bottom: segmented control is **chromy** (28-tall S, on a tonal track) → canvas is **dominant** (240-tall) → sliders are **functional** (40-tall combined, with the L eyedropper extending the column to a deliberate square) → footer is **editorial** (32-tall M, holding the precise value).

#### Forward implications

- **The "smaller segmented control matching Input radius" question is documented**: S-tier segmented (container radius 12, item radius 8) is the tier that pairs cleanest with M-tier Inputs (radius 10) for "chrome above a form" use cases. Other use cases where the segmented control wants more visual presence should keep M.
- **The "eyedropper matches stacked sliders combined height" formula** = `2 × track-height + 1 × gap` = `2 × 16 + 8 = 40` = L tier. Pattern-2 calc-derived per §1.4.4; would adjust automatically if a future picker variant changes `--cp-track-h` or `--cp-gap`.
- **The hex-flex / opacity-hug pattern** is now the documented default for any 2-segment InputGroup where one segment carries variable-width content and the other carries fixed-width content. Equal-flex remains valid when both segments carry comparable content widths.

