### §B.30 Color Picker — eyedropper L→M, sliders 16→12, three views (Spectrum default) (May 17, 2026, same day as §B.29)

#### User directive

> "make the eye dropper button smaller and the sliders shorter in vertical height. the button size should be the standard button size that matches the input and segmented control corner radius. can you make the different versions of the color picker: grid and slider. spectrum should be the default and should be in front."

Two coupled changes (a re-tier of the eyedropper button + a proportional re-tier of the slider tracks) plus the introduction of the picker's view-mode variants (Grid + Slider) with Spectrum locked as the default.

#### What changed

| Sub-element | Before (§B.29) | After (§B.30) | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Eyedropper button | `size: "l"` (40-tall, icon `icon-m` 20, radius `radius-2m` 14) | `size: "m"` (32-tall, icon `icon-s` 16, radius `radius-2s` 10) | The L-tier eyedropper from §B.29 worked geometrically (bookended the 40-tall slider column) but read as a "hero affordance" rather than a "standard form control." The user feedback was unambiguous: "the button size should be the standard button size that matches the input and segmented control corner radius." M-tier (radius 10) lands at the system's "standard form control tier" alongside Input M, Select M, Button M, and the M-tier InputGroup in the footer. The eyedropper's `radius-2s` (10) now matches the footer InputGroup's outer radius (10) exactly, so the right edge of the sliders row + the right edge of the footer share the same chrome family. |
| Slider track height (`--cp-track-h`) | `var(--icon-s, 16px)` (16-tall hue + 16-tall opacity) | `var(--icon-xs, 12px)` (12-tall hue + 12-tall opacity) | Tied to the eyedropper re-tier via the math-derivation rule from §B.29: eyedropper height = `2 × --cp-track-h + 1 × --cp-gap`. With eyedropper now at 32 and gap fixed at 8, slider track height resolves to `(32 − 8) / 2 = 12 = icon-xs`. The bookend math is preserved across the rev — only the absolute size band changed. |
| Slider handle size (`--cp-slider-handle`) | `var(--icon-s, 16px)` | `var(--icon-xs, 12px)` | Handle width = track height (handle is a perfect circle filling the track's vertical extent). Tracks the slider track size change automatically. |
| Default view | (single view — only Spectrum existed) | **Spectrum (default, in front) / Grid / Slider** — three view-mode variants exposed via the segmented control at the top | The user directive explicitly asked for Grid and Slider versions and locked Spectrum as the default/leading segment. Matches Apple ColorPanel's canonical view-mode trio. The segmented control's items reorder to `[Spectrum, Grid, Slider]` (was `[Grid, Spectrum, Slider]` from an earlier rev). |
| View-conditional rendering | Single canvas always | Three views render conditionally based on `opts.view` — Spectrum = 240×240 SL canvas, Grid = 8×8 HSL swatch palette, Slider = three stacked R/G/B channel sliders | Each view occupies the SAME 240×240 outer dimensions so swapping segments doesn't shift any geometry below. The picked-color state (hue/opacity sliders + footer hex/alpha) is preserved across view swaps — view-mode is a NOTATION choice (same color, different surface representation), not a value choice. |

#### The math-derivation principle is preserved (this is the durable lesson)

The eyedropper height = combined slider-stack height formula from §B.29 (`2 × --cp-track-h + --cp-gap`) survives the rev intact:

| Rev | `--cp-track-h` | `--cp-gap` | Computed eyedropper | Maps to tier |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| §B.29 | 16 (icon-s) | 8 (space-s) | `2 × 16 + 8 = 40` | L (40×40, radius-2m 14) |
| §B.30 | 12 (icon-xs) | 8 (space-s) | `2 × 12 + 8 = 32` | M (32×32, radius-2s 10) |

The "eyedropper bookends the slider column" semantic is identical; only the absolute size band moves. This is exactly the customization-lever-style propagation §1.4.3 promises: a designer pulling `--cp-track-h` from 16 to 12 reflows the eyedropper height through the formula automatically — no per-component edit needed. The formula itself is the system invariant; the numbers fall out.

#### Why M-tier eyedropper is the right resting state (not L)

Three factors converge on M:

1. **The "standard button" identity.** The user's directive named the target explicitly ("the standard button size that matches the input and segmented control corner radius"). M (32-tall, radius 10) IS the system's default form-control tier — Input M, Select M, Button M, InputGroup M, Composer M all sit here. The eyedropper at M reads as "this is a form control in the picker," not "this is a hero affordance bolted onto the picker." Industry alignment: Figma's eyedropper button sits at their form-control standard size, Sketch's at theirs, Adobe Color's at theirs — none of them ship a notably tall eyedropper.

2. **Radius continuity with the footer.** M-tier `radius-2s` (10) matches the footer InputGroup's outer radius (10) exactly. The eyedropper's right-edge and the InputGroup's right-edge now share the same curve tier, so the right column of the picker (eyedropper above + InputGroup below) reads as one continuous chrome family rather than two competing radius tiers. L-tier (radius 14) was one step too round for that role.

3. **The sliders becoming shorter feels right too.** 16-tall sliders at icon-s read as "chunky tracks" — visually heavy and tonally similar to the canvas surface above. 12-tall sliders at icon-xs read as "fine, deliberate, scrubber-style" — more like Apple ColorPanel / Figma / Adobe Color / Sketch where the slider chrome is intentionally lighter than the canvas. This is the calmer, more deliberate aesthetic the user has been steering the picker toward through every rev since §B.28.

#### Why three views, not one or two

Apple ColorPanel ships exactly three views (Color Wheel ≈ our Spectrum, Color Sliders ≈ our Slider, Color Palettes ≈ our Grid, plus a fourth Image Palette we don't replicate). The three views answer three different mental models:

| View | Mental model | When users reach for it |
|---|---|---|
| **Spectrum** (default) | "Drag through a color space to find what I want" | Exploring; tweaking; visual selection |
| Grid | "Pick from a curated palette" | Discrete known-good color choice; brand-color picking |
| Slider | "Set exact channel values" | Precision; numeric matching; design-token authoring |

Spectrum being the default + leading segment matches Apple ColorPanel's tab order and shadcn / Figma / Sketch convention — users default to the visual surface; Grid and Slider are alternates for users who already know either the palette they want or the exact channel values. Locking Spectrum's position to first reads as "the canonical entry point," not "one of three equal options."

#### Visual surface design per view

- **Spectrum**: existing 240×240 SL canvas with handle ring at `--sx, --sy` percentages. Unchanged from §B.28.
- **Grid**: 8×8 HSL swatch palette rendered as CSS grid with `overflow: hidden` and `border-radius: radius-xs` (8) on the outer container. Column 0 is a grayscale ramp (lightness only); columns 1–7 are 7 hue bands at 65% saturation. Rows step lightness from 92% (top, near-white) down to 8% (bottom, near-black). The selected cell carries a layered `box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px var(--color-surface-default), inset 0 0 0 3px rgba(0,0,0,.35)` ring as the "this swatch is picked" indicator — at the small 30×30 cell scale, a ring works better than a checkmark icon. Hover transforms scale 1.04 for tactile feedback.
- **Slider**: three stacked R/G/B channel rows, each laid out as `[12px letter label] [gradient track flex-grows] [28px numeric value]` with `tabular-nums` on the value text so single/double/triple digit values stay aligned. Per-channel gradient runs `linear-gradient(to right, #000, #<saturated channel>)` (R = `#000→#ff0000`, G = `#000→#00ff00`, B = `#000→#0000ff`). Channel handles use the same `--pct` + `margin-left` positioning constraint as the hue/opacity slider handles below (handle's body stays flush inside the track at endpoints — never overhangs). The slider stack distributes via `justify-content: space-evenly` within the 240px-tall content area, giving each row comfortable vertical breathing.

All three views inherit the canvas's `border-radius: radius-xs` (8) so the outer silhouette is identical across views — only the inner surface changes.

#### Forward implications

- **The bookend formula (`eyedropper_height = 2 × --cp-track-h + --cp-gap`) is the system invariant.** If a future picker variant changes either token, the eyedropper tier recomputes automatically. Don't ever pick a fixed eyedropper height — derive it from the slider column.
- **`view: "Spectrum"` is the documented default API value.** Any future consumer that wants Grid or Slider as the leading view must pass `view: "Grid"` / `view: "Slider"` explicitly. The segmented control's items array order is also locked to `[Spectrum, Grid, Slider]` so the leading segment is always the canonical default.
- **The picked-color state is preserved across view swaps.** A real consumer's implementation should keep one source-of-truth color value (hex / HSL / RGB) and re-derive the per-view UI state (canvas handle position, grid selected cell, channel slider positions) from that single value. This is what makes view-mode a notation choice rather than a value choice.
- **The slider re-tier from 16 to 12 generalizes**: any future picker primitive (date scrubber, opacity-only picker, gradient editor) that wants Apple ColorPanel-style "fine deliberate sliders" should use `icon-xs` (12) for track + handle. Reserve `icon-s` (16) for chunky/dominant slider chrome where the track is the primary affordance (e.g., a standalone audio scrubber).

