### §B.31 Color Picker — sliders shrink to 8, eyedropper M→XS ghost, slider-row Spectrum-only, Grid expanded to 12×11, content-fit heights (May 18, 2026)

#### User directive

> "can the slider tracks be even smaller in height? slightly? like 10px height or is that value not in our system? also maybe the dropper button can be a tertiary ghost style button. should there be a title and closer button header at the top? also add more color square options in the grid? for the grid and slider options, should the height of the container reflect the size of the content better and not be a fixed height through everything? and is it also necessary to have the slider tracks + hex code dropdown row?"

Six coupled questions, each answered with a specific structural change. The user is steering the picker toward a quieter, more focused, per-view-tailored aesthetic — the same direction as §B.28–§B.30 but at a finer grain.

#### What changed

| Sub-element | Before (§B.30) | After (§B.31) | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Slider track height (`--cp-track-h`) | `var(--icon-xs, 12px)` | `var(--space-s, 8px)` | User asked "10px or is that not in our system?" 10 isn't on the ramp (`space-s` = 8, next step is `space-m` = 12); 8 lands cleanly on the spacing ramp and reads as "fine, deliberate, scrubber-style" — one step lighter than §B.30. |
| Slider handle (`--cp-slider-handle`) | `var(--icon-xs, 12px)` | `var(--space-s, 8px)` | Tracks the slider-track change automatically — handle width = track height invariant preserved. |
| Eyedropper button | `Button M soft-neutral icon-only` (32×32, radius `radius-2s` 10, icon `icon-s` 16) | `Button XS ghost-neutral icon-only` (24×24, radius `radius-xs` 8, icon `icon-xs` 12) | User asked for "tertiary ghost style button." Ghost-neutral IS the system's tertiary fill (per §3.2 — solid is primary, soft is secondary, ghost is tertiary). XS size follows from the bookend formula: with slider tracks now at 8 and gap at 8, eyedropper = `2 × 8 + 8 = 24` = XS tier. The math-derivation invariant from §B.30 holds across this rev too — only the absolute size band moves. |
| Slider row gating | Always rendered in every view | **Spectrum-only** — Grid and Slider views skip the row entirely | User asked "is it also necessary to have the slider tracks + hex code dropdown row?" In Grid view, the swatches themselves ARE the value picker, so a separate hue+opacity row reads as redundant chrome. In Slider view, R/G/B channels already cover the "drag a value" affordance — adding hue+opacity duplicates the slider idiom. Each view stays focused on its own input mechanism. Apple ColorPanel's Grid mode doesn't show hue/opacity strip; Sliders mode shows only its own RGB sliders. |
| Grid swatch count | 8×8 = 64 swatches (column 0 grayscale + columns 1–7 at 65% saturation, rows step lightness 92→8%) | **12 cols × 11 rows = 132 swatches** — top row is 12 grayscale steps (white → black), then 10 color rows × 12 hue bands at 65% saturation, lightness stepping 90% → 18% in 8-point increments | User asked for "more color square options in the grid." Industry alignment: Apple ColorPanel Color Palettes ships ~13–15 columns × variable rows; Figma quick-pick has a similar wide grid. Splitting grayscale into a dedicated top row (instead of embedding it as column 0) reads as "neutrals + hues" rather than "8 stripes" — clearer information architecture. |
| Container height | Fixed 240×240 across all three views | **Content-fit per view**: Spectrum keeps 240×240 (SL gradient needs square real-estate), Grid expands to fit its 11 rows (240 wide × 220 tall at 20×20 cells), Slider shrinks to fit 3 channel rows (~64 tall total) | User asked "for the grid and slider options, should the height of the container reflect the size of the content better and not be a fixed height through everything?" Each view now sizes its content area honestly. The picker's outer height shrinks for Slider, grows for Grid, stays for Spectrum — matches how Apple ColorPanel's window also resizes per view-mode. |
| Title + close-button header | (not present) | (declined — not added) | User asked "should there be a title and closer button header at the top?" Declined as the default because the picker is overwhelmingly used inside a Popover anchored to a Color Input trigger — the trigger field IS the visible label, and the Popover's outside-click + Esc handle dismissal. A header would be redundant chrome for the popover case (matches Figma / Sketch / Adobe Color, none of which ship a header on their anchored color picker). An opt-in `header: { title, onClose }` variant remains plausible for standalone use (modal-style, drawer-anchored, marketing-flow color builders) but is deferred until a real consumer ships that case — §2.2.1 anti-bloat applies. |

#### The bookend formula survives a second rev — and that's the system invariant

| Rev | `--cp-track-h` | `--cp-gap` | Computed eyedropper | Maps to tier | Eyedropper fill |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| §B.29 | 16 (icon-s) | 8 (space-s) | `2 × 16 + 8 = 40` | L (radius-2m 14) | soft-neutral |
| §B.30 | 12 (icon-xs) | 8 (space-s) | `2 × 12 + 8 = 32` | M (radius-2s 10) | soft-neutral |
| **§B.31** | **8 (space-s)** | **8 (space-s)** | **`2 × 8 + 8 = 24`** | **XS (radius-xs 8)** | **ghost-neutral** |

Two independent levers — `--cp-track-h` and the eyedropper's fill style — moved simultaneously in this rev, but the bookend semantic (eyedropper height = combined slider-column height) is preserved automatically by the formula. The fill change (soft → ghost) is orthogonal: it answers a different question ("which fill style should the eyedropper carry") not driven by the bookend math. The fill-tier and the size-tier are independent levers per §2.3 (size is a height/radius/icon ramp) and §3.2 (fill is solid/soft/outline/ghost) — picking one doesn't force the other.

**The lesson: when a primitive's geometry is math-derived via a formula composing base tokens (§1.4.4 Pattern 2), changing any input recomputes the output automatically — and that's what makes the system parameterizable per §1.4.3.** Don't ever hardcode the eyedropper height (or any analogous bookend element); derive it from the column it bookends.

#### Why XS-ghost is the right resting state for the eyedropper

Three factors converge on XS-ghost:

1. **Tertiary affordance per §3.2's fill hierarchy.** The eyedropper is a tertiary action: it's an alternate way to land on a color value, not the primary mechanism (the canvas / grid / sliders are primary; the InputGroup hex/alpha is secondary). Tertiary actions get ghost fill (transparent until hover, then `action/hover` overlay). soft-neutral at §B.30 read as "secondary chip" — visually heavier than the role warranted. ghost-neutral reads as "available but not promoted" — exactly the right pitch for a sampling tool that most users don't reach for first.

2. **XS-tier is now the natural bookend.** §B.30's slider tracks at 12 forced the eyedropper to M (32) via the formula. Shrinking sliders to 8 forces the eyedropper to XS (24). The geometry doesn't lie: when the bookended column shrinks, the bookend shrinks. The XS button (24-tall, radius `radius-xs` 8) reads as "small inline affordance," matching the visual quietness of 8-tall slider tracks. M-tier at 32 against 8-tall tracks would have been geometrically off-balance — a tall standalone button bookending a flat thin column.

3. **Industry alignment.** Figma's eyedropper button is small + tertiary (icon-only in the chrome strip, no fill). Sketch's eyedropper is small + tertiary. Adobe Color's eyedropper is small + tertiary. Apple ColorPanel's eyedropper button has no fill at rest. No major picker ships a tall, filled, primary-style eyedropper — they're all small + transparent + sampled-from-the-canvas-via-click. XS-ghost is the convergent industry pattern, and §B.31 catches the system up.

#### Why slider-row gating is the right ergonomic call (not "always-on for consistency")

Earlier revs treated the picker as a single fixed layout where every row appeared in every view, on the rationale that "the picker should look identical across views — only the canvas swaps." §B.31 reverses that on user feedback: **each view should be maximally focused on its own input mechanism, not visually padded with redundant affordances**.

The trade-off:

| Option | Behavior | Cost | Benefit |
|---|---|---|---|
| Always-on (pre-§B.31) | Hue + opacity sliders appear in every view (Spectrum / Grid / Slider) | In Grid: row reads as "extra hue/opacity controls on top of the swatches." In Slider: row reads as "redundant — you already have channel sliders." | Visually consistent picker silhouette across views. |
| **Gated per view (§B.31)** | Sliders appear only in Spectrum; Grid + Slider hide them | The picker's outer height shifts when switching views (Spectrum tallest, Grid medium, Slider shortest). | Each view stays focused on its own input mechanism. Less chrome competing for attention. |

Industry pattern is gated-per-view: Apple ColorPanel's Color Palettes mode doesn't show the hue/opacity strip; Color Sliders mode shows only its own RGB sliders with no separate hue strip. Figma's color picker is mode-aware too — Solid mode shows a different chrome than Gradient mode. The gated approach honors the fact that **different input mechanisms have different chrome requirements**, and forcing visual consistency across them dilutes each one.

The picker's outer height shift is acceptable because the picker lives inside a Popover anchored to a Color Input — the Popover's positioning logic handles the height change (Floating UI / Popper.js / Radix Popover all reposition the popover when its content size changes). For consumers who really need a fixed-height picker (e.g., the picker is rendered in a sidebar with a stable layout), the slider row can be opted back in via a future `forceSliders: true` API; deferred until a real consumer ships.

#### Why no header (popover-first picker)

The user asked about a title + close button header. Declined as default, not declined entirely:

| Picker context | Header needed? | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Popover-anchored** (the default — picker opens from a Color Input trigger) | No | The trigger field IS the visible label ("Background color: [#D9D9D9]" + chevron). The Popover's outside-click + Esc handle dismissal. A header would duplicate the label and stack a second close affordance on top of the trigger's natural one. Matches Figma / Sketch / Adobe Color / Apple ColorPanel popover modes — none ship a header. |
| **Modal-anchored** (rare — picker opens inside a Dialog or full-page color builder) | Yes (opt-in) | Modals are blocking, so they need explicit close affordances and labeling. A `header: { title, onClose }` opt-in API surface is the right place to add it when this consumer ships. |
| **Drawer-anchored** (rare — picker lives in a settings drawer) | Yes (opt-in) | Same as modal — drawers carry explicit titles and close X. |

The first context covers ~99% of color-picker usage in product UIs; the latter two are documented escape hatches. The opt-in header is deferred per §2.2.1 until a real consumer ships — the API surface is plausible but not yet justified by demand.

#### Why expand Grid to 12×11 (vs 10×10 or 16×12)

The user asked for "more color square options." Three candidate grids:

| Grid | Total swatches | Cell size at `--cp-w` 240 wide | Mental model |
|---|---|---|---|
| 8×8 (pre-§B.31) | 64 | 30×30 cells | "8 stripes of hue + 8 lightness steps" — the eye reads 8 columns as a deliberate count |
| **12×11 (§B.31)** | **132 (12 grayscale + 120 color)** | **20×20 cells** | "Neutrals up top + 10 rows of 12 hues" — clear two-zone IA, matches Apple ColorPanel's curated palette format |
| 16×12 | 192 | 15×15 cells | Cells too small — individual swatches lose visual weight, the grid reads as "a wall of color noise" |

12×11 lands at the upper end of the readability band (20×20 cells still read as discrete clickable squares, not pixel-art). Splitting grayscale into a dedicated top row (rather than column 0 of the hue grid) is the IA win — the user sees "neutrals" and "hues" as two named zones rather than "1 column of grays + 7 columns of color" which reads as off-balance. Apple ColorPanel's Apple-shipped palettes follow the same pattern (Crayons palette has rows of named hues; Web Safe palette has a grayscale ramp separated from the hue grid).

#### Forward implications

- **The bookend formula `eyedropper_height = 2 × --cp-track-h + --cp-gap` is now a doubly-validated system invariant.** Three revs in a row (§B.29, §B.30, §B.31) have changed the inputs and the formula has produced the right output each time. Future revs MUST derive the eyedropper from this formula, not pick a fixed height.

- **Fill style and size tier are independent levers.** §B.30 moved size (L→M) without changing fill (soft-neutral stayed). §B.31 moves both. The system allows either to be changed independently; treat them as two separate decisions when designing future picker variants.

- **Slider-row gating per view-mode is the documented pattern.** Any future view added to the picker (e.g., a future "Gradient" mode for picking gradient stops) should make its own decision about whether the hue/opacity strip belongs in that view's chrome. Default to "no" unless the view's primary input mechanism genuinely benefits from the strip.

- **Header opt-in is reserved for modal/drawer-anchored pickers.** The popover-anchored picker is headerless. When a real modal-style picker consumer ships, add `header: { title, onClose }` as an opt-in API; until then, the picker stays headerless.

- **The "max readable grid" is ~12 cols × 11 rows at 240 wide.** Going denser (16+ cols) makes cells too small to read as distinct. If a future picker variant needs to expose a curated palette with named tones (e.g., "Material 3 tonal palette" picker), it should ship as a separate grid with explicit labels per row/column, not as a denser version of this Grid view.

### §B.32 Color Picker — sliders 8→12, eyedropper XS→M (revert of §B.31's slider/eyedropper portion only) (May 18, 2026)

#### The rev

User feedback after a side-by-side comparison of the §B.31 and §B.30 states: "the eye dropper button should go back to whatever the recommended size is, also the sliders can go back to 12px height." The slider-row gating (Spectrum-only), grid expansion (12×11), and content-fit heights from §B.31 are preserved; only the slider tracks and eyedropper revert.

The bookend formula remains the system invariant. With tracks back at 12 (`icon-xs`) and gap at 8 (`space-s`), the formula resolves: `eyedropper_height = 2 × 12 + 8 = 32` = M tier. The eyedropper's fill style stays ghost-neutral (§B.31's call holds — ghost is the system's tertiary fill per §3.2, which is the correct semantic for an alternate input mechanism). So the eyedropper is now `Button M ghost-neutral icon-only` (32×32, radius `radius-2s` 10, icon `icon-s` 16).

| Rev | Track h | Gap | Bookend math | Eyedropper tier | Fill |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| §B.30 | 12 (icon-xs) | 8 (space-s) | `2 × 12 + 8 = 32` | M | soft-neutral |
| §B.31 | 8 (space-s) | 8 (space-s) | `2 × 8 + 8 = 24` | XS | ghost-neutral |
| **§B.32** | **12 (icon-xs)** | **8 (space-s)** | **`2 × 12 + 8 = 32`** | **M (radius-2s 10)** | **ghost-neutral (preserved from §B.31)** |

#### Rationale for reverting the slider height

§B.31's 8-track shrink was driven by user-curiosity ("can it be even smaller? 10px?") rather than by a deficit at 12. After viewing the 8-track state in product context, the user felt 12 read as more deliberate. The system stance is "dense-default" (M = 32 across the control ramp); 12-tall slider tracks at the picker's M-tier eyedropper land in the same density band as every other M-tier control in the system, which makes the picker compose naturally next to Input M, Button M, Select M, etc. The 8-tall iteration read as "ultra-thin scrubber band" — visually elegant but slightly off-band from the system's broader density identity.

The matching ratio (track-h vs containing canvas) is also healthier at 12 vs 8: against a 240-wide canvas, 12-tall tracks read as ~5% of canvas height, which sits in the readable-but-not-dominant range that Figma (12-14), Sketch (12), Adobe Color (10-14), Apple ColorPanel (~12) all converge on. 8-tall tracks at ~3.3% of canvas height read as "barely there" — flat, hard to track visually during drag, and prone to mis-clicks on touch.

#### What changed and what survived from §B.31

**Reverted from §B.31**:
- Slider track height: 8 → 12 (`space-s` → `icon-xs`)
- Slider handle: 8 → 12 (handle-equals-track-height invariant)
- Eyedropper tier: XS → M

**Preserved from §B.31**:
- Eyedropper fill: ghost-neutral (the tertiary-affordance rationale holds independent of size tier)
- Slider-row gating per view (Spectrum-only)
- Grid expanded to 12×11 with separated grayscale top row
- Content-fit heights across views
- Header-less popover layout

The two-decision separation §B.31 made explicit (fill style and size tier are independent levers) was used here: §B.32 moves size back without re-moving fill. The system's rev history now has three data points (§B.30 changed size only, §B.31 changed both, §B.32 changed size only in the opposite direction) confirming that the two levers genuinely act independently.

#### Forward implications

- **Don't shrink tracks below the system's M-tier density band** (12-tall sliders) without an explicit consumer asking for ultra-dense chrome. The 8-tall iteration was visually thin enough to read as "deliberately minimal" but lost the deliberate-input feel that 12 conveys. If a future "compact mode" or "dense-mode" picker variant ships, that's when 8-tall tracks (and the matching XS eyedropper via the bookend formula) become appropriate — as an explicit opt-in mode, not as the default.
- **The "recommended size" for chrome inside the picker is always the one the bookend formula resolves to.** When a user asks "what's the recommended eyedropper size?" the answer is computed from `--cp-track-h` + `--cp-gap`, not picked freely. This is the third rev where the formula has produced the right answer; future revs must derive, not choose.

### §B.33 InputGroup divider extends through transparent borders + comp-frame__preview row wrap (May 18, 2026)

#### The rev — two coupled corrections to the playground's visual layout

User feedback covered two separate but converging issues:

1. **InputGroup divider didn't visibly extend full-height** in the Color Picker footer — specifically `div.ds-input.ds-input--m.ds-input--mono` showing a 30px outer height but the `::before` divider rendering only 28px.
2. **Component-frame variant rows overflowed horizontally** at narrower viewports — Card Tones, Color Picker Views, and other multi-instance demos didn't wrap when the viewport was too narrow to fit them in a single row.

Both issues trace to the same general theme — "components should compose into available space rather than overflow it" — and both are addressed with single-rule fixes that preserve all existing behavior in the wide-viewport case.

#### Fix 1: InputGroup divider extends into the child's transparent-border zone

**Root cause** (CSS box model interaction with `!important` chrome-shed):
- `.ds-input-group` (the outer container, e.g., `--ig-h: 32` at M tier) has `box-sizing: border-box` with a 1px border, so the inner content area is 30px tall.
- Each `.ds-input` child has `height: 100%` (override from the chrome-shed rules) and `box-sizing: border-box` with a 1px **transparent** border (the chrome-shed `!important` rule sets `border-color: transparent`, but the 1px border width remains in the box model so swapping a non-shed Input back in causes no layout shift).
- The child's padding box is therefore inset 1px from the top and 1px from the bottom of its border box (which is 30px tall to match the group's content area).
- The `::before` divider with `top: 0; bottom: 0` positions relative to the child's **padding box** (not its border box), rendering only 28px tall — visibly 1px short on each end.

**The fix**: `top: -1px; bottom: -1px` on the `::before` pseudo-element. This pushes the divider into the child's transparent-border zone so it spans the full 30px of the visible group content area. The group's `overflow: hidden` (already set on the outer `.ds-input-group`) safely clips any bleed against the rounded outer corners, so the divider can never escape the group's silhouette.

The rule:
```css
.ds-input-group>*:not(:first-child)::before{content:"";position:absolute;left:0;top:-1px;bottom:-1px;width:1px;background:var(--ig-divider);pointer-events:none;z-index:1}
```

**Earlier revs (rationale recap)**:

| Rev | Mechanism | Why dropped |
|---|---|---|
| Pre-§9.27 | `border-left` on inner children | Specificity arms-race with deep `:hover:not(.disabled):not(.is-error)...` selectors meant the divider color would leak through child-state overrides. |
| §9.27 initial | `::before` pseudo-element at `top: 0; bottom: 0` | Divider was 1px short on each end because of the transparent-border / padding-box interaction documented above. The bug was invisible at the standalone-InputGroup test cases but surfaced in the Color Picker footer where the divider had to read crisp against the picker's softer chrome. |
| **Current §B.33** | **`::before` pseudo-element at `top: -1px; bottom: -1px`** | **Divider spans full 30px (or whatever `--ig-h - 2` resolves to per tier). `overflow: hidden` on the outer group clips any bleed at rounded corners. No specificity issues. No layout shift.** |

The pseudo-element mechanism stays (it's the right tool — see §B.16's rationale for not using `border-left`), only the positioning constraint changes. This is the smallest possible fix for the smallest possible bug.

#### Fix 2: Component-frame row wrappers wrap responsively by default

**Root cause**: `.comp-frame__preview` ships with `flex-wrap: wrap` for its DIRECT children, but most multi-instance variant demos wrap their instances in an inner `<div style="display: inline-flex; gap: var(--space-2xl, 32px)">` (matching the §7.1.1 "32px between separately-instantiated components" rule). That inner div becomes the single child of the preview, and without explicit `flex-wrap` on the inner wrapper, the row overflows when the viewport is too narrow to fit all components.

The Card Tones demo (3 × 264px squares + 2 × 32px gaps = 856px) explicitly shipped with `flex-wrap: nowrap` on the rationale that "single-row comparison is the point." That worked at the comp-frame's ~870px content width at standard desktop viewports, but broke at common laptop widths (sidebar collapse, split-pane open, narrower windows) by overflowing the preview's rounded right edge.

**The fix**: a system-level catchall CSS rule on `.comp-frame__preview` that extends `flex-wrap: wrap` to every row-direction inner wrapper automatically:
```css
.comp-frame__preview>div:not([style*="flex-direction:column"]){flex-wrap:wrap}
```

The `:not([style*="flex-direction:column"])` exclusion preserves the existing column-direction state-stack convention (§7.1.1 "variant comparison — column with shared left edge") — column wrappers MUST NOT wrap, because `flex-wrap: wrap` on a column flex creates a multi-column overflow layout that breaks the state-stack rhythm. The exclusion relies on the compressed inline-style convention (`flex-direction:column` without spaces, 95 occurrences with 0 spaced variants in the codebase audit) used throughout `index.html`; new demos following the same convention auto-inherit the correct wrap behavior.

The Card Tones inline `flex-wrap: nowrap` was flipped to `flex-wrap: wrap` in lockstep so the canonical Card Tones row is now responsive. This both reduces drift (the Tones demo no longer fights the system-level catchall) and aligns the Card spec with the new system stance.

**Specificity contract**: the catchall lands at 0,0,2,1 (preview class + child wildcard + `:not` predicate). Inline `flex-wrap: nowrap` on a specific demo lands at 1,0,0,0 (inline style), which overrides the catchall — preserved as an escape hatch for the rare case where a demo genuinely needs to refuse to wrap (e.g., showing how a row would scroll horizontally at very narrow viewports). The default behavior changes; the escape hatch remains.

#### Why both fixes ship together

Both fixes are in the "no longer overflowing or clipping at the right edge" theme. Fix 1 prevents a 1px clip at the divider's top/bottom; Fix 2 prevents an N×264 horizontal overflow of card stacks. They reinforce a system-level invariant: **the rendered output stays within its declared chrome**. Future fixes that catch similar overflow / clip issues (e.g., text truncation in a fixed-width Card title, popover panels exceeding their max-width) should land in this same theme and reference §B.33 as the canonical pattern.

#### Forward implications

- **Don't position pseudo-elements via `top: 0; bottom: 0` on children with transparent borders.** The transparent-border-in-box-model trick (used by chrome-shed for layout-shift-free swapping) means `top: 0` is 1px below the visible top edge. Either extend with `-1px` (as here), use a `box-shadow` strategy, or compute the offset explicitly. This is now a documented gotcha for any future inset-divider pattern.
- **Demo wrappers that need to refuse to wrap must say so explicitly via inline `flex-wrap: nowrap`.** The catchall makes wrap the default; nowrap is now the opt-in. This reverses the previous convention (nowrap was the canonical Card Tones, Color Picker Views, etc. pattern via inline style; wrap is now the default and nowrap is the exception).
- **`overflow: hidden` on a contained surface is a load-bearing safety net for pseudo-element bleeds.** §9.27 InputGroup already had it for chrome reasons; §B.33 leverages it for the divider extension. Any contained surface that wants to render content extending past child padding boxes should ship `overflow: hidden` proactively.

### §B.34 Brand-color audit — Pagination + Tabs + Chip drop brand from active states (May 19, 2026)

#### The rev — system-wide audit of where brand color signals active state

User feedback: *"do a comprehensive pass on where the brand color is being used throughout to show active states. make sure where the color is being used is consistent with top design systems so that its not overwhelmingly all color. for example, why is the brand color used in pagination, but not in navigation? i definitely do not want the brand color to be used everywhere and want it to be used sparingly, so please be careful about applying it."*

The audit covered every component with an active/selected state and asked: **what carries the active signal, and is brand color the right tool?** The framing question was driven by the inconsistency the user surfaced — pagination's active page used a saturated brand fill while every Navigation primitive (Vertical, Horizontal, Bottom) used a neutral backplate. Two patterns can't both be the canonical "this one is current/active" treatment in the same system; one of them has to give.

#### The semantic split that emerged from the audit — "brand for values, neutral for navigation"

A component's active state encodes one of two distinct semantics:

| Semantic | What "active" means | Right active treatment | System examples |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Value selection** | "This is the chosen value out of a set; I've committed to it" | Saturated brand fill (`solid-brand` or `soft-brand`) — brand IS the choice signal | RadioCard, Radio checked, Checkbox checked, Switch on, Segmented Control active |
| **Current position in a navigable set** | "This is where I am right now in a list/grid I'm browsing" | Neutral backplate (`action/hover` bg + `border/default` inset ring) — the chrome marks position without claiming a value commitment | Nav vertical/horizontal/bottom active, Select panel selected, Pagination active, Chip selected |

The split is **not arbitrary**: a Radio's active state encodes the user's commitment ("I picked option B and the form will submit with B"); a Pagination's active state encodes navigation state ("I'm on page 4 of 17, and the URL/scroll position reflect that"). Brand color was reserved historically for value selection, but Pagination, Tabs, and Chip had slipped into using it for the navigation semantic. The audit pulled them back to the neutral camp.

#### Industry consensus — the navigation camp is the majority

| Component | Brand-active systems | Neutral-active systems |
|---|---|---|
| Pagination | Material 3 (brand-tonal), Atlassian (solid-brand), Tailwind UI (solid-brand) | Vercel Geist, Linear, GitHub Primer, shadcn (all neutral-active) |
| Tabs | Material 3 (brand underbar) | Vercel Geist, Linear, Notion, Apple HIG, shadcn (all neutral underbar) |
| Chip (selected filter) | Older Material 2, some legacy systems | Material 3 (neutral surface + small brand check), Polaris FilterPill, Linear, Notion, shadcn |

In every case the neutral-active camp is the majority among modern systems we benchmark against. Material 3 is the most consistent brand-active holdout — and Material 3's whole design language is heavier on brand color than ours by design. Our system stance is "designer-forward, generous radius, **quiet at rest, responsive on interaction**" (§1.1) — which sits squarely with Vercel / Linear / shadcn rather than with Material 3.

#### The three changes

| Component | Before | After | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Pagination** (default preset) | `soft-brand` active fill + `selection/border` stroke + brand chevron arrows | `ghost-neutral` active with `action/hover` bg + `inset 0 0 0 1px border/default` ring | Same selected-row treatment as Nav active per §9.3 / §4.5.1 |
| **Pagination** (outlined preset) | `outline-brand` active stroke | `outline-neutral` with `border/subtle` rest + `border/default` active + `action/hover` bg | Stroke contrast carries the active signal |
| **Pagination** (filled preset) | `soft-neutral` rest + `solid-brand` active | `soft-neutral` rest + `solid-neutral` active (filled-neutral-active lane) | Vercel / Linear / Primer convention |
| **Tabs** | 3px `action/solid/brand/bg` active underbar | 3px `text/primary` (near-black) active underbar | Hover underbar still gray; active escalates to near-black, not brand |
| **Chip** (selected) | `action/soft/brand/bg` fill + `text/brand` text + `selection/border` stroke (full brand surface) | `action/hover` bg + `text/primary` text + `border/default` stroke (neutral surface), **leading check icon stays `text/brand`** as the sanctioned single brand accent | Single brand touch per Standing Rule #4 Rule C (icon = brand when icon is a state indicator) |

#### Why Chip keeps the `text/brand` check icon (the audit's nuance)

A purely neutral selected chip — no brand color anywhere — was considered and rejected. The leading `ICON_CHECKMARK` is a **state indicator** (Standing Rule #4 Rule C explicitly covers this case: "icon = brand when icon is a state indicator"), and the brand-colored check is the convergent industry pattern across Material 3 (outlined Filter Chip — brand check), shadcn (filter chip — brand check), Linear (filter chip — brand check), and Polaris (FilterPill — brand check). Keeping a single small brand accent gives a row of selected chips a quiet sequence of marked items rather than a wall of brand fills, while preserving the visual affordance that says "this one is on" at a glance.

The pattern generalizes: **one small chromatic accent IS sparing brand use**; floods of chromatic fill IS the failure mode. A selected chip with a brand check + neutral surface lands in the sparing camp; a selected chip with a brand surface + brand text + brand stroke lands in the flood camp.

#### What didn't change — components that legitimately use brand color

Brand color was preserved everywhere the value-selection semantic applies:

- **Radio / Checkbox checked**: filled brand surface — "I picked this value"
- **Switch on**: green track (semantic-success, not brand) but the green→brand visual rhythm is preserved
- **RadioCard checked**: brand border + halo — "I committed to this card"
- **Segmented Control active**: filled raised pill with subtle brand-adjacent contrast (no change — this is value selection)
- **Button solid-brand**: the canonical primary CTA — "act on this with the recommended choice"
- **Focus ring**: 2-layer brand halo (no change — this is keyboard interaction state, not a persistent active state)

The audit did NOT touch any of these because their semantic is "user-committed value," not "current position in a set."

#### Forward implications — the rule for any new component

When a new component ships with an active/selected state, the question to ask is: **does this active state encode a user-committed value, or a current navigable position?**

- **Value commitment** → brand fill is the right tool (RadioCard pattern, Radio/Checkbox checked pattern, soft-brand button selected pattern). Surface + text + stroke can ALL go brand.
- **Current position** → neutral backplate + optional small brand accent (Nav active pattern, Pagination active pattern, Chip selected pattern). Surface stays neutral; brand color may appear ONLY on a single small element (an icon, a chevron, a dot indicator) per Standing Rule #4 Rule C.

The phrase "active state" in casual speech covers both semantics, which is exactly why the audit was needed — the language was ambiguous and the components had drifted. The "brand for values, neutral for navigation" rule disambiguates: figure out which semantic applies, then pick the right treatment. Don't introduce a brand-fill active state on a navigation primitive without justifying why navigation has become value selection in this case.

#### Cross-reference

- §3.5.1 — Shape language and chip-vs-badge differentiation
- §3.3.1 — Chromatic foreground "rule of last resort" (don't stack chromatic carriers)
- §4.5.1 — Stroke profile rule and selected-state ring pattern
- §9.3 — List-row state matrix (canonical neutral active treatment)
- §9.11 — Tabs spec (text/primary underbar)
- §9.16 — Pagination spec (three neutral presets)
- §9.17 — Chip spec (neutral surface + brand check)
- §12.1 — Cardinality rule (single-select vs multi-select differentiation between Chip and other horizontal patterns)
- Standing Rule #4 (icon color rules — Rule A inherit, Rule B chrome, Rule C state indicator)

