### §B.35 Drop Zone redesign + Date Picker today fix + state-demo consolidation rule (May 19, 2026)

#### The rev — four user observations driving one coordinated audit

User feedback came in a single message that surfaced four distinct issues:

> *"what about date picker? also drop zone has so many examples of states, can you make sure all components dont have the states in different component frames? also make sure the correct focused state effect is being applied. is the dotted line sequence spaced out enough and/or thick enough stroke? for all examples, make sure the icon colors are correct and consistent with other components. also the filled state does not make any sense. can you research other design systems drop zone components"*

Four observations, in order:
1. Date Picker — does the brand-color audit (§B.34) apply here too?
2. Drop Zone has split state demos — fix the inconsistency with the rest of the system.
3. Drop Zone dotted line — is the stroke thick enough? Are the dashes spaced enough? Is the focus effect right? Are the icons consistent?
4. Drop Zone "filled" state — doesn't make sense; research industry alternatives.

Each turned out to be a real issue. The rev addresses all four and adds a system-wide rule about state-demo consolidation that surfaced during the audit.

#### Fix 1 — Date Picker "today" indicator changes from brand to neutral

Date Picker's `today` cell (when not also selected) carried a 1px inset `border/focus` ring — the blue ring used everywhere else in the system for active focus. This was wrong for two reasons:

1. **Conflation with the focus state**: the same blue ring meant two things — "this cell is today's date" AND "this cell has keyboard focus." If a user opened the picker and tabbed to a non-today day, two cells now read as focused (the keyboard-focused one and today). The visual language got muddy.
2. **Conflation with selection**: when today happened to be selected, the spec already dropped the blue ring (correctly — brand fill carries the position). But when today was unselected and another day was selected, today still read as a quiet brand accent, competing for "current value" attention with the selected cell.

Per the §B.34 brand-color audit's "brand for values, neutral for navigation" rule, today is a **positional/navigational marker** ("you are here in the calendar") — not a value selection. The fix swaps the ring from `border/focus` (blue) to `border/default` (neutral gray):

```css
.ds-date-picker__day--today:not([aria-selected="true"]){
 box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--color-border-default);
}
```

Industry alignment: shadcn's Calendar runs today as a subtle accent (the same neutral as the cell border); Atlassian DatePicker uses a neutral underline for today; Apple HIG uses a neutral dot. Material 3 keeps today colored, the outlier. The neutral camp is the majority among modern systems we benchmark.

Range start/end cells KEEP brand fill because range endpoints ARE values the user has committed to (start date + end date = the value being entered).

#### Fix 2 — Drop Zone dashed stroke: 1.5px → 2px, dash pattern 6 6 → 8 6

The dashed border on the drop zone is rendered as an SVG `data:image/svg+xml` background-image, not a CSS `border-style: dashed` (browsers render `border-style: dashed` inconsistently across density and don't let you control dash length precisely). The SVG approach lets us tune both stroke width and dash pattern as design tokens.

The old values (1.5px stroke, 6px dash + 6px gap) read as a soft hairline — too quiet for the "drop content here" affordance to register at a glance. Cross-system research found that every modern drop zone runs at 2px stroke with longer dashes:

| System | Stroke width | Dash pattern |
|---|---|---|
| Polaris DropZone | 2px | ~8px dash / 6px gap |
| Material 3 file-upload | 2px | ~8px / 4px |
| Atlassian Attachment | 2px | ~6px / 6px |
| shadcn-extension FileUploader | 2px | ~8px / 4px |

We landed on **2px stroke + 8px dash + 6px gap** — sits in the middle of the cross-system band, reads as a deliberate dotted boundary at 480px width, and degrades gracefully at narrower viewports.

#### Fix 3 — Drop Zone icon colors aligned with system Rule B chrome conventions

Icon colors had drifted across states. The audit set the canonical pattern:

| State | Icon color | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Default | `text/tertiary` | Standing Rule #4 Rule B — chrome icon family (icons that categorize a slot, not its content) |
| Hover (drag-over) | `text/info` | Same family as Toast info / Pagination active-indicator chevron / "pending action" semantics |
| Error | `text/danger` | Matches Input validation error icons + Toast danger / Banner danger |
| Disabled | (inherits from `.ds-dropzone.is-disabled` opacity .5 wrapper) | No separate disabled icon token needed |

Title weight was also corrected: 500 → 400 to align with the §5.1.1 components-default-to-Regular rule. Hierarchy from title to hint comes from size (14/20 vs 12/16) + color (`text/primary` vs `text/tertiary`), not from weight.

#### Fix 4 — Drop Zone "filled" state redesign

User: *"the filled state does not make any sense."*

The old `.ds-dropzone.is-filled` state swapped the dashed border to solid + replaced the centered icon-cluster with an inline file list. The drop zone became a solid bordered list — and at that point it stopped looking like a drop zone at all. Users had no affordance to add more files because the drop affordance had been consumed.

Cross-system research:

| System | Filled-state pattern |
|---|---|
| Polaris DropZone | Drop zone STAYS visible; uploaded files render in a separate `UploadedFiles` block below |
| Material 3 file upload | Drop zone + file list are distinct components; drop zone stays interactive |
| Atlassian Attachment | Upload affordance + attachment rows render side-by-side |
| shadcn-extension | `FileUploader` + `FileList` are siblings; uploader stays interactive |

**Industry consensus is unanimous**: the drop affordance stays visible after upload; the file list is a separate sibling element. The old "consume the zone with a list" pattern was a design accident, not a convention.

The redesign:
- Removed `.ds-dropzone.is-filled` CSS entirely. There is no "filled" state.
- `dropZoneEl({ files: [...] })` now ALWAYS renders the dashed drop zone first; if `files` is non-empty, it appends a sibling `<ul class="ds-dropzone-list">` containing `<li class="ds-dropzone-file">` rows.
- Each file row carries `ICON_IMAGE` at `text/tertiary` (Rule B chrome icon) + file name on top + optional size on a second line (`body/s` `text/tertiary` — separated from the name for legibility) + trailing `Button XS icon-only ghost-neutral` with `ICON_XMARK` + `aria-label="Remove {filename}"`.
- File row chrome: `surface/default` bg + 1px `border/subtle` Job C definition stroke + `radius-xs` (8) + `space-s` (8) padding-y / `space-m` (12) padding-x — matches the system's small-contained-sub-element row idiom.
- The drop zone STAYS interactive after upload so users can drop additional files; the file list grows below.

API impact: `opts.filled` was removed from the helper. Existing call sites that passed `filled: true` are migrated to `files: [...]`.

#### Fix 5 — Drop Zone state demos consolidated into ONE comp-frame

The Drop Zone variants page previously had FIVE separate `variantBlock` calls — one each for Default, Drag over, Focused, Error, Disabled. The user surfaced this directly: *"drop zone has so many examples of states, can you make sure all components dont have the states in different component frames?"*

The fix consolidates the five states into a single `compFrame` using the variant-comparison-column pattern from §7.1.1:

```js
compFrame(
 '<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2xl,32px);align-items:flex-start;width:480px">'
 + dropZoneEl({ ... default ... })
 + dropZoneEl({ ... drag over ... })
 + dropZoneEl({ ... focused ... })
 + dropZoneEl({ ... error ... })
 + dropZoneEl({ ... disabled ... })
 + '</div>',
 code
)
```

Width is **480px** — matches the RadioCard/BottomNav demo-width tier from the §7.1.1 canonical table. Drop Zone is a card-class layout primitive (sizable by container width, not by control tier), so it lands with the other card-class state stacks.

Variants (Default, With files, Multiple files, Custom hint) remain as separate `variantBlock` calls because they're showcasing distinct behaviors/configurations, not state escalation. The new rule (Fix 6 below) makes this distinction explicit.

#### Fix 6 — System-wide state-demo consolidation rule added to §7.1.1

A side-discovery during the Drop Zone audit: **Drop Zone was the only component with split state demos**. Every other state-bearing component (Composer, Input, Select, Button, Checkbox, Radio, Switch, RadioCard, Tabs, Toast, Banner, Card, etc.) already stacked states in one comp-frame using the variant-comparison-column pattern. Drop Zone had drifted.

To prevent regression on future components, §7.1.1 now codifies the rule:

> **State demo consolidation rule (non-negotiable, May 2026)** — when a component documents state escalation (Default / Hover / Focused / Pressed / Error / Disabled / etc.), all states MUST be stacked inside a single `compFrame` using the variant-comparison-column pattern. Do NOT split states across N separate `variantBlock` calls or N separate `compFrame` instances. The split layout forces the reader to scroll between disconnected blocks and breaks the perceptual side-by-side comparison the state matrix exists to enable.

The rule distinguishes between **state matrices** (must consolidate) and **variants** (icons / sizes / specific behaviors — may occupy separate `compFrame` instances). The distinguishing test: if the comparison value is "the visual delta per state against a baseline," consolidate. If the comparison value is "what does this configuration look like," variants can split.

#### What didn't change — Drop Zone's load-bearing chrome decisions

The audit preserved every design decision that was correct:

- **Dashed border is the right affordance**: solid borders read as "container," dashed reads as "drop target." Industry-unanimous.
- **Centered icon-cluster anatomy** (large icon + title + hint, vertically stacked, centered): matches Polaris / M3 / Atlassian / shadcn-extension exactly.
- **`radius-m` (16) outer**: correct for a card-class surface. Not derived via the §2.1.2 inverse rule because the drop zone has no corner-adjacent inner elements at the M tier (the optional `outline-neutral S` browse button sits centered, not corner-anchored).
- **`space-2xl` (32) vertical / `space-l` (16) horizontal padding**: generous breathing for the centered icon-cluster; horizontal tighter than vertical because the content stack is vertical and the hint line needs less side-breathing than top/bottom breathing around the icon.
- **`<button>` element by default + `<div role="button">` when containing an inner button**: same chrome-with-nested-button-affordance pattern as Chip removable (§9.17).

#### Forward implications

1. **State demos**: future components ship with their state matrix consolidated in one comp-frame. The §7.1.1 rule is non-negotiable.
2. **"Filled" / "loaded" / "completed" states for action surfaces**: when designing a state where a surface receives content, the question to ask is "does the action affordance stay visible after content arrives?" If yes (industry-canonical), render the content as a sibling. If no, justify why the affordance is consumed (and expect to be challenged on it).
3. **Dotted/dashed stroke chrome**: 2px stroke + 8px dash + 6px gap is the canonical pattern for this system. Future components needing dashed chrome should reference this token set, not invent new values.
4. **Date Picker today + range cells**: today = navigation (neutral); range endpoints = values (brand). The split is consistent with §B.34 and propagates to any future calendar-class primitive.

#### Follow-up — Drop Zone file row chrome aligned with Toast single-row (May 19, 2026, same day)

User feedback after the §B.35 redesign shipped:

> *"the with files section should be modeled after the single row toast components, with the name of the file and the size being on the same row next to each other, make sure the copy is placeholder and the corner radius makes sense"*

Three correctable points in the initial file-row rev:

1. **Corner radius mismatched the inverse-rule answer.** The first rev shipped `radius-xs` (8) on the rationale that file rows are "small contained sub-element rows" (the list-row idiom — Dropdown items, Select panel items, Nav items all sit at `radius-xs`). But file rows aren't list-row idiom — they have a single icon, a single text block, and a single trailing affordance, which is the **inline-notification row** idiom (Toast / Banner shape, not Dropdown-panel-item shape). Toast sits at `radius-m` (16) because its trailing Button XS at `radius-xs` (8) + `space-s` (8) padding → `8 + 8 = 16` per §2.1.2 inverse rule. The Drop Zone file row has the **same trailing Button XS at the same `space-s` (8) inset from the corner**, so the inverse rule gives the **same `radius-m` (16) outer**. The rev fixed file-row radius from `radius-xs` (8) → `radius-m` (16) to match.

2. **Name + size were stacked, but should be inline.** The first rev shipped `.ds-dropzone-file__meta` as a flex column holding name on top of size on the bottom (the canonical "primary text + secondary metadata" stacked pattern used by Apple Files / macOS Finder / Atlassian Attachment). User correction: the file row should follow **single-row Toast**, which has title and description (when present) side-by-side on one row, not stacked. Stacked would make the file row a *two-line* row (~52px tall), inconsistent with single-row Toast's 40px floor and visually heavier than the rest of the Drop Zone's compact chrome. The rev renamed `__meta` (flex column) → `__body` (flex row) with `space-s` (8) gap between name and size, name truncating with ellipsis (`white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; min-width: 0`), size flex-shrinking to its natural width (`flex-shrink: 0`). Now the row reads as: `[image icon] [name ……… size] [X]` — same flex shape as Toast's `[tone icon] [title …………] [close]`.

3. **Demo copy wasn't obviously placeholder.** The first rev used `"image-1.png"` / `"image-2.png"` / `"document.pdf"` — those read as realistic copy, which violates the §7.3 generic-placeholder rule. The rev moved to the canonical "noun + numeral" pattern: `"File 1.png"` / `"File 2.png"` / `"File 3.pdf"`. Generic enough to read as placeholder, distinct enough to make the truncation behavior visible if a row narrows.

**System-wide alignment**: Toast and Drop Zone file row now share the **same chrome contract**:

| Property | Toast (single-row) | Drop Zone file row |
|---|---|---|
| Layout | `[icon] [body] [trailing]` flex row | Same |
| `min-height` | `var(--control-l, 40px)` | Same |
| Padding | `var(--space-s, 8px)` uniform | Same |
| Border | 1px `border/subtle` (neutral) | Same |
| Border radius | `var(--radius-m, 16px)` | Same |
| Body gap (between name/title and size/description) | `var(--space-s, 8px)` | Same |
| Type tier | `body/m` (14/20) | Same |
| Bg | `surface/default` | Same |
| Position | Floating (`var(--shadow-3)`, `--z-toast`) | In-flow (no shadow, base z-layer) |

The shared chrome is intentional. The two surfaces have different temporal contracts (Toast auto-dismisses, file row persists until removed) and different positions (Toast floats at the viewport corner, file row sits in document flow as a sibling of the drop zone) — but at the **per-row visual chrome level** they're the same primitive. The system's cross-component vocabulary stays tight: a user who learned what a Toast looks like at a glance can read a Drop Zone file row the same way without retraining.

**This fits the §2.2.1 anti-bloat rule**: instead of inventing a separate "uploaded file row" chrome contract with its own padding/radius/font tokens, the system reuses Toast's row chrome via shared base tokens (`--control-l`, `--space-s`, `--radius-m`, `body/m` type tier). Zero new tokens, zero new visual contracts. If a third "inline notification row" consumer ships later (a "saved search" row, a "selected file" row in a multi-step form), it inherits the same chrome and the system stays consistent.

**Cross-reference**: §9.8 Toast (the canonical chrome); §9.25 Drop Zone (consumer); §7.3 placeholder copy convention (noun + numeral); §2.1.2 inverse rule (`outer = inner + inset = 8 + 8 = 16`).

