### §B.36 Playground accuracy audit — radius drift across Nav, Dropdown, Select panel, Breadcrumb + theme toggle migration to ghost-neutral Button (May 19, 2026)

A direct user-driven audit pass on the visual language playground itself: "double check that the navigation components being used here are using correct corner radius nesting principles, and that all components in the visual language playground are accurate themselves." Plus a specific call-out: "the light dark mode toggle in the top right is not correct, can you use the actual toggle component or button."

The audit surfaced **two classes of drift** that had accumulated through component churn over prior revs, plus one playground-host-chrome bug. All three are documented here as one fix-set because they share an audit origin and a single user message.

#### Category 1 — Radius drift on small-element family controls

The §2.1.1 ramp prescribes `radius-xs` (8) for every 24–28-tall interactive control (Button XS/S, Input/Select/Textarea S, Nav vertical S/M, Nav horizontal S, Dropdown items at the locked panel-item tier, **etc.**). Several consumers had drifted to `radius-2s` (10) — the next ramp step up — for reasons that didn't survive audit:

| Consumer | Before | After | Why drift snuck in |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Playground sidebar nav** `.nav-node__title`, `.nav-leaf` (host chrome, mimics Nav M) | `radius-2s` (10) | `radius-xs` (8) | Copy-paste lift from a different tier; never re-validated against the 28-tall row height |
| **`.ds-nav-item`** base + `--s` + `--m` (system Nav S=24 / M=28) | `--ni-rd: radius-2s` (10) | `--ni-rd: radius-xs` (8) | Same — token was set once at a larger tier assumption and never re-validated when S/M tiers were added |
| **`.ds-nav--horizontal .ds-nav-item--xs` / `--s`** (horizontal Nav 24/28) | `--ni-rd: radius-2s` (10) | `--ni-rd: radius-xs` (8) | Tier overrides inherited the wrong base value |
| **`.ds-menu`** panel (Dropdown) | `--mnu-rd: radius-2m` (14) | `--mnu-rd: radius-s` (12) | Drift past the §9.3 "panel locked at `radius-s` (12), do NOT inherit from trigger" rule |
| **`.ds-menu__item`** (Dropdown items) | `--mnu-item-rd: radius-2s` (10) | `--mnu-item-rd: radius-xs` (8) | Drift past the §9.3 "items locked at `radius-xs` (8), fixed at all sizes" rule. Concentric corners restored: `panel 12 − pad 4 = item 8` ✓ strict regime |
| **`.ds-select-panel`** | `radius-2m` (14) | `radius-s` (12) | Same §9.3 rule for Select panel (anchored overlay sibling of Dropdown) |
| **`.ds-select-panel__item`** | `radius-2s` (10) | `radius-xs` (8) | Same §9.3 item-locked rule |
| **`.ds-breadcrumb`** M default | `--bc-rd: radius-2s` (10) | `--bc-rd: radius-xs` (8) | Drift on the hover backplate radius. Breadcrumb M = 28-tall, so per §2.3 should be `radius-xs` (8). The default radius cascaded to the S variant (24-tall) too, where it was *also* wrong; the S variant inherits from the base so no additional override needed once base is corrected |

**Why these tier-radius pairings matter — restated for future audits**. The radius ramp in §2.1.1 maps **height-tier → radius-tier** for every 24-tall through 48-tall interactive control:

| Control height | Radius tier | Token |
|---|---|---|
| 24 (XS) | `radius-xs` | 8 |
| 28 (S) | `radius-xs` | 8 |
| **32 (M)** | `radius-2s` | **10** |
| 40 (L) | `radius-2m` | 14 |
| 48 (XL) | `radius-l` | 20 |

The mistake every drifted consumer made was the same: **picking the radius value from the wrong row of the table**. A 28-tall row using `radius-2s` (10) is picking the M-tier radius for an S-tier height — visibly more rounded than its siblings (Input S, Select S, Button S all at 8). At a glance the row looks "off" without being able to articulate why; in side-by-side comparison the asymmetry is obvious. The §2.1.1 spec text on "smoothing the curve" explains the non-linear nature of the ramp, but the consumer-facing rule is simply: pick the radius from the same height-tier row as the control. Don't reach for the next step up because it "looks nicer" — that breaks the cross-component rhythm.

**Concentric-corners math, restated for the panel→item pair (§2.1.2 strict regime)**: with panel at `radius-s` (12) and `space-2xs` (4) padding, the corner-adjacent item lands at `12 − 4 = 8 = radius-xs`. The pre-fix state (panel 14, item 10) coincidentally satisfied a *different* concentric pair (`14 − 4 = 10`), which is why it didn't read as visually broken — but it violated the spec's explicit "panel locked at 12, items locked at 8" rule (§9.3). The fix restores the spec-intended concentric pair AND the spec-intended absolute values; both invariants now hold.

#### Category 2 — `radius-3xs` (4) was a non-existent token

`radius-3xs` doesn't appear in the §2.1.1 ramp (the smallest step is `radius-2xs` (4)). Eleven consumers were referencing `var(--radius-3xs,4px)` — the fallback `4px` made them render correctly *visually*, but the token resolution failed silently because no `--radius-3xs` is defined anywhere in `:root`. Any consumer customizing the radius ramp (the §1.4.3 "re-round" lever) would see these components fail to update because they were referencing a phantom token.

All references resolved correctly via the fallback because every site that used `radius-3xs` was pinning to the same 4px value `radius-2xs` already provides. The fix is a search-and-replace: `var(--radius-3xs,4px)` → `var(--radius-2xs,4px)`. No visual change, but the system's "every value resolves through a documented token" contract (§1.4.4 Pattern 1) is restored.

Consumers cleaned up in this pass: `.chev-btn`, `.ds-input__swatch`, `.ds-checkbox`, `.ds-tabs__trigger`, `.ds-chip__dismiss`, `.ds-skeleton text/text-lg`, `.token-badge__dot`, plus a stale comment block.

#### Category 3 — Theme toggle was using an undefined fill style + textContent for SVG content

The host-chrome theme toggle in the top-right of the playground was using `ds-btn--tertiary` — a Button fill variant that doesn't exist. The Button family ships `solid` / `soft` / `outline` / `ghost` × `neutral` / `brand` / `danger` / `success` (§3.2 / §11). There's no `tertiary` modifier; the rendered button was therefore falling back to whatever browser defaults `<button>` ships, ignoring the design system's chrome contract entirely.

Three coupled fixes:

1. **Class swap**: `ds-btn--tertiary` → `ds-btn--ghost-neutral` (the documented Button variant for icon-only chrome affordances — Dialog close, Toast close, and Pagination prev/next all use the same).
2. **Icon-injection mechanism**: `ico.textContent = darkMode ? ICON_MOON : ICON_SUN` → `ico.innerHTML = darkMode ? ICON_SUN : ICON_MOON`. `textContent` was rendering the raw SVG markup string as literal text inside the span; `innerHTML` parses it as SVG. Also flipped the conditional: when the page is in dark mode, the toggle should show the *sun* (the destination the user is going TO if they click, not the state they're currently IN). This matches Linear / Vercel / Notion / Material 3 convention (icon shows the alternative theme, not the current one).
3. **Dead `.sf` class removed + a11y attributes added**: the `.sf` class was a remnant from a pre-Phosphor SF Symbols icon system that was deleted in §5.3.1 but left a class reference behind on this one consumer. Replaced with the standard `aria-hidden="true"` on the icon span and `aria-label="Toggle light / dark"` on the button itself (per §14.3 icon-only affordance rule).

The result is a theme toggle that's a real instance of the system's `Button XS icon-only ghost-neutral` chrome — the same primitive used everywhere else in the system. No special host-chrome CSS. No undefined tokens. Same hover, focus, pressed behavior as every other ghost button.

#### Why this audit found drift other passes missed

The drift in Category 1 wasn't visible from a single component view — every individual component's docs page rendered fine because the radius value was *consistent within that component*. The drift only surfaces in side-by-side cross-component comparison: a `Button S` next to a sidebar `nav-leaf` showed visibly different corner radii at the same height. Same for `Dropdown item` vs `Select panel item` vs `Nav vertical item` — all three are 28-tall list-of-rows children, all three should share `radius-xs` (8) per §9.3, but the system shipped them at 10/10/10 (Dropdown item), 10 (Select panel item), and 10 (Nav vertical M) instead of 8/8/8.

The audit-by-side-by-side discipline is the canonical way to catch this drift. Every new component is built against the §2.1.1 ramp tables, but those tables describe the system's intended state; verifying *current* state requires looking at adjacent components rendered next to each other. The user's explicit "double-check that the visual language playground is accurate" request is precisely the kind of audit that surfaces this drift — a developer building one component at a time isn't well-positioned to catch radius drift because they're looking at the component in isolation.

#### Lesson — what's needed to prevent recurrence

The radius drift across 8 consumers points at a recurring failure mode: **tier overrides cascade incorrectly when the cascading author doesn't re-derive the radius for each tier**. Example: `.ds-nav-item` shipped with `--ni-rd: radius-2s (10)` set in the base selector. When the S and M size modifiers (`.ds-nav-item--s` / `--m`) were added later, they overrode `--ni-h` (the height) and `--ni-fs` (the font) and `--ni-icon` (the icon) — but didn't override `--ni-rd`. So both S (24-tall) and M (28-tall) inherited the wrong radius from the base. The fix is to either (a) move `--ni-rd` to be derived from `--ni-h` via a formula, or (b) override `--ni-rd` in every size modifier alongside the height/font/icon overrides. We took option (b) here because the radius-tier mapping isn't a clean linear function of height (§2.1.1's "smoothing the curve" discussion explicitly says so).

The lesson generalizes: **whenever a component ships a size modifier that overrides height, the size modifier MUST also override every other tier-coupled token** — radius, font-size, line-height, icon-size, gap. The §2.3 control-scale table is the source of truth; every modifier should set all six values explicitly. Setting only some of them creates drift the moment one of them is at a different tier than the component's base assumed.

This is now an audit rule: "for each size modifier, verify it overrides H + radius + font + LH + icon + gap, not just H." Future components should ship the override block as a whole, not partial.

