### §B.37 Table cell alignment — `vertical-align: top` system-wide (May 19, 2026)

User request: "make sure all the cells are set to align top left and not centered in each cell."

The audit surfaced a real visual bug: tables with mixed-content-height rows (e.g., the typography family table where one cell contains "Reading serif" and the adjacent description cell wraps to 2-3 lines) were rendering short cells vertically centered against tall cells. The short cells appeared to "float" at the visual center of the row instead of sharing a top edge with their neighbors. This is the browser's default `<td>` behavior (`vertical-align: middle`), which was explicitly cemented in the system's `.ds-table td` rule.

The pre-fix state had two related issues:

1. **`.ds-table td { vertical-align: middle }`** — the default-middle approach reads correctly only for single-line uniform-height rows. As soon as any data has height variance (multi-line code blocks, description text wrapping, badge cells vs single-character cells), the asymmetry surfaces.
2. **`.ds-table th`** had no explicit `vertical-align` declaration — falling back to the browser's `<th>` default of `middle`. Same problem.
3. **`.ds-table--tvp td, .ds-table--type td { vertical-align: top }`** — a per-variant override that worked correctly for the docs tables but acknowledged the bug only in those two variants; the other tables (motion, semantic-token, comp-frame tables) kept the broken middle-default.

**The fix is to invert the default**: `vertical-align: top` system-wide for both `<th>` and `<td>`, drop the per-variant overrides. Single-line rows are unaffected (only one line to align); multi-line rows now read as crisp left-justified text blocks where every cell shares the top edge of its row.

Why this wasn't caught earlier: most demo tables in the system don't have rows with variable cell heights. The typography family table is the canonical example because each row pairs short token names with long descriptions and small previews — exactly the variance pattern that surfaces the bug. The user's screenshot (the family table showing "Source Serif 4" with "Transitional serif" / "Weight, Optical size" / preview rendered at default browser middle-alignment) made it immediately visible.

**Specificity gotcha encountered during the fix**: an initial attempt added `text-align: left` to the base `.ds-table td` rule alongside `vertical-align: top`. That broke the right-aligned columns (`.ds-table__cell--num`, `.ds-table__cell--actions`) because `.ds-table td` (0,1,1) outranks `.ds-table__cell--num` (0,1,0). The corrected rule deliberately does NOT set `text-align` on the base `td` — `<td>`'s browser default of `left` carries left alignment for most cells while the column-kind classes override on the cells that need right alignment. The vertical axis works fine with just the base declaration because no column-kind class sets `vertical-align` (they all share `top`).

**Cross-system alignment** (every modern data-table primitive defaults to top-aligned for the same reason):
- shadcn Table → `align-top` is the default in their data-table examples; cell-padding default doesn't center vertically
- Atlassian Atlaskit Table → cells default to `vertical-align: top` per their styling spec
- Linear data tables → top-aligned, multi-line rows are crisp
- Vercel Geist Table → top-aligned
- Material 3 data tables → top-aligned

Browser default `<td>` middle-alignment is a relic of HTML 4.0 text rendering that almost no modern data-table component honors. Our system was carrying it for no good reason.

