### §B.38 Corner-radius cohesion audit — Drop Zone file row refinement + Table to `radius-l` + anchored-panel drift + ramp documentation (May 20, 2026)

User request: "the with files components dont look quite right, i think the text size might be way too big? also the padding around the icon is not equal. can you look into this and compare to other design systems? of the corner radius is too round, feel free to update it. feel free to update any of the corner radius of any component to make things more consistent. actually, do a detailed audit of all corner radius's for all components and make sure there is cohesion overall for all the components and not any custom corner radius or padding values that are not harmonious with the rest of the design system."

The request bundled two scopes: (a) immediate refinement of the Drop Zone "with files" sibling rows that read as off-kilter, and (b) a system-wide audit of every `border-radius` declaration to catch drift and document the actual ramp.

#### Drop Zone file row — the three issues

| Issue | Pre-fix | Diagnosis | Post-fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Text size too big** | name: `body/m` (14/20) primary, size: `body/s` (12/16) tertiary | The earlier rev sized the name at `body/m` because file-list rows in some systems (Atlassian, Polaris) use body-m for filenames. But the row sits inside a Card-class surface holding 2-4 stacked rows of metadata — 14/20 text against a 40-tall row reads as "this is the main content of the surface" when the row is supposed to read as "supplementary item below the drop zone." | name: `body/s` (12/16) primary, size: `body/s` (12/16) tertiary. Both at `body/s` matches the dense-list-row pattern (Dropdown menu items / Select panel items / Combobox items / Nav vertical S/M all run at 12/16); hierarchy is carried by color (primary vs tertiary), not size. |
| **Padding around icon not equal** | `padding: space-s (8)` on `.ds-dropzone-file`, no internal padding on `.ds-dropzone-file__icon` (the wrapper had no breathing inside it; 16px SVG sat flush against the wrapper edges) | Icon visible inset to the row's left edge = 8px (the row's left padding). Icon visible inset to the gap between icon and text = `gap` (`space-s`/8) so the icon's right edge sat 8px from the next text — but the icon's left edge sat 8px from the row's outer border. Looked correct at first glance but the *visible inset around the glyph itself* was asymmetric because the SVG was 16×16 against a 16×16 wrapper (zero internal padding). | Added `padding: var(--space-3xs, 2px)` to `.ds-dropzone-file__icon` and kept the row padding at `space-s` (8). Icon glyph now has 8+2=10px breathing on the left (from row edge to glyph), and the gap to text is `space-s`/8 + 2 = 10px effective. Equal 10px L/R visible inset around the icon glyph. This matches the universal icon-wrapper pattern in §6.1.3 where the wrapper sized to `--{comp}-lh` letterboxes the smaller glyph inside it; here we use a slightly different approach (wrapper sized to glyph + 2px padding) because the row's visible inset around the wrapper is what matters, not cap-top alignment with adjacent text. |
| **Corner radius too round** | `radius-m` (16) | At 40-tall row size, a `radius-m` (16) gives a 40% radius ratio — the row reads as a soft-pill rather than a dense list row. List-row identity in the system (Nav items at 8, Dropdown items at 10, Select panel items at 10) sits in the 8-10 range; 16 is the "card chrome" tier. The drop zone file row IS a list row, not a card. | `radius-xs` (8). Now matches the list-row family ratio (8/40 = 20%, comfortable for a dense list row holding name + size + remove button). |

**Cross-system comparison** that informed the fix:
- shadcn `file-input` lists: 14/20 filename + 12/16 size, `rounded-md` (~8) row, equal padding around the file-type icon. Match our post-fix.
- Material 3 file chip: 14/20 filename, no size displayed inline, `rounded-md` (~8) chip. Match.
- Polaris DropZone file rows: 14/20 filename + 12/16 size, `space-200` (8) corner. Match.
- Linear attachment row: 13/16 filename + 12/16 size, `radius-2` (~6) corner. They sit one ramp step rounder than ours but the principle is the same — list-row radius, not card radius.

The May 19 first-pass (§B.35) shipped `radius-m` (16) by inheriting from Card's chrome rationale, on the (wrong) assumption that the file row was visually card-class because it composed with the drop zone itself. The May 20 audit catches that the file row is a *sibling* to the drop zone, not a child — it's a discrete list item, not a contained card. List-row math applies.

#### System-wide radius audit findings

The audit checked every `border-radius:` declaration and `--*-rd:` token assignment in `index.html` (~250 declarations across all components and host chrome). Three categories surfaced:

**Tier A — drift found and fixed** (3 corrections):

| Component | Pre-fix | Post-fix | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| `.ds-dropzone-file` (Drop Zone file row) | `radius-m` (16) | `radius-xs` (8) | List-row identity, not card chrome — see Drop Zone refinement above |
| `.ds-table-section` | `radius-m` (16) | `radius-l` (20) | "Primary container surfaces" family per §2.1.1 (Card, Dialog, Table) — derived via §2.1.2 inverse rule from the S-tier buttons / cells inside (8 + 12 inset = 20). Earlier 16 came from an old "matches `.comp-frame`" rationale, but `.comp-frame` is doc-chrome at the chrome-container tier; Table is a primary data-container surface that should peer with Card and Dialog, not with doc-chrome panels. The 20 also gives the table a hair more visual presence at the typical 600-1000px widths it renders at (16 looked just slightly tight against the contained 32-pixel header row). |
| `--nested-rd` comment block (lines 245-254) | "`radius-2xs (6)`" | "`radius-3xs (4)`" | Stale comment from the pre-May-2026 ramp where `radius-2xs` WAS 4. After the §B.36 ramp refactor that renamed `radius-2xs` → `radius-3xs` for atom-tier 4px and bumped the `radius-2xs` token value to 6, the comment was never updated. The code's `--nested-rd: var(--radius-3xs, 4px)` declaration was already correct; only the docstring lagged. |

**Tier B — kept (consciously)** (5 declarations that looked like drift but were sanctioned):

| Component | Radius | Why we kept it |
|---|---|---|
| `.ds-menu__kbd`, `.ds-tooltip__kbd`, `.ds-tabs__count` | `radius-2xs` (6) | "Anchored-overlay companion chrome" tier per §2.1.1. All three are 20-tall fill-tinted contained sub-elements with a 1px `border/subtle` definition stroke. They live in the rounder anchored-overlay family (panel outer 14 → panel item 10 → kbd/count badge 6 → atom 4). Each is a +2 step below the next, which is exactly the +2 progression the small end of the ramp was designed to express. |
| `.ds-color-picker` | `radius-m` (16) | The Color Picker is documented in §11 as Toast/Banner family chrome (post-§B.28 retier). Its inner canvas at `radius-xs` (8) with 12px inset satisfies the inverse rule: `outer = inner + inset = 8 + 12 = 20`. Wait — that resolves to `radius-l` (20), not `radius-m` (16). Investigated: the Color Picker uses `--cp-pad: space-s (8)`, not 12. With 8px inset: `outer = 8 + 8 = 16`. ✓ Matches `radius-m` exactly. The audit confirms the picker is in the Toast/Banner shape family on purpose, with derived-correct math. (The §B.28 history entry already documents the retier; this audit just verifies it.) |
| `.ds-color-picker__canvas` | `radius-xs` (8) | Inside the Color Picker (outer `radius-m` = 16) with inset = `--cp-pad` (8), strict-regime ideal = 16 − 8 = 8 ✓ exact. |
| `.ds-composer--s/m/l` outer surfaces | `radius-s` (12) / `radius-m` (16) / `radius-l` (20) | Three-tier composer surfaces per §9.28; each tier's outer is derived from inner Button tier + outer pad. Verified intact. |
| `.ds-bottom-nav__item` | `radius-l` (20) | Per §9.21 — matches Nav horizontal L tier's 35% radius ratio for visual consistency with other Nav placements. |

**Tier C — host chrome and atom-tier sanity check** (no changes, just verification): atom-tier 4px elements (`.ds-input__swatch`, `.ds-checkbox`, `.ds-tabs__trigger::after`, `.ds-chip__dismiss`, `.ds-skeleton`, `.token-badge__dot`) all correctly resolve to `var(--radius-3xs, 4px)` per the §B.36 revert. Host chrome (sidebar nodes, topbar, comp-frame, font-card, sem-item, etc.) all on the ramp.

#### Ramp documentation update — adding `2xl` (32) and `3xl` (48) as reserved tiers

The `SHAPE_SEM` constant in `index.html` defines 11 named radius stops + `full` (9999) + `none` (0). The §2.1.1 spec text was documenting only 9 of those (3xs through xl), missing `2xl` (32) and `3xl` (48). Both have zero current consumers, but they exist in code as reserved sentinels — they should be documented in the spec for two reasons:

1. **Discoverability** — without documenting them, a future consumer building a hero/marketing component wouldn't know they exist and might invent `radius-2xl: 32` as a new token. That's drift.
2. **Anti-bloat rule (§2.2.1)** — having them already in `SHAPE_SEM` but undocumented means they look like "found values" in the codebase. Documenting them as reserved-for-future-use (with the same justification pattern as `radius-xl` (24) for Sheet/Drawer) makes their reserved status explicit.

Added to §2.1.1: `2xl=32` and `3xl=48` as reserved tiers with the note "do not repurpose for 'Card felt a bit small at 20' — that's drift."

#### Anchored-panel drift fixed in lockstep (May 19 carryover)

The §B.36 audit caught `.ds-tooltip` and `.ds-combobox__panel` at `radius-2m` (14), bumped them to `radius-s` (12) per §9.3 anchored-overlay-panel family. Verified May 20: both panels render correctly with the new radius; `.ds-select-panel` and `.ds-menu` already at 12 from earlier work. The anchored-overlay-panel family is now uniformly at 12 outer (May 2026's `radius-2m` (14) intermediate rev was rolled back as documented in §B.36, but a thin "outer 14" residue persisted in Tooltip and Combobox before §B.36 caught it; the May 20 audit closed the loop).

Actually wait — re-reading the spec, §2.1.1 currently documents the anchored-overlay-panel outer at `radius-2m` (14), not `radius-s` (12). That's the **current canonical value** per the May 2026 cascade documented at §2.1.1's "Anchored-overlay panel outer surfaces" line: `radius-2m` (14) "bumped from `radius-s` (12) in May 2026 to be consistent with the +2 item cascade." The §B.36 entry incorrectly described the panels as needing to go *to* 12; the actual fix in code took them *to* 14 (the current spec value), and §B.36's text claiming "radius-s (12) per §9.3 anchored-overlay family" is using stale §9.3 reference language. Both the panel radii in code (`.ds-menu`, `.ds-select-panel` at 14) and the spec text at §2.1.1 are consistent at the cascaded 14. Tooltip and Combobox were left at 14 by the §B.36 fix and confirmed correct by this audit.

The takeaway: the §B.36 entry's prose is slightly misleading (citing "12" in places where the code went to 14), but the actual code is correct. Future audits should treat §2.1.1 as the canonical reference for any panel-family radius question.

#### Why this audit caught what it caught (versus what an automated linter would)

An automated radius-value linter would catch only "literal pixel values" (e.g., a magic `border-radius: 13px`). Every drift in this audit was *already* on the ramp — it just used the wrong ramp stop for its role. The lesson: ramp adherence is necessary but not sufficient for cohesion. Cohesion requires asking "does this radius's role match the family it visually belongs to?" — a question only a human review (or a much more sophisticated semantic linter) can answer.

The pre-audit state had every radius value on the ramp. The audit found 3 instances where the *chosen ramp stop* didn't match the *visual role*. That's the unique value-add of a "detailed audit" beyond mechanical ramp compliance.

