### §B.40 Composer text-padding drift — align code to spec §9.28 uniform/equal-pad rev (May 20, 2026)

User request (continuation of §B.36–§B.39 cohesion audit): "make sure each component frame has a title. then do a comprehensive pass on all components, making sure the copy is generic, the components are consistent, and all details are accounted for: semantic tokens, colors, consistency, everything documented in design system."

The spacing portion of the cohesion audit found exactly one off-ramp pixel value in the entire `index.html` after the §B.36–§B.39 fixes landed: `.ds-composer--l { --cmp-text-px: 10px }`. The 10 sat between `space-s` (8) and `space-m` (12) on the ramp, sanctioned in the inline comment as "a temporary per-component literal pending a future `space-2s` (10) ramp step" — but that ramp step was never added, and the spec §9.28 had already moved to a different padding model in a later rev.

#### What the code was carrying vs what the spec documented

| Tier | Code `--cmp-text-px` (pre-fix) | Code `--cmp-text-py` | Pattern | Spec §9.28 `--cmp-text-px` (target) | Spec pattern |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| S | `space-xs` (6) | `space-2xs` (4) | H = V + 2 (asymmetric) | `space-2xs` (4) | uniform/equal-pad |
| M | `space-s` (8) | `space-xs` (6) | H = V + 2 | `space-xs` (6) | uniform/equal-pad |
| L | **10px (off-ramp)** | `space-s` (8) | H = V + 2 | `space-s` (8) | uniform/equal-pad |

The code's "H = V + 2" pattern dates from an earlier Composer rev where the rationale was case-B asymmetric breathing scaled per tier (the §6.1 case-B rule says text needs slightly more horizontal padding than vertical because the line-box provides ~3-4px of leading above the cap-top for free, but the horizontal edge has no equivalent buffer). Applied per-tier, the pattern produced visible H/V ratios of 1.19 / 1.35 / 1.61 across S/M/L — comfortably inside the §6.1.1 case-B band (1.0-1.5 ratio sanctioned for text-bearing surfaces) at S/M, but **L overshot the band at 1.61**. The spec was updated to fix this overshoot by switching to uniform/equal-pad, where text-py = text-px raw, but visible cap-top inset still produces case-B asymmetry naturally because the line-box absorbs ~4.4px of leading per side. The new visible H/V ratios are 0.96 / 1.15 / 1.29 — all comfortably inside the band.

The code lagged the spec by two revs. Standing Rule #12 (math-first system, no off-ramp pixel literals) was the explicit trigger for the audit; the 10px stuck out.

#### The fix

Three CSS lines updated:

```css
/* Before */
.ds-composer    { --cmp-text-px: var(--space-s, 8px) }   /* M default */
.ds-composer--s { --cmp-text-px: var(--space-xs, 6px) }
.ds-composer--l { --cmp-text-px: 10px }                  /* off-ramp */

/* After */
.ds-composer    { --cmp-text-px: var(--space-xs, 6px) }  /* M default */
.ds-composer--s { --cmp-text-px: var(--space-2xs, 4px) }
.ds-composer--l { --cmp-text-px: var(--space-s, 8px) }   /* on-ramp */
```

`--cmp-text-py` left unchanged (S=4, M=6, L=8) because the field's box-model still needs to size the field height correctly in `--single` layout. At M: `padding-top (6) + line-height (20) + padding-bottom (6) = 32 = --control-m` ✓. At S: `4 + 20 + 4 = 28 = --control-s` ✓. At L: `8 + 20 + 8 = 36`; sibling buttons are 40-tall so the row resolves to 40 via flex max-child and the field's 36-content sits centered via `align-items: center` — same as the pre-fix layout. The fix touches only `text-px`; vertical padding stays where it was.

The 53-line inline comment block that described the H = V + 2 rationale was replaced with a shorter block documenting the equal-pad rev, the §6.1.1 sanctioned band, and a brief "earlier rev replaced May 2026" note for future archaeologists.

#### Visible consequence

Text inside every Composer in the playground sits **2px closer to the surface edge horizontally on each side** at every tier. The change is small (4px total H tightening per Composer) but visible side-by-side. Buttons in the action row are unaffected because they're outside the `__field` wrapper and govern their own padding via `--cmp-pad` (which didn't change).

#### Why this is the right direction (versus aligning spec to code)

Three reasons:

1. **The off-ramp 10px violates Standing Rule #12.** The spec is the source-of-truth for math-first compliance; the code's "sanctioned per-component literal pending a future ramp step" carve-out depended on a ramp step that was never added. The drift was a temporary hack that became permanent.
2. **The L tier's old ratio (1.61) overshot the §6.1.1 case-B band.** Even setting aside the off-ramp issue, the H = V + 2 pattern at L produced text breathing that read as "text squashed vertically, floating in a wide pillow" rather than "comfortable case-B asymmetric padding." The spec's uniform/equal-pad rev was explicitly motivated by fixing this overshoot.
3. **The post-fix visible ratios sit inside the documented band at every tier.** S at 0.96 (visibly uniform, V slightly dominant — matches Textarea M's behavior per §6.1.1), M at 1.15 (modest H dominance, between Dialog 1.0 and Card 1.33), L at 1.29 (comfortable H dominance matching Card/Banner 1.33). Three perceptibly distinct case-B treatments stepping up with surface size, all inside the canonical band.

#### What the spacing audit confirmed (the broader sweep)

Beyond the Composer fix, a `Grep` for direct pixel values in custom properties (`--[a-z]+-[a-z]+:\s*\d+px`) returned ~120 occurrences. Each was classified:

- **On-ramp via direct token reference** (e.g., `var(--space-s, 8px)` with `8px` as the fallback): the vast majority. These are Pattern 1 per §1.4.4 and explicitly sanctioned.
- **On-ramp via `--radius-*` token**: every component's `--*-rd` declaration. Sanctioned.
- **Pattern 3 sentinels** (50%, 100%, 9999px): Switch track, Avatar round, dot indicators, full-bleed widths. Sanctioned per §1.4.4 Pattern 3.
- **Demo visualizations**: `swatch-checker` 10px background-size for transparency pattern; `token-badge__dot` 10×10 width/height for demo dots. Sanctioned as demo content per §1.4.7 Tier 3.
- **Component-specific font sizes off the main type ramp**: `--bn-fs: 10px` on Bottom Nav. The 10px label is documented in §9.21 as the canonical mobile-nav label size (matches Apple iOS Tab Bar, Material 3 Navigation Bar) and is sanctioned per-component. The spec text already documents this; the audit just verified the value matches the spec.
- **Off-ramp drift**: exactly one — the Composer L `--cmp-text-px: 10px`, fixed here.

The spacing audit closes the loop: after this fix, every pixel value in `index.html` traces back to either a ramp token (Pattern 1), a formula composing ramp tokens (Pattern 2), or a documented Pattern 3 sentinel. Standing Rule #12 compliance is restored.

#### Cross-reference

- §9.28 — Composer anatomy (the spec section that documents the uniform/equal-pad rev)
- §6.1 / §6.1.1 — Case-B padding rule and visible H/V ratio band
- §1.4.4 — Pattern 1/2/3 (the three sanctioned forms for any numeric value in component CSS)
- §1.4.7 — Tier 3 demo visualizations (the sanctioned exception for `swatch-checker` and `token-badge__dot`)
- §12 (Standing Rule) — Math-first, parameterizable system (the rule the off-ramp 10px violated)

#### Lesson for future audits

The cohesion audit's value isn't in catching ramp violations (a linter could do that); it's in catching cases where the code lags the spec by one or two revs. The Composer's H = V + 2 pattern was a perfectly valid design choice when it shipped, and the spec at that time documented it. The spec then moved on (uniform/equal-pad fixes the L overshoot), but the code didn't catch up. The off-ramp 10px was the only mechanical sign of the drift; the rest of the H = V + 2 pattern would have been invisible to a literal-value linter because every other tier's text-px (6 at S, 8 at M) was on the ramp.

The audit cadence going forward: every comprehensive component pass should diff `index.html` against the spec section-by-section for each completed component, not just check the raw pixel values. A drifted component can have every value on the ramp and still implement a superseded design rev.

