### §B.41 Dialog double-padding fix, scroll regression, and Danger anatomy consolidation (May 20, 2026)

#### The three issues

User-reported during a Dialog inspection pass:
1. **Double H padding on title and description.** Inspecting `.ds-dialog--danger .ds-dialog__header-text` showed the wrapper had 2px H padding AND its children (`.ds-dialog__title`, `.ds-dialog__description`) also had 2px H padding each — net 4px of inset against the wrapper's implicit baseline. This also misaligned title/description (16px from outer dialog edge) with the body (14px from outer edge), since the body wrapper applied its own single 2px padding without a redundant inner layer.
2. **Scrollable Dialog broken.** The `--scroll` body modifier set `overflow-y: auto` but had no effect — the body stretched past the dialog's `max-height` instead of scrolling. The dialog itself uses `display: flex; flex-direction: column`, and the default `min-height: auto` on flex children prevents shrinking below content size. Without an explicit `min-height: 0` on `.ds-dialog__body`, the body would not yield to the container's max-height constraint, so the `overflow-y: auto` never activated.
3. **Danger demo includes a `body` slot.** The current Danger variant rendered `title + description + body`, anatomically identical to the "Description" demo (which exists to show the full 3-slot anatomy). User question: "should there be a 'body' shown here? how do u decide what copy to show for each component?" — implying the Danger variant should focus on showing the tone treatment, not the slot anatomy.

#### Diagnosis — three independent failures with one cross-cutting fix

The first two are CSS bugs; the third is a content/demo-architecture decision. All three are fixed in lockstep because they touch the same component.

**Bug 1 — Double padding.** Per the inline comment in `.ds-dialog`'s CSS, the dialog's text inset is meant to be `--dlg-px (12) + --dlg-text-px (2) = 14px` from the outer edge, applied uniformly to title, description, and body so all three rows share the same H baseline. The wrapper provides the 2px (`.ds-dialog__header-text` and `.ds-dialog__body` both carry `padding: 0 var(--dlg-text-px)`). Title and description had inherited an extra `padding: 0 var(--space-3xs, 2px)` from an earlier rev when there was no `__header-text` wrapper — the wrapper was added later, but the title/description's own padding wasn't removed. The body, lacking a wrapper of its own (it IS the wrapper), correctly carries the 2px directly. Net effect: title and description sat at 16px H, body at 14px H — a 2px misalignment that read as the title block being slightly farther from the chrome edge than the body. The fix removes the redundant `padding: 0 var(--space-3xs, 2px)` from `.ds-dialog__title` and `.ds-dialog__description`, restoring the uniform 14px H baseline across all three text rows.

**Bug 2 — `min-height: 0` on the flex child.** Standard flexbox gotcha. When a flex container has `max-height` and a child needs to scroll, the child needs `min-height: 0` to override the default `min-height: auto` (= intrinsic content height). Without it, the child resists shrinking, the container overflows past `max-height`, and any `overflow-y: auto` on the child is never triggered because the child is always sized to its content. The fix adds `min-height: 0` to `.ds-dialog__body` unconditionally (not just under `--scroll`) because the constraint is harmless when no `max-height` is in play — the body grows to fit content as normal — and is load-bearing when scroll is enabled.

**Decision 3 — Danger anatomy.** Cross-system audit of "destructive confirmation" / "alert" dialog patterns:

| System | Component | Body slot? |
|---|---|---|
| Apple HIG | Alert | No — message text serves as description, no third slot |
| Material 3 | AlertDialog | No — supporting text is the description; no body |
| shadcn | AlertDialog | No — `AlertDialogDescription` is the message; no body |
| Atlassian | ModalDialog (danger appearance) | No — body is reserved for non-confirmation dialogs |
| Linear | Confirmation dialog | No — title + description only |
| Vercel | Destructive confirmation | No — title + description only |
| Stripe Dashboard | Destructive confirmation | No — title + description only |
| Notion | Destructive confirmation | No — title + description only |

Industry consensus is unanimous: destructive confirmation dialogs use the 2-zone pattern (title + description + footer), not the 3-zone pattern (title + description + body + footer). The "body" slot belongs to dialogs whose purpose is to host substantive content — forms, scrollable prose, embedded controls — not to dialogs whose purpose is to confirm a decision.

The fix drops `body: "<p>Body</p>"` from the Danger variant demo. The Danger demo now renders title + description + danger-styled footer buttons, which is the canonical destructive confirmation pattern across every modern system. The other dialog demos retain their bodies because they serve different purposes:
- **Default** (title + body) — simplest case, demonstrates how a single-message dialog reads
- **Description** (title + description + body) — full anatomy showing all 3 slots
- **Danger** (title + description) — canonical destructive confirmation, body slot is intentionally omitted
- **Form** (title + description + form body) — dialog hosting form fields
- **Scrollable** (title + description + long body) — exercises overflow behavior
- **Stacked footer** (title + description, alertDialog) — full-width stacked footer pattern (already body-less)

Each demo now has a distinct purpose with no anatomical redundancy.

#### How widespread is the double-padding pattern?

A `Grep` across `index.html` for `padding:0 var(--space-3xs)` returned many hits across Button, Badge, Nav, Breadcrumb, Segmented Control, Date Picker, form fields, Checkbox/Radio/Switch rows, Select, Menu, Card, Tabs, Chip. Most are single-layer applications (e.g., `.ds-btn__label` inside a button — the button itself doesn't add another layer of `--space-3xs` padding, so the label's 2px H is its only layer). The doubled pattern requires BOTH a wrapper and its children to apply the same 2px H — and that only happens in two places in the system: Dialog and Card. Both had `__header-text` wrappers carrying 2px H *and* title/description children also carrying 2px H.

The fix applies symmetrically: remove the redundant `padding: 0 var(--space-3xs, 2px)` from:
- `.ds-dialog__title`
- `.ds-dialog__description`
- `.ds-card__title`
- `.ds-card__description`

The wrappers (`.ds-dialog__header-text`, `.ds-card__header-text`) retain their 2px H so all text content within each component shares a consistent H baseline with the body wrapper.

#### What about the body's 2px?

`.ds-dialog__body` and `.ds-card__body` correctly retain `padding: 0 var(--space-3xs, 2px)` because they are their own wrappers (no `__body-text` exists). The 2px is single-layer there, not double, and removing it would put the body at 12px H while the header-text wrapper (and its now-clean title/description) stays at 14px H — that would re-introduce the misalignment in the opposite direction. The right answer is "every text wrapper has exactly one 2px H layer" — applied to `__header-text` and `__body` directly, never to the text elements inside them.

#### The lesson

When a wrapper component (like `__header-text`) is introduced to hold formerly-direct children (`__title`, `__description`), the children's own padding values must be audited as part of the introduction. A wrapper that adds `padding` to a region where children already have `padding` is a near-certain double-counting bug. The Dialog and Card components both went through a "wrap title + description in a flex column for descriptionless variants" refactor (so the column has `gap: 0` and treats title+description as one text block per §9.13's "size-uniform weight-differentiated" rule), and the wrapper inherited 2px H from the eventual layout — but the title/description's own 2px H wasn't removed in the same pass.

The audit rule going forward: when introducing a wrapper around N existing children, EITHER move all padding to the wrapper (and zero it on children) OR keep all padding on the children (and zero it on the wrapper). Never have both.

#### Cross-reference

- §9.6 — Dialog anatomy (the spec section that documents the three-padded-slots pattern)
- §9.13 — Card anatomy (same pattern, same wrapper introduction)
- §6.1.4 — Leading-induced cap-top inset (the rule the 2px H absorbs)
- §1.4.4 — Pattern 1/2/3 (the three sanctioned forms for any numeric value)

### §B.42 Scrollable Dialog regression — the real fix is `flex-basis: 0` + `overflow: hidden` on the dialog (May 20, 2026)

#### The bug, recurring

§B.41 added `min-height: 0` to `.ds-dialog__body` on the theory that the standard flexbox gotcha (`min-height: auto` preventing flex children from shrinking below content height) was the only blocker. **It wasn't.** User reported "the scrollable dialog is still broken" with a screenshot showing the body content overflowing past the dialog's `max-height: 320px` constraint, with the dialog itself stretching past the constraint AND the body's `overflow-y: auto` never activating.

#### Diagnosis — three independent flex-layout failure modes, two missed in §B.41

For a scrollable region inside a fixed-max-height flex column to work, **all three** of the following must hold:

1. **The flex child needs `min-height: 0`** so it can shrink below its intrinsic content height. (Fixed in §B.41.)
2. **The flex child needs `flex-basis: 0` (not `auto`)** so the flex algorithm sizes it from the remaining space, not from its content. With `flex: 1 1 auto`, the body starts at content-height and grows; with content much larger than the dialog can hold, the body wins the flex battle and stretches the dialog past `max-height`. With `flex: 1 1 0`, the body starts at 0 and grows ONLY into the remaining space after header and footer have taken their share.
3. **The container needs `overflow: hidden`** so any content that escapes the body's scroll region (e.g., a renegade child with `position: absolute` or a width-overflowing inline element) is clipped at the dialog's rounded corners, not leaked into the page.

§B.41 fixed only #1. The body still had `flex: 1 1 auto` (basis: content), and the dialog itself had no `overflow: hidden`. So when the body's content was bigger than the available space, the body's flex-basis (= content height) dominated the flex calculation and the dialog grew to accommodate, never hitting the `max-height` ceiling. This is the canonical "flex: 1 1 auto fights max-height" gotcha that every modern layout framework (shadcn, Radix, Headless UI, Mantine) documents in their dialog scroll examples.

#### The fix — three coordinated rules

```css
.ds-dialog {
  /* … existing rules … */
  min-height: 0;        /* allow shrinking when nested in a flex parent */
  overflow: hidden;     /* clip any escapees at the rounded corner */
}
.ds-dialog__header,
.ds-dialog__footer {
  flex: 0 0 auto;       /* explicit "natural height, no grow, no shrink" */
}
.ds-dialog__body {
  flex: 1 1 auto;       /* default: content-sized, grows to fill */
  min-height: 0;        /* allow shrinking past content height */
}
.ds-dialog__body--scroll {
  flex: 1 1 0;          /* override: basis 0 so we ONLY get remaining space */
  overflow-y: auto;     /* and then scroll past it */
}
```

The `--scroll` modifier specifically overrides the base body's `flex: 1 1 auto` with `flex: 1 1 0` (basis: 0). This is the load-bearing change. The dialog's own `overflow: hidden` is the safety-net rule (catches any escape paths), and `min-height: 0` on the dialog covers the case where the dialog itself is a flex child of a constrained parent (the playground's `.comp-frame__preview` is a flex container, so the dialog needs `min-height: 0` to honor `max-height` inside it).

#### Why split base body and `--scroll` modifier (not unify to `flex: 1 1 0` always)

Non-scrollable dialogs should grow to their content's natural size. With `flex: 1 1 auto` on the base body, the dialog renders at content-height (typical case: title + 2-paragraph body = ~120px tall dialog). Switching the base to `flex: 1 1 0` would force EVERY dialog to fill its parent height, regardless of content — visually wrong for the simple confirmation dialog case.

The split is: base `__body` uses `flex: 1 1 auto` (content-sized); `__body--scroll` uses `flex: 1 1 0` (remaining-space-sized + scroll). Both cases satisfy `min-height: 0` because the constraint is harmless when no `max-height` is in play.

#### Cross-system verification

| System | Scrollable dialog pattern | Their fix |
|---|---|---|
| shadcn (Radix Dialog) | `<ScrollArea>` wrapping content area with explicit `max-h` + `overflow-auto` | Same as ours: parent has max-h, scroll region has `flex-basis: 0` + `overflow: auto` |
| Material 3 AlertDialog | `surface` has fixed max-height; content area has `flex: 1` + `overflow: auto` | Same |
| Apple HIG (UIKit) | Sheet content uses `UIScrollView` for scrollable region | Equivalent via UIKit's native scroll-view |
| Atlassian Modal | Scrollable body container with `overflow-y: auto` | Same |
| Polaris Modal | Section with `overflow: scroll` + parent `max-height` | Same |

All converge on the same pattern: container constrains height, scroll-region has flex-basis: 0 + overflow-y: auto. We now match.

#### The lesson

Flexbox scroll regions are not "add `overflow-y: auto` and you're done." The flex algorithm has to allocate space such that the scroll region IS the constrained child. That requires the scroll child to have `flex-basis: 0` (or a small basis), `min-height: 0`, AND the container needs `min-height: 0` or `max-height` set. Forgetting any one of the three creates the "scroll never activates" bug.

The audit rule going forward: when a component has a scrollable region, the spec MUST explicitly call out the three coordinated rules. Single-rule fixes (just adding `overflow-y: auto`) are a common drift pattern because the failure mode is "looks fine at default content, breaks at large content" — and dev-time testing rarely exercises the overflow case.

